        <p>Ois scapegrace son was universally well spoken of. Th• factory-master had been chafing and fuming. constantly during the past few days. Besides tne fifty pounds which had been stolen a few weeks before, other sums were missing, and the thief had been found in his own son. On this day of Christmas, therefore, when peace and good-will should have been extended to all, animus and against his youngest son pre- vailed in James Cartleigh's heart. He had resolved to unburden himself finally to Ralph, give him a Christmas-box of a thousand pounds, and tell him to "clear out," and begin the New Year afresh elsewhere. But hearing of his son's plucky deed, his heart was softened towards him. .:41Elang it! The lad's not bad. He can't be," he asseverated, as if to silence his inner doubts. "Yet there's much more bad than good in him. He robs me every week; he treats me disrespect- fully on every occasion; he insults all of us; he has given himself up to all kinds of evil, vicious practices—yet on this day, of all others, he does a deed the like of which blots out a lot of sinning." The better man was speaking now; not the hard, relentless, cold, calculating commercial man, but the man of old time. Had James Cartleigh per- mitted this good spirit to direct his thought and action, to dominate his whole life, he would have been a happier man, a kindlier father, and mayhap his son would have been attached to him more filially. In the midst of his ruminations the young man entered the room, swinoin his skates as he advanced with a jaunty, careless air. His easy, devil-may-care bearing, roused within the father the old irritating spirit. "Been skating, eh ? " he grunted. "Nice exercise to be engaged in on Christmas Day:" "You're right. Nice exercise, indeed. Simply I glorious I've had a ripping time!" said Ralph, flinging himself into an easy-chair. . "Your mother, your sister; and I have been attending afternoon service at the mission church," continued James Cartleigh, in a severe tone. "Indeed! That's where my dear brother is pastor, I presume? I'm glad to hear that you've been there. We need to support the family; but such religious exercises are not to my liking. I prefer the other sort," and he jingled his skates. "But I'm hungry, father. Skating makes one hungry; sermons make one sleepy, especially my beloved brother's," and with a cynical smile, the flippant young fellow rose and passed out of the Mom. "I wish to see you after tea," his father called after him. "Very good. I shall be at your service—after tea. A full stomach, a contented mind, and a good temper go together." "Can he have a contented mind ? " the father asked himself as he re-entered the drawing-room and mused on his son's complex character. "That he can arsume such an easy, careless bearing, knowing that he is guilty of the vilest practices, is to me incomprehensible." Many things were incomprehensible to James Cartleigh. Of cotton-buying, cotton-selling, cotton- weaving, and cotton-spinning he knew much; of other matters, he knew but little. Of politics, of art, of literature, of science, of the wondrous, subtle • forces at work in life, of the laws of heredity, of the effect of environment on human character—of these and a thousand such questions he knew nothing. Why then try to fathom the depths of his son's • „ composite nature? Ralph was truly incomprehensible to a man of his father's limited capacity, What wonder then that in the wordy tournament which later ensued, the son should again emerge therefrom triumphant? What wonder that he disarmed his parent so successfully that James Cartleigh felt his inability to combat further with his offspring. "It's no use being vindictive and revengeful on this day, above all others," Ralph had said. "You and I have been fighting pretty hard with each other during these past twelve months. To-day we will declare an armistice—a suspension of,hostilities —until the year is out, at least." "I'll not make peace at any price," said his father. "You have made war upon me mercilessly for months past. You have ruined yourself, and you would ruin me and your family. I'll have no more of it. Christmas Day or not, this is my day, and I'm going to defeat you this time." "Now, father," expostulated the son, in mock serious tone, "you've just returned from a religious service, you know, where you've doubtless been making solemn vows, asking forgiveness and promising that you will give it to others. Now, I'm not begging for forgiveness—it's not my way you know; but I ask you, is it Christianlike to talk about defeating me, and—er—that sort of thing ? " .9 "Perhaps it isn't Christianlike," retorted James Cartleigh; "but is your conduct Christianlikeli young man ? " "Al, well, now," said the tricky, shifty Ralph,l "that's nothing to do with it. I don't go to ' religious services; I go to skating-parties. I don't profess to be a religious, Christian man, like you or—" "That will do," said his parent, severely. The contest soon ended, and at its close Ralph congratulated himself on starting anew with a clean sheet.</p>
        <p>Johannesburg had many grades. The weattny mining magnates, mostly Jews, whose palatial offices graced Commissioner-Ftreet, and whose private residences overlooked joubert Park, were the tughes_ grade, socially considered; the hangers.. on of this class, the "rising" section, the " Change" men, the company promoters, the large importers of the city, the commercial men, ranked second in importance • then came the hotch-potch, mixed crowd of nobodies who were aiming to be "somebodies" a very numerous class of all nationalities. Below these, the men about town, the canteen loungers, the Micawbers, the corner men; lower still, the scum of the city, Jew and Gentile, white and black, yellow and copper-. coloured—a prowling, thieving, lawless, desperate crowd like hideous carrion, ever seeking whoifl they might devour. A strange, conglomerate, cosmopolitan people were these Johannesburgers. The unsophisticated Boer first making their acquaintance was unfavour- ably impressed, and regarded the Outlander as the worst of the "voordomed reoineks."</p>
        <p>His Government, too, seemed to share largely in his opinion, and were loth to concede any reforms to the Outlander.</p>
        <p>Thus it happened that much unrest and dissatis- faction prevailed in the Golden City on this day of Christmas. Stephen Hart walked on through the streets, bright in the glare of the tropical sun, and reflected on the remarks he had overheard as he passed the boarding-house and street-corner politicians. It behoved him to know more of these Dutchmen. Happy thought !, He would begin this very day, and go over to Pretoria. He had heard that the Boers would be celebrating the nacht-mahl in the capital to-day, and would be there in their thousands. To the railway station he hurried, and soon was travelling on a slow Nether- lands excursion train to Pretoria. The carriages were packed with noisy trippers of all nationalities. Here was the Cornish miner, "Cousin Jack," the smart City man attired in, faultless Bond-street garb, the "lady" of the Pritchard-street boulevard, the Cape boy and his girl, both "touched by the tar-brush," though fancying themselves white folks; English, Scotch, Irish, Cape Colonists, Natalians, Australians, Americans, Germans, Frenchmen— all races, all tongues, all colours.</p>
        <p>Steve looked out on the far-spreading veld, and as his eyes fell on the blue mountains fringing the western horizon, far, far away, blue and very clear, he thought of dear old smoky Dalesworth, with its cottages huddled together in long rows, and its mighty factories, with their tall chimneys, frowning over all. How different from this sparsely- populated region, where scarce a single homestead could be seen. Yet Dalesworth was' very, very dear to the exile. He thought of Fernleigh Mill, the noise of machinery, the rattle and clang, the shouting of the workers one to another; he thought of the weavers, of Ella Rayburn. Ah, what happy day-trips they had had together? How would she be spending this Christmas Day Oh, how he yearned for her, hungered for a sight of her dear face.</p>
        <p>From his pocket he surreptitiously pulled out a letter and a photograph, and, shielding himself behind a newspaper, he kissed them passionately. . .</p>
        <p>A moment later his tender refledions were-cut short as the train bustled into Pretoria. Here there was much to divert his attention. Standing near the station entrance, he looked down on the quiet, restful town nestling in the basin below. The capital was hemmed in on all sides by low hills or kopjes. Trees and fair, green pasture-. lands refreshed the eye after the barrenness of the Rand. Proceeding down the main street, by the side of which a purling brook glistened, Steve was rejoiced by the sight of flowers and shrubs, which adorned the gardens by the wayside. Hereabouts resided the foreign Consulates, the chief Govern- ment officials and State dignitaries. Oom Paul's modest dwelling—a two-storey struc- ture, situated in a by-street—interested the visitor greatly. Before the house a young Dutch soldier paraded. He glanced suspiciously at the rooinek, who inquisitively looked over the Presidential residence.</p>
        <p>"Move on, Englishman I " he muttered, and Steve quietly obeyed. In the great Market-square, near the Parliament House and the Dutch Reformed Church, he saw scores of tents and ox-waggons placed in order round the church, which occupied the centre of the square. Sturdy-looking women, in black bombazine shirts, were "bossing up" the Kaffir servants and superintending the cooking of the midday meal. Over each wood fire . was a steaming Kaffir pot, and whenever the lids were temporarily removed from the latter a delicious appetising smell tickled the nasal organs of the bystanders. The Boer children, clad in "Sunday best," were romping about with the native boys and girls. Passing in and out amongst the waggons, Steve peered into tents and waggon-hoods, his inquisitive glance's being not unkindly met by the occupants. Soon the tables were laid for dinner, and squatting in the cool shade, the womenfolk gossiped until the service ended and the worshippers filed out of the sacred edifice. Then there was much movement for a time, but no bustle, no noise. The menfolk gave instructions to the "boys" to attend to the outspanned oxen, the women filled plates with food, the Kaffir servants ran to and fro bear- ing cooking utensils; and presently all was com- paratively still again as the Boer families sat down to dinner.</p>
        <p>"And these shock-headed, simple, devout-looking fellows are typical specimens of the fighting Boer,' eh ? " mused Steve. "Well, they look peaceful and quiet enough now, anyhow; but this being a sort of Sunday to them, they will be in a Sunday' humour. I must see more of them in their week- day state."" For many hours lie rambled about the capital, returning again to the market-place at sundown, when the Boers were inspanping their oxen and preparing for the trek homeward. The Mars were storing away cooking-pots and utensils under the body of the waggons, and here and there portly vrouws, with sleeves tucked up, were washing the last of the cups and platters. Soon the last cups of "caffee " were supped, the gossip ended, the last greeting given, and with a straining at the yokes, the long spans of oxen struggled into line as the Boers flourished their long whips and yelled: "Umbagee " "Umbagee 1" yelled the native drivers, and with shouts and yells, the waggons proceeded forward from all sides of the market-place. So ended Steve's day in the Transvaal capital. The memory of his visit remained long with him; and ofttimes, in later years, when strife and bloodshed was in the land, did he recall with plea- sure the vision of the peaceful, pleasant city, tha Mecca of the Boer—Pretoria. (To be continued.)</p>
        <p>~............</p>
