      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>I may say at the very outset that just recently I have seen anew shape in millinery which is worn very much off the face; giving a halo-like appear- ance; this hat is usually very large, and rather overwhelming to the personality of the wearer. On the other hand, the picture hat, smaller in build and tilted a goo(1 deal on one side, is worn well over the s crow broader and flatter. A new f, '‘,l,t is formed of</p>
        <p>- picatro or tr.irr,,c</p>
        <p>frills of pleated tafi lifted at one side by a huge bunch oi tile crown often composed of nrlsF.:cd flowi-r9 or the rough basket straw. It is a glorified edition of the bkSbe hat, which sprang into being in the summer time of last season. White kid toques worked and em- broidered with flower, in gold and silver and natural colours in cher.ille, or appliqne with puffed and padded chiffon flowers, are decided novelties, and the kid is not at all perishable, like chiffon and tulle.</p>
        <p>I have noticed that the fashiona,ble colours this season are light in tone. Khaki in its varieties, putty colour, mastic, and.all shades of biscuit are popular, while among more decided tints blue is certainly first favourite,. A pinky shade of ame- thyst, too, is much in evidence, and for blouses, bodice-fronts, etc., a very bright geranium-pinlc is a good deal used. Entire costumes of cream or palest biscuit cloth are already worn, but they need to be made by a first-class dressmaker, and in any case soil terribly easily. R6seda green is another colour which promises to be decidedly fashionable during the coming season. For lace, the favoured tint seems to be a deep yellowish tons of ecru, which, however, is not becoming to all people. All of the above will make 1.113 into hand- some bodices if required.</p>
        <p>I have heard a rumour that coloured velvet brocades will make the smartest eoateei in a little while, but in England our climate changes so sud- denly and quickly from cold to heat that velvets will have to stand aside in favour of light-weight silks. Peau de soie will be a wise choice; it embroiders delightfully with gold or silver braid, and can be either just touched with this embellish- ment or heavily covered with it, as the taste of the wearer may dictate. White silk, covered entirely or in part with rows of black stitching, is also used extensively for revers, cuffs, and collars of black coats, whether the latter are of cloth, velvet, or silk. Blaek silk, by the Way, promises to be more popular for jackets in the coming season than has been the case for some time past. A very stylish shape for a silk coat is open-fronted with a vest attached of white satin covered with jetted passementerie, the jacket itself being handsomely trimmed with black cord embroidery.</p>
        <p>Some sensation was caused in a London fashioa- able street recently by a lady clad in khaki cloth sla.shed with red and trimmed with round red buttons all over the chest and down the sides and front. Nothing more realistically suggestive of the horrors of war could well be imagined in a walking-costume. . .</p>
        <p>The -new way of piping or tucking the short coats of silk or cloth enables the clever tailor to do wonders in improving the figures of the wearers. The effect of number of lines slightly converging at the waist is to give the latter a look of slight- ness. When width is lacking in the figure, lines carried across add'to the apparent size, while lines carried straight down make the wearer look tall. The slender pipings are in this way utilised by practised hands to produce, so far as may be pos- sible, the ideal figure of the tailor and dressmaker, A very different shape &amp;Apra that of Nature. Lace scarves and 4. kinds of pretty filmy neck-wraps are much in vogue, and make a charming finish to a blouse worn with an open- fronted coat. The simplest type of scarf passes twice round the throat and ties in a bow with rather long ends under the chin, but there are many more elaborate styles in make-up cravats. Crepe-de-Chine and gauze scarves with fringed ends are also in favour, and some of the daintiest are delicately hand-painted with sprays of flowers. Then there are some attractive things in the way of little turn-over collars and cuffs of embroidered muslin and lace, the newest of which are worked with tiny shamrocks in pale green thread. The soft crepe and gauze scarves mentioned above are occasionally passed through a ring instead of being tied to a bow. Quaint silver rings are sometimes itilised for the purpose.</p>
        <p>Your loving Jienni.</p>
        <p>THE POLYP RON.</p>
