      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The Duke of Norfolk, who is voing to the front, in command of the Sussex Msunted lefentry, is the Earl Marshal, and premier duke of England, besides being Postmaster-Gemmel, and a member of the Government. It may be hoped that most of the fighting will be over before those men leaving will arrive at the seat of war, but the Duke will certainly be away some months, and in the meantime there is the great publics department to be carried on, upon which we depend to continue ou: business and correspondence. It has been suggested, that, as in the case ef other volunteers and reservists, the Duke's place " should be kept open for him," and in the meantime Mr.Henniker-Heatoreappointed temporary Postmaster, when he would be able to give Us universal penny postage, and all those ether great postal reforms he bats so Long advocated. We might then see a leading Governmeet dapartment relieved of its regime of WA Tape, and it would be interesting to see it it then really gave us all those great improvements which the re- formers are always promising us, if they only bad their way. The Duke is not ening te South Africe for the "fun of the thing." but rether as a personal exsmple to the Yeomen and others, of whose services the country has need. Es believes that a large landewner has special obligations of this chanctor, and therefere does not hesitate to take his place, in the Seems% Corps, which he , has been largely instrumental in raising for service at the front. There tire already four threes servine in the wart all of whom are comearatively youne. There is the Duke of Tech, who is a tittle ever thirty, the Duke ef Marlborough who is twenty-nine, the Duke of Rox- burgh who is twenty-four, and the Duke of Westminster who is tea youngeat, hsviese enly just turned twenty- one. The Duke ot Norfolk is however, fitty-two,an agel when a man must take his fighting like everything else in life, somewhat seriously. Eise of course, like most of the members of the older peerage, cornea of a race of warriors. The first Duke of Nerfolk was killed in the battle of Bosworth in 1485, after bang made Earl Marshal for his services in the French wars. The second Duke lost his life in the battle of Flodden Field. The third and fourth Dukes also dame te violent ends, but these were on the scaffold, or, at least, under circumutanees when displeased menarche weld to relieve their feelings by simply saying" off with their head,'." The present Dolts is the fifteenth of the line, and as is well-known is a prominent member of the Roman Catholic Church. Parisian hotel-keepers are looking forward to a -golden harvest this year, in view of the early opening of the International Extiibition- The Presto's capital is always a costly place for visit3rs, bet this sernmer the price of everything will be very much more than ustitl, and to people of limited means this uncertain element of ex- pense is an important point in the matter of making holiday arrangements for the year. Some idea of the increased charges they be gathered from the foreign "Handbook and Guide" of the Cyclists Touring Club. Among a list of hotels, usually patronised by visitors from the country, it mention. one which intends to charge 29 francs a thy instead of lilf. 50e. for board and lodging at ordinary times. Another charging 101. 7511. at present, will raise its tariff for the same *COULD- needation to 15 francs, and another which usually asks 121. 50e. will put up its price to 18 francs. It will be seen that this is about half as mach again, and taking everything at the same proportion, this is probably the least one must he prepered te pee, on account of the Ex- hibition, ever and above the Ritual Parisian charges. There is another class of people besides the hotel keepers who expect to reap a golden harvest in Paris this year, and that is the light-fingered gentry of all stations. From all quarters the rogues and vent:mods of all coun- tries are making !leeks for the French capital and according to the Parisian papers, there ere already several well-known London thieves loafing about the Exhibition buildings, no doubt with a view Is studying tbe topography of the place, before the time of aotion arrives. There is a special police force put on during the Exhibition season—oleo who are specially familiar with all the known scamps and pickpockets of any re- pute, and there is no doubt that, an a police force gener- ally, that of Paris is second to none in the world. No doubt the popularity of Rebia Hood among the demeeraey of his time, was largely duo to the reputation which he had for robbing, only the rieb, and for reliev- ing the necesaities of the poor with the goods whieh he stole. The modern pickpocket on the other hand, se:m- amas himself little, as to whom he rolos,—and is just as ready to steal the purse of a poor person, as of a rich one. There was, for example, a recent cause in which a young man was shown to have abetractad £2 from the pocket of a woman whose husband. having gone to work at a distance, had sent her the money in order te enable her and the children to live. Sines the theft she told the magistrate, she had been nearly starving. The perpetra- tor of the mean and heartless offence was sent to prison fee six months, steps being taken to provide some tem- porary assistance for the prosecutrix. Sir John Leag, in speaking at the annual ladies' dinner at the National Liberal Club, rejoiced that the time hod gone by when the ordinary clubman was shocked at the idea of women ente:ing his stub. The social and politisal influence of women had, he said vastly increated, and showld be frankly recognised and encouraged. That this is the tendency of the times is shown by the fact that at least a Cozen or twenty among the leading London clubs, now open their doors to women.</p>
        <p>Miss Simmer Morten, who responded to the principal toast of the evening at the dinner of the National Liberal Club,dealt rather with the lighter side if human nature, than with serious politics. Women bad, is the long past, done the cooking, and providing ot dinners, as well as suffered there being grumbled at, and she web- coedit as a great advance, that they were new in- vited to participate in them, especially as she had dis- covered that men measure most things by their meals, and could scarcely du them a greater honour than share them with the other sex en these public occasions. Miss Morten had also made another ehrewd diseevery, and that is, that men cannot be truly companionable unless they can smoke. That Irish colours and Irish fabrics will be very fashionable this summer, goes without saying. Poplin is already in great demand especially for wedding and tea gowns. and different kinds of Irish lace, including guipure, Limerick, Carrick:necrosis. and crochet, are much used for trimming spring blouses. Irish trine taller made gowns are being built to extensively and the colours displayed are beyond compare, more particu- larly those refined and exquisite shades of pink, blue, green, and mauve. Coloured transparencies will be the feature of the coming summer, and in muslin some beautiful creations are in progress. White eilk slips are employed Isy lead- ing modistes for coloured meslins or other transper.-n- -cies, but coloured silk slips will still be permissible with flewered white rnusline,provided they match the leading tone en evidence in the pattern on _the muslin. As to whether these particular frocks will boast full gathered skirts yet remains a matter of uncertainty. " MADGE."</p>
      </div>
