      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>TEE GREAT MISSION OF 1901.</head>
        <p>TEE GREAT MISSION OF 1901.</p>
        <p>The enthusiastic adoption of the Rev. Thomas Law's project for a united simultaneous mission all over England and Wales early next year in itself makes the Sheffield meetings epoch-making. The mission is intended for all classes, not only the congregations and ad- herents of the churches and the working-classes —these ordinarily influenced by missions—but the leisured and cultured classes also. The Rev. J. Tolefree Parr, whl read an excellent paper on the subject, estimAt ,s that the mission will add at least 100,000 merobers to the churches. Mr. Price Hughes, who gave his enthusiastic support to the project, thinks that Mr. Parr's much to modest an estimate of the likely</p>
        <p>Rev. F. W. Bonnie, who said that he saw no reason why 1,000,000 should not be the number in London alone. Mr. Hughes would add 3,000,000 more for the provinces. He described the proposal as the most practical one ever submitted to a• religious assembly. By the way, the April Free Church Chronicle contains some interesting and important letters on the mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>FREE cHumoir GIRLS' GUILD</head>
        <p>THE FREE cHumoir GIRLS' GUILD</p>
        <p>Next to the simultaneous mission, this was, perhaps, the most important business that came bef.re t 1 C.,uncit. Mrs. Get »e ();kdbury and Mr,. l'••!,_9; llthlth,;,mt e(hit,A. i the soect in two exeullent pipers. Mrs. Ca;ittury made a strong point of co-operation with the Y.W.C.A., which, in fact, is provided for by a clause in the constitution. Mrs. Bunting put in a word for "a mother's union." Dr. Townsend, who moved the adoption of the scheme in a powerful and convincing speech, said that the Executive Committee was of opinion that the Free Churches ought to have a Girls' Guild of their own. A delightful speech in support of the Guild was made by Marianne Farningham (of the Christian World), who had a very cordial reception. She said that the girls societies of the country had not secured one hundredth part of the girls that ought to be secured. The scheme was unanimously and en- thusiastically adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>PRESIDENT'S ADDRESS.</head>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>Great things were expected of the Rev. C. 11. Kelly, the President, and he did not disappoint his friends. His address, entitled "Retrospect and Prospect," was a manly, straightforward, vigorous, rousing utterance. He kept his audience keenly interested for about an hour. Some of his anecdotes were delicious. For instance: "I saw recently an extract from a Church record to this effect: Mr. and Mrs. -- in the order of Divine Providence have during the year removed to London. This church has lost nothing by the removal, and has no regret to express."! Again, "Popery is still the foe of tree liberty-, of free speech, of private judgment. In the lands where it has been dominant, ignorance, superstition, and moral evils prevail. It always rears it own monu- ment. In a great grave ground lies the body of a well-known doctor. His epitaph is most complimentary. Its final sentence is, If you wish to see his work look around you!' Look around you--and you will see the graves of his poor, dead patients. In any land where Popery has ruled, if you want to know the results of its work and teaching, 'look around,' and you will see you are where that which is best for humanity lies buried." Mr. Kelly proved himself a strong as well as a genial President. The programme of work was heavy, and if it had not been for Mr. Kelly's skill, suavity, and firmness, there might have been, at times, rather serious congestion.</p>
        <p>THE ORGANISING SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Thomas Law. the Organising Secre- tary of the National Council, was faced by a crowded audience when ho rose to submit his annual Report. This document grows longer year by year. In accordance with custom, the ex-President, Dr. Mackennal, moved its adop- tion. He described it as "more important and more interesting than any document of the kind which Mr. Law has previously submitted to us." Dr. Mackennal dwelt on the importance of making early and ade- quate preparation for the united mission of next year. Dr. Townsend, who seconded, paid Mr. Law some glowing compliments, noting particularly his indefatigableness and skill. Dr. Monro Gibson referred to the excellence of the work done by Mr. Law's staff. He con- gratulated Mr. Law on his skill in surrounding himself with such able men. The Rev. Hirst Hollowell spoke of the "extremely valuable labours of Mr. Law. I never go into any region where the Rev. Thomas La* has not been, or is not now there, or is not coming soon." It would be useless to attempt to sum- marise the Report We understand that gratis copies may be had by application to the Publi- cation Department of the Council, Memorial Hall, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>ABSENTBES.</head>
        <p>ABSENTBES.</p>
        <p>Illness took considerable liberties with the programme of the meetings. The President- elect, the Rev. J. G. Greenburgh, M.A., of Leicester, was prevented by indisposition from hearing the acclamation with which the Council ratified the selection of him to the highest post within its gift. The Rev. F. W. Macdonald, the President of the Wesleyan Conference, was to have preached the annual Council sermon, but he was ill and could not come. His place was taken by his predecessor in the Chair of the Conference, the Rev. H. Price Hughes, M.A. The Rev. R. J. Campbell, 8.A., has gone to South Africa to work for the Federation movement there. Mr. Herbert Lewis, M.P., who was to have discussed secondary educa- firm, was also amung the absentees. Mr. Lloyd George, M.P., was detained in the House of Commons to give his vote in favour of Mr. Albert Spicer's Bill for the extension of the Welsh Sunday Closing Act to Monmouthshire, and consequently the mass meeting at the Albert Hall was deprived of his fervid and vigorous eloquence. The Rev. Marshall Hartley was also among the absentees on account of illness. All the sermons and papers are to be published verbatim in the Free Church Year Book.</p>
        <p>"THE FREE CHURCH CATECHISM."</p>
        <p>"The Free Church Catechism," though it it only fifteen months since it was published, has already been translated into Welsh, French, and Italian. There is now in preparation, says the Free Churchirean, a version in the Bobangi tongue spoken in the Upper Congo. The Rev. John tongue, of the Baptist Missionary Society, Lukolela, says that the Catechism contains just such a statement of Christian i truth as s needed for the mission converts *gad scholars</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>JP P S 'IS</head>
        <p>JP P S 'IS</p>
        <p>G-3ATEFEJL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>44. 0 A</p>
        <p>:7'.REAKFAST AND SUPPER,</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>STEVE, THE OUTLANDER.</p>
        <p>By ARTHUR LAYCOCK, Author of "The Young Gaffer," "Seaside Sketches,ll "lAncashire Sketches," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Steve Hart, a factory operative, in love with Ella Ray- burn, another factory hand, is compelled by the maiiciousness of young Cartleigh, the factory manager, to leave his post. He determines to go to South Africa. Cartleigh intends, meanwhile, to win Ella from Lim. As soon as Hart has gone Cartleigh lays his plies, and seeks Ella's company in and out of work hours. By his disgraceful course of life he has alienated his parents, but, spurred by his pur- suit of Ella, he determines to reform outwardly, so as not to be cast off by them. He makes good pro- gress with the girl, in spite of the fact that she hears from her absent love. By an incautious attempt to make love too quickly, however, Cart- leigh loses some ground again. He is, moreover, in the toils of one Barrett, a racing man, to *hom he owes money, and on the threat of exposure he steals £5O from his father.</p>
        <p>• CHAPTER XL So many • other matters had occupied Ralph Cartleigh's attention of late, that he had given but little thought to Ella Rayburn. He had seen her passing to and from work con- tinuously, had gone out of his way occasionally to • saunter through the weaving-shed and pass her alley, but beyond this the pair who had made such gay frolic on the morn of the Buxton holiday, saw little of each other. Ella had heard that Master Ralph was blamed for the loss of fifty pounds of "wage-brass " ; she had further heard that one of the spinners had seen him fighting in an Oldham tavern, and also that there was much trouble between Cartleigh, senior, and his recalcitrant son. This gossip affected Ella but slightly. She assured herself that she was now perfectly indifferent to all that concerned her quondam, dashing admirer. If he was such a wastrel and ne'er-do- weel, she would be better without him; be- sides. Steve was doing well now, and it behoved her to keep herself above suspicion. Early in December, however, she received a letter from Steve whit h disconcerted her considerably. His "bright prospects" were soon dimmed. There was much disaffection amongst the Outlanders of Johannes- burg, of whom he was one. Business was almost at a standstill, hands were being discharged on the mines; he was out of work and likely to be until the bother about the franchise and other reforms was settled. Since the Jameson Raid the Boers had become bumptious and blustering, and, instead of redressing grievances, had added to the troubles of the unfortunate Outlanders. , "Though this Outlander was satisfied with things as he found them," he added, significantly. "I was coining money, and so were all the fellows about me, but the Reform men told us we were oppressed and they kicked up a row, so we've had to join them, and I'm an aggrieved Outlander now, because I can't get back to work." Ella could not grasp the meaning of the political references, but what was of much more import to her, she recognised that Steve was not "coining money," as heretofore, and she relapsed into her old disaffected state. Dame Rayburn did not cease to rail at Steve; in season and out of season she sought to prejudice her daughter against the absent lover, contrasted the prospects of marriage with a man of "quality," with marriage with a plebeian like Stephen Hart. "We should be able to live in rare style, lass, if you married one o' th' quality, a factory-mestur's son, for instance," she said, reverting to her familiar theme on the night that Steve's letter was received. "Just think o' what it would mean, lass, if you really were wed to such a one." "Don't talk like that, mother," protested the girl. "Factory-masters' sons aren't always the best of men, even if they do possess money and social position." "Oh, I know who you're thinkin' of," said Mrs. Rayburn, pettishly. 'lt's young Mestur Cartleigh you have i' mind, but let me tell you, Ella, I don't believe he's as bad as some folks reckon he is. I've heard some o' th' talk 'at's bin goin' round lately about him, but r don't believe one halve of it. Young fellies will be young fellies, an' I daresay Mestur Ralph may be a bit wild, but he'll tame down in time, they all do, an' he'll not mak' a bad sort of husbant, I'll warrant." "If you are thinking of him as a likely husband for me, mother, you are a long way out of your reckoning," the girl rejoined, spiritedly. She was annoyed at her mother's persistence in revert- inr to a subject which was daily becoming more ob2toxious to her. "I'm not hankering after a factory-master's son," she added, "especially one who will be cut off with a shilling." "Oh! that's it, is it ? " cried her mother, as if she had made an Important discovery. "You've heard that tale, have you, lass ? But don't you believe it. He'll not be cut off with no shillin', never fear. His fayther may threaten that, an' may call him for all 'at's bad, but yo'll see he'll be remembered in th 'bit o' papper when th' owd man's dead. They reckon to be down on th' wild 'uns, but they allus have a tender thowt (thought) for 'em at th' bottom. I know. Look how many times th 'owd man has threatened to turn Mestur Ralph away altogether, an' how many times th' lad's bin given another chance. I tell yon, Ella, if yo'll only mind your p's an' q's, an' make your- sel' agreeable to Ralph Cartleigh, yo'll find him a fish 'at's worth catchin'." "I'm not going to offer mySelf as bait," said Ella, with dignity. "I had quite enough of the fish on the Wakes trip." "Well, I know nowt of what he did to offend you on th' Wakes trip," said her mother, "but I know this, as young lasses canno' afford to be too touchy an' particular these days; though by that, lass, of course, I dunnot mean 'at you must make yoursel' too cheap either with Ralph Cartleigh or any- one else." Continual dropping wears away the stone. By her persistent efforts Widow Rayburn gradually induced her daughter to regard the factory-master's son more favourably.</p>
        <p>_ Christmas Day in Dalesworth came with ideal Christmas weather, fine and frosty, and was there- fore gladly welcomed by young and old, for though the latter, by, reason of age and infirmities, some- times long for a mild Christmas, yet they truly rejoice more when a "seasonable' one comes. Quiet were the huge factories, smokeless the tall chimneys, for the operatives were making merry holiday. Skating parties were out skimming over the mill-lodges and the reservoirs of the district. Ella Rayburn and several girl friends wended their way to Rhine Lake, and over its glittering surface skated to their hearts' content. The exhilarating exercise was productive of gay spirits amongst the skaters and none there was livelier than pretty Ella who darted to and fro, chatting and laughing as if the world held no care for her. One portion of the lake was prohibited to skaters, and here a "danger" board was prominently placed. Several of the more adventurous spirits, heedless of the warning notice, skated over the forbidden spot. Ella, being chased by one of her companions in a game of "tig," rushed towards the danger- patch, and slipping, fell with her full weight on the thin ice. In a moment the ice cracked and gave way, and,the unfortunate girl was sinking benonth the cold water, when a strong hand gripped her jacket, and, miraculously, she was hauled from her terrible position and borne to the hard, firm ice beyond. The man who had thus skimmed swiftly along the Margin of the treacherous ice and rescued her from a watery grave, was Ralph Cf.Artleigh. His feat was, indeed, marvellous. Skating along at full speed a short distance away, he had seen the girl's danger, and rushing head- long forward, with a determination to "chance all or nothing," he had succeeded in doing what few men could have accomplished. The impetus he had gained in dashing up, carried him and his burden forward into safety. Ella was conveyed to an adjacent house, and after a change of clothing, was little worse for her partial immersion. Ralph Cartleigh remained about, and as Ella came from the house congratu- lated her. She, in return, thanked him warmly, and together they returned to Dalesworth in a carriage which the young man engaged. The exciting incident had been witnessed by scores of people, and soon all Dalesworth was dis- cussing the affair; and tributes to young Cartleigh's bravery and presence of mind were many. "If he is a wastrel an' a rake, he's not a coward anyhow," said the gossips. "A mon as 'll do a deed like that isn't all bad. There is good parts in him somewheer ; an' p'raps it's not all his fault if they have no' bin found out." Ralph was the hero of the hour; his name was on everyone's lips. News of his exploit reached his home, and James Cartleich was gratified to find that for once at least</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CHAPTER XII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XII.</p>
        <p>Christmas Day in South Africa was much different from Christmas Day in England. So Stephen Hart discovered as he went out into the streets of Johannesburg at an early hour. A scorch- ing sun, flashing out from a cloudless sky, made him seek the shady side of the street. Little knots of men were gathered about at street-corners, or in twos and threes were seated on the stoeps of the boarding-houses discussing the political situation. As Steve passed by, he heard many references to the events of twelve months before, when the Jameson Raid occurred and Johannesburg was like a city in a state of siege, with shops and houses barricaded and troops of Outlander Volunteers drilling in its streets and squares. Since then matters had gone from bad to worse, and the Golden City was under a cloud. Many meetings of Outlanders had been held of late, calling upon the Transvaal Government to grant various measures of reform. Petitions had been signed, and much fuss had been made abou the franchise for the Outlander.</p>
        <p>Stephen Hart was in the position of the average working-man on the Rand—quite indifferent to a vote.</p>
        <p>Truth to tell, up to this time he had given no attention to political affairs.; his whole thought had been of his work and how best he could make "his little pile." That work should be stopped and everything brought to a standstill, was a matter of keen regret to him. He was told that the Boers were to blame for this stoppage of work; they were determined to injure the mining industry, and harass and inconvenience the mine-owners and everybody else; they were absolutely to blame for everything that went wrong. The Boer was a stupid, ignorant, blustering fool, and his Government must be got rid of, or at least must be so reformed as to come into line with Outlander ideas of what a Government should be.</p>
        <p>That a Government controlled by these benighted, boorish Boers, should oppress, unfairly tax, and altogether treat as inferiors, a majority of intelli- gent, enterprising, educated Outlanders, was intolerable. The Boer and his Government must go, or both be radically, reformed. So said the leaders of public opinion; so said the local newspapers' so said the man in the street. Stephen Hart, like most mine-workers in Johan- nesburg, had seen very little of the Boers. Occasionally he had noticed them come into the market-place of the city, early in the morning, with their huge waggons, drawn by big spans of oxen; he had seen these farmers transact their business and quietly go out of the city, and trek away to I.4eir homesteads, far away across the veld. \To Steve, therefore, these men appeared inoffen- sive, decent, civil fellows. They compared favour- ably with the cunning cheats and rogues who infested the market-place, the canteens, and plague spots of the city. They even appeared to be more desirable fellows than many of the habitues of Stock and Sharedom—the crafty, keen men who assembled daily between "the chains" in Commissioner-street. Of this latter class, Stephen had seen much, and was not favourably impressed with them. Compared with the typical Outlander, the stolid, taciturn, slow-moving, slow-thinking Boer appeared wofully behindhand. The former was alert, sharp, keen, ever' on the look-out for a chance of striking a good bargain, ' constantly seeking to "make money," restless, untiring, ever wideawake. From all parts of the universe these Outlanders had come, with one all- absorbing thought in their minds, one sole-Object ever before them, and for which they were con- stantly scheming, working, cheating, slay ing—gold. Many unscrupulous, selfish, grabbing, soulless men were there in this class; many who were as honest and upright as the peculiar circumstances and conditions of life in the Golden City would nermit them in he. - _ .</p>
      </div>
