        <p>the war. (Hear, hear and laughter). They wer heartily and unanimously the friends of freedom an liberty and equal rights for all sorts of thinkers an speakers. In a naeetinz like that they hai a momentou, duty to discharge in the asertion of the right of Eng nehmen to perfectly</p>
        <p>:UNFETTERED A.ND UNHINDVRED IFREEDODI OF SPEECH. (Hear, hear and applause.) Recent disturbances which had been reported up and down the country had been nothing other than mob law. Riotous proceadiaas by which meetings called by an extreme ,sectiou of peace advocates had been forcibly broken up, of physical violence made spon persons who advocated obnoxious views, and of assaults on their houses, their business premises or private property were dishonourable) to their country and to them as Englishmen. (Hear, hear.) They as Free chnrcbmen entered their protest against the unseemly and unruly conduct which had so disgraced those who indulged in it and made their demand for the protection of speakers of all kinds so that they might be able to speak freely what was in them without being afraid of any physical results which might follow itlie was bound to say that in his opinion it was probable such meetings as those which had been broken up were in the present strong feeling of public opinion, not quite opportune. At the same time whether opportune or not, it was perfectly certain that in this country they must stand for unfettered, and unhindered freedom of speech. (Applause). There was one other way in which the Free Caurch Council might accomplish very useful work. He referred to the old idea which had been carried on with great success and great benefit to churches in large centres of population of a</p>
