      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>HOUSE TO HOWE VISITATIOU</head>
        <p>HOUSE TO HOWE VISITATIOU</p>
        <p>with the objectof - ascertaining whether those visited were in attendance at any church or their children at any Sunday School, and inviting them to come into per- sonal contact with one form or other of the vari- eels forms of church life. He noticed that the South Mancherer Section of the Manchester and Salford Free Church Council had just completed a house to house visitation. The visitation covered about 15,000 homes and with very few exceptions the visitors were well received. Owing to the incompleteness of some returns it was not possible to aummarise the results f more than 13,000 houses, whieh were visited by 21 churches. Taking that number, 4530 houses were reoor- te I as attached to one or other of the Evaneellcal Free Churches, 3,830 to the Established. and 880 to the Reman Catholic Church, while 190 belonged to other than those named. Thus 9,430 professed to belong te some section of the Church. At 1,940 houses it was I stated that they did not attend any church, while from ; 450 the occupiers went anywhere, In 810 cases an answer to the inquiries was either refused or that given was unsatisfactory. The total number was further made up by persons belonging to other nationalities than English or by houses standing empty. In brief, they had out of the 13,000 houses 9,430 hoisses professing asso- ciation with some particular church. 3,200 having no such religious connection. With one consent the reports said that those engaged in visiting had been strongly impressed with the manifest indifference with which religion was regarded. Continuing, the speaker remarked that in all probability the report referred to was very much more favourable than Wel, would find in Warrington. He appealed to the Free Churches of the town to unite in carrying out this domiciliary visit and bring about a state of greater interest and Christian sympathy in the town in which they lived. (Applaus?..)</p>
        <p>- _ ,11 THE PRINCIPLES FOR WHICH WE STAND." The Rev. C W L CHRISTIEN then addressed the meet- ing on this topic. He said their principles were very largely in their Bible. They stood there as the repre- sentatives of different evangelical churches feeling that they were branches of the one invisible church of Christ. That seemrd a common place with th,,m and yet it seemed necessary these days to empbas:ze it. Tney held that they might differ in ecclesiastical policy and yet belong to the one true church of Jesus (Hear, hear.) Christ did not lay down any hard and fast lines for church government. God's plan, so far as he could underwarld, was to give the principle and leave the adaptation to circumstances. They were different members of the one church of the Lord Jesus Christ. When one church claimed for itself apostolic succession. which lay at the base of all sacerdo- talism, they as Free Churchmen were! entitled to ask for proof and not mere assertion. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
