      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>FREE CHIJ;--;CH COUNCIL</head>
        <p>FREE CHIJ;--;CH COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the . Warrington Branch.• There was ouly a moderate attendance at thei annual meeting of the Warrington and District Free Church Council held in Bold-street Schoolroom on Thursday last wedc.The Bev John Yonsze(president) Occupied the chair and he was accompanied on the Platform by the Revs C IV L Christien (Runcorn), F Matthews (Latchford). (.; M Hocken, E T Reed, and Councillor Worthington, J.P. ( Wigan.) THE ANNEfAL REPOR The Rev. C. H. HOCKEN preeentel t,he annual state-. Meat which showed that the expenses were £1.2 158 BJ., which were 4591 in exc.as of the receipts. As tar as their finances were concerned their position was very. satisfactory. During the year the Council kid met once and the executive committee four times. A number of 'Public questions were dealt with. A clmrnittee bad been formed to arrange for Free Church services at the Workhouse, and these had been carried on during the past year. A resolution was 'sent to the Parr Hall Sub-committee with reference to the holdine of Sunday concerts for money payments. and another resolution W 5 torwarded to the Government on the Clergy Rating sill. The Council had also applied to the central authority for a loan of books. They had also dealt with such a ,question as the bill prohibiting the sale of intoxicating nth* to children and the Member for War- rington had acknoweed4ed the receipt of the resolution and promised to give the very hest support possible to the measure. (Applause.) The Council had also taken an interest in the famine in India. (H.ar, hear.) A resolution of congratulation was also s.nt to the Mayor, expressing their great satisfaction and gratifica— tion at the election of one so wartuti- ;ft top with the Free Church Council. The Mayor ti,,cl kindly acknowledged the same and exprmsed recipr -teal interest in their work. (Hear, hear.) ADDRESS BY THE CHAIRM a.N The CHAIRMAN said he supposed tret the Conned vie sO thoroughly well understood in the t -wn that it .vs a 6"Perfluity to explain that the Ccunctl consistent of ministers and delegates irorn as in ev of the free evangelical churches in ths town a:. sho e r.hernse'Yes 'willing to join in fellowshie, for ire that end ae. teal Christian service. It was fouride 'pen the g eat idea of Christian unity, ani thee hon d its means to help on th^ realisation of their great Master's prayer that all His disciples mi t n one. The eaker proceeded to answer the questi e. of what use it for the Free Churches of the town to unite in Council for conference and cn-orer ion in stun se."":k, by reading some extracts from thm Rev C. H. filly's address at the ineetinv ol the National Federa- tion. Proceeding he said he wished to emphasize Tin PROTESTANT ASPECT u_r)f their Free Church organizmiJti. Ho wished them to r Convinced of this that the Protestantism of this coun- :lT had come to be in these days in the sole charge of the Evangelical Free Churches. (Hear, hear.) The old ,!swer to those who objected to the principle of a S:-ate Church,, was that the existence of the State Church was absolutely necessary, as the bul- wark of Protestantism. They need only be acquainted With the actual state of things to-day to be Perfectly certain that it was altogether uselcss to talk about the Established Church being the bulwark of Protestantism. A large proportion of the clergy dis- dained and scorned the very term, Protestant. They claimedi as their own the term catholic. In a sense all Christians were catholics because Christianity was a reiigion• for the world and for all classes of naen. But what the Established Church meant Was quite another thirg. With them and the ()man Catholic Church it meant sacerdetalism ; the'lre- ugion of the pries', ; !priestly absolution ; of priestly sacrifice and priestly altar. The Established Church Was no longer the bulwark of Protestantism ; but the pitrenclied upholder of sacerdotalisra and agaiest that the Evangelical Free Churches of every kind are the only, effectual protest we had to-day. They worn Making their principles all the more palpable ;Ind for- cible the steadfast unity arnenest themselves. (Ap- plause). There were other was in which the Free Laurch Council was of the rreatest use. It waa of great 14.80 in giving a platform to the .Free Church on which they could unite in exprening ,l'eir esp;nions on great social and religious matters. Thee did not admit ItsttY Politics in the Evangelical Fr:e Chttrch Council. a meeting like that they followed the example of the Conference a few days ago and did net even talk about</p>
      </div>
