        <p>sfra. asalaT.wkw._4.' L7_ . _</p>
        <p>THE EXAIVIINEE, FRIDAY, MARCH 30 1900. AROUND THE BULLETIN BOARD.</p>
        <p>frem a catechism which had been written and published by the . Rev. Obert Gace, a vicar of the Eat iblished Church, wieich ought to Dissent was a great sin ; that it was wicked to enter a meeting house ; and that Diseenters were heretics. Proceeding, he said he was aware that some in the Establideed Caurch woulJ disown, such a catechism, but if they as Free Church- men,cnce granted the fiction of apus.olic succession they could, not get away from such a catechism. They could not agree to such a fin 1m that the church of Christ that was to he governed in one set form, and that it had come dewn in unbroken suc- cession. It was a d eetrine which could not be proved in any shape or term from the word of the Most, High God. Threes who believed such a doctrine inieht call all thuse who disbelieved it, denominations, communities, communions or bodies, but they felt bound with the Bible before them and history before them to call them- Alves various branches of the one universal church of Christ. (Hear, hear, and applause). If they accepted the doctrine of apostolic succession they would have to believe that it had come through the fox-hunting par- sons of last century aad of theenen in the Roman church in the dark ages who were stained through and through with every nameable vice and every possible crime. Some of the foulest beings who ever disgraced the world were the ropes of Rome. Even their own authorities were not prepared to wholly deny it and excused them- selves on the plea that there were only a few of them. And yet if the Apostolic succession were unbroken, as was contended, the .charm had come down through them. As meg of common-sesse they declined to believe it ou the ipse dixit of any section of the church. Those who did not believe in apostolic succession were told that they died in the sin of schism. If that were true then the great majority of Christians who composed the free churches of England. Scotland, the United States, Canada and Australia had died in that sin. Tale uni.rue doctrine of apostolic succession made it clear that the great majority of the men and women who God had saved from their sins and woo had given every evidence of their religious life were outside the church that Christ told them was His own. As Free Churchmen they be- lieved only in the priesthood of Christ Jesus. (Applause). There was not one assertion in the New Teetament that would ever lead any unbiassed man to believe teat there reamins in the Ceurch of Christ an official priest. Toe dark shadow at the priest was to be seen upon every fair country that it passed over. After defining the faith of the Evangelical Free Churches, the speaker said they must pray until all held the faith a:, it is in Jesus and not the traditiens of men. (Applause).</p>
        <p>FREE CEIURCR FEDERAFION AND TEUPERANCE</p>
        <p>LEGISLATION.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. :F. MATTHEWS read the following paper on "Free Church Federation a ,c 1 Temperance Legislation." Our Federation of Frei? Chumbes hat., been impossible.he said, but for the supposition that there was a "unity" of life aud aitwderivee, from our common loyalty to Jesus Curist as the Redeemer of men. Not that it by any moans is the ideal some of us have of "complete unity.' Some day, perhaps, I may be permitted to speak on an "ideal Church on earta." Federation. however, has done one thing for us. It has brought together all the finest possibilities for good that lie within our respective communions. It is fi r more possible now for a "United Free "Church to speak with one conviction upon matters of civic and.national moment, particularly where great moral ii,sees are at stake. The time has come also when we must COO, to talk of our power and to exert it. There never was a</p>
        <p>opportunity of drinking, and this is the kind of proLec- tion many people require. Now let me come to the minority report itselt apart from the sanction it gives to two or three points I hay,' now touched up,au. It inu6t not be forgutLan that its lecommendatieus Will 134 wade the bala of future temperance legislation. I vecituie to think that it</p>
        <p>FAIIIS THE CENTRE around which must cluster ae the reforming forces of our organized ti mperence anti church life. Here are a few of its proposals :-1. Compensation from public fuuds " cannot tor one moment be entertained." 2. If any compensation is to be paid, it must be reised by the trade i;self. 3. A large reduction in the number of public-houses is greatly needed. One on-licensed house to every 750 persons in towns and 400 in country dis- tricts is suggested as a maximum number. 4. At the end of seven years in England and Wales and five years in Sue land the licensing authority should have power to reduce the certificates below the statutory maximum, and no compensation whatever would be given. 5. The new licensing autlacrity should consist of a committee of from tax to ten members for each division. Half ot the tneenbers should be cnosen by the justices of the division from their own number, the other half by the &amp;Aunty Council or the lawn Council. 6. There will be no appeal to Quarter Sessions. 7. A new appellate buoy to to substituted thereror ; (a) in borougns to censist of a proportion of toe original licensing autho- rity, half to be elected by the Town Council and half by the borougla justices ; (b) in the counties, the county justices and County Council shall elect an equal number to a Coesity Appeal Committee. 8. Men interested in the trade are thereby disqualified from serving on Watch Committees in bar stages. Deis would be a fine thing for Warrington, particularly if the Chief Constable's position be made secure, by enacting, as is proposed, that he be removable only with the consent of the Home Secretary. 9. The bona fide traveller .nuisance will be swept away, 10. The eb nition of hack doors to public-houses. These, together with reduction of the hours of cpening on Sunday, viith the Real abolition of Sunday trading altogether, the present extension of Sunday closing to Monmouthshire, and the prohibition of the sale of beer, wine, and spirits to children under 16 years of age. are —to sum up—a few of the most prominent recommendations of the minority report. Our attitude as Christian reformers, and as Free Cnurcheien, I suggest should be one of cordial approval and accept- ance. Hew may we work for their embodiment in the law of the land? (1.) I suguest as Free Churchmen that we educate this constituency to begin with. (2. That any man, be he Tory or Liberal, who seeks ou affrages sleeted be" sounded," and his approval gained to the principles upon which we agree, and that to free our town from its drunkenness we ought only to send men to our Town Council who have neitaer sympathy with nor interest in the drink traffic. We have it in our power to make our Watch Committee different from what it is without waiting for these reeornmendations to become law. You ask me whether I expect these things to becsme part of the law of the laud. I say emphatically, yes! It does not require a man to be a prophet, either major or minor, to deduce from the tone of the House of Commsus in its recent action on the temperance questions broeght before it the fact that their action has had the marked approval of the country. 1 rue, 388 of its members may be ticketed as favourable to the trade ! True, Mr. Balfour is only a half-hearted supporter of temperance legislation. True, the Home Secretary disapproves of it altogether. True, the trade has the money and men and a capital organ- ization ; but what is there on the other side? You have your large temperance organizationee you have the growing feeling among all classes of social reformers that temperance legislation is needed ; you have the moral sentiment of the geaeral public aroused by the moderate proposals of the Peel Cenemission,and yeti have the united and organized foreesef the Free Cburches.l I anticipate during the next five years that same if net at the proposals referred to this evening will have become permanently establehed amongst us. And what for the future? You have to remember that in the next tea years you will have to reckon with two new forces which at present are felt in the full tide of their power. The Christian Endeavour Societies are full of young temperance enthusiasts, many of whom will be- house- holders with votes. You are reinforcing our Bands of Hope with seientifio teaching ,on temperance in day schools. These yeung people twenty years hence will be voters. Is all this accumulating power going to be wasted ? And finally our cnurchas are not societies for cultivating a fad, but for bringiug men face to face with evil and sin, from which they are taught to flee, and face to face with good which they are taught to welcome and embrace; and back of all our power is a living and a righteous God " who is siftieg out the hearts of men before His judgment seat" and I ask with ' the certain answer of present and future victory" If Ged be for us who can be against ua ?" ADDRESS BY COUNCILLOR WORMINGTON.</p>
        <p>Councillor WORTEGINGTON, Cot Wigan, woo is the chair- man of the West LancashireFederk,tion of Free Churches, also addressed the meeting. He said he rejoiced in the unity of the Free Churches. He was afraid that wrong conclusions had been drawn in the past from the seclusiveness of the Free Churches. The Free Church Council was an organisation which would do much to awaken Free Church life in this country. The work before them was to make men and women. boys and girls like angels and make earth like heaven. They would sae that there was a mighty work to be done. Every member of the Free Churches was as much called to this work as any reverend geotleman in that room or outside it. This was the work which had been laid on our shoulders. The work of the Free Church Conn eil was a spiritual work more than anything- else. No single ch, rch or denomination was able to do all the wink which devolved upon them as members of Christ's church. It required a council ot all Ch.: churches to organise and distribute the work. They needed to come into contact with the individual life of the town, and if they did so he predicted the greatest possible reeuhs. In order to solve the social problems of the day they needed to reach the heart of the individual. They might improve a man's environment but if any lasting benefit was to result it was necessary to change the man himself by making him feel his sonship with Christ. The speaker illustrated his meaning by in- stances which had come under his own observation. Proceeding to refer to the position of Free Churchmen he said they were in this majority in this country. They would have to be the preservers of Protes- tantism in this country. The Established Church was already divided on this subject and the High Church party was its the ascendent. The low church section would have to appeal to the Free Churches of this country to defend it. In conclusion he predicted for the Warrington Council a very great suc CESS. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of sir:—STR—INGER, seconded by Councillor Evans, the services of the speakers and organisers of the meeting were heartily acknowledged. The CHAIRMAN in responding, urged a domiciliary visitation in Warrington. The meeting then terminated.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Whitham sticks up for "My Country, right or wrong."</p>
        <p>A LIVELY EXPERIENCE. A question in ethics tout must be somewhat em- barrassing to many good people just now shapes it- self in the words "Am I right in acting out the whole principle contained in the words—my coun- try, rigat or wrong?" There are several features about this unfortunate war that, I fear, can never be made clear to those who think that, to use the words of Justin McCarthy," the noble,peaceful,conscientious policy of Gladstone " was the ideal policy for the British Empire. I need not enumerate these, they have made their presence sufficiently manifest in the fact that their adoption has lad to the displacement of a number of press mtn, and have led able men like Mr. Courtney and Mr. Bryce into such seeming unpatriotic attitudes that the Canadian press charges that they have "screngthene the resistance of the Afrikanders." For myself lam bound to say that after reading an immense amount of stuff that has been issued on the subject I condemn the policy that allowed England to drift into what must be under any circumstances an inglorious war. Des- tiny, perhaps not so manifest, yet still destiny seems,so far to have ordered it that England should police the whole world. I say drop the role, and allow the nations now pushing to the front to take their share of " making war in the cause of civi- lization." Possibly the war may have done good in that ,it will leave England, unless, the tables are turned in South Africa after this date,with an army of veterans used to Long Toms, and barbed wire. It may also clear out a few of the old Tory marti- nets from the war office, and make the army and navy popular among the masses instead of being, as they are now, mere happy hunting grounds for the classes. I must say that there is no feature in the Baer's character, or in his dealings with the " rednecks," that receives my support, yet I main- tain that time, and that not so distant, would have accomplished all that the British Government pro- poses to accomplish, ant that without making South Africa into a sort of Adullam Cave into which all who hate England,and"they are a mighty horde, may meet and batch mischief. Yes, England's enemies are pretty numerous, to an extent that the stay-at home cannot even</p>
        <p>imagine. In a city like San Francisco, where one is constantly coming in cpntact with men from all lanes, it comes hard to the Englishman, especially to one who has done some little reading in his</p>
        <p>time, to hear his native land reviled, and ahused, and charged with all the political,and social crimes man ever dreamt of. The course of the war is the all-absorbing subject here. Againaldo, and his do- ings have taken a back seat. In the car,and in the crowd. and woerever two or three are gathered to- gether there is the war question in tbe midst of them. The arguments used are so reckless, and so wide of the truth that ,he blood begins to tingle, and tile En..lishman feels the sentiment. "my</p>
        <p>country, right or wrong,' take shape within his breust,and iorgets all else. Yes,that's the stand we, the few, take here, and it W4S with tl,ele feelings that oti Saturday evening February 2,5t1i I started off to the centre ot press information, three miles away,and,atter eleven p.m.The streets were crowded, peop!e wer3 gazing at the bulletin boards as if life and death depended upon what they read there. It was po,ted that Cronje had escaped ; this called forth a roar of cheers. Then, afrer a time, came the announcement that Kitchener with his sixty guns _</p>
        <p>was most innumanly slaughtering the whole of Cronje's forces; a burst of detestation followed this. Thinking it useless to wait for more information I left for home and bed, only to be up early on the Sunday.morning and start off to the city. When I reached the city I found crowds around each of the paper offices, and I prepared for some fun, and I had as good a share of Au I have had for some time. In a short time I reached the centre of the crowd whore I found a German lashing the English in great style. Spurred on by the cheers of the crowd he was in a most hysterical manner advocating a wholesale clearance of the English. He demanded that they be driven from the city without further delay. In the vilest and most reckless language he abused the Queen and all the aristocracy, and said, in choice broken English,all an Englishmen could do, or was fit for, was to worship the aristocrat. ' As soon as I could stop his arms from flying around like windmill sails, I gave him a etraight shot in the words, " here you German sausage, here's an Englishman, try your hand at throwing him out of the city." I thought the fellow would have had a fit. His arms began flying again, but 1 wouldn't have it, and I told him so.and that I didn't care one Continental dam if he and all his friends standing around were friends of the sham republic ; I wanted i.e have a talk with him about the Garman aristocrat, of whose doings in the German Army and on the American continent I knew something, and the crowd got it in full. I let him know, and the crowd, that there were but two countries on earth where such a crowd could discuss the merits of their rulers, and those countries were England and America. If he and I were to attempt such a thing in. Germany some sneaking German would re- port us and "Lase Majeste' would consign us to a prison. This brought the crowd down with cheers and shouts of" that's true boss." Atter this I had plain sailing while I gave them a sketch of the Boer business. I spoke until I was quite hoarse, and then some heckling began, and you can het these Germans and French and Dutch, and all nations remember, know how to badger a fellow. What had England ever done for the world? Well, I told them what she was doing for Egypt, and India. I showed the difference between the Free States, the Cape Colony, sud the Transvaal, I dsscribed the German Colonies in Africa, and the doings of France in Africa,and in Madagascar,end as a wind up,l gave them a short sketch of the doings of Englishmen, and only Englishmen, in establishing the American republic. This brought down a yell. When peace was restored I gave them names of those who had not only written most of the eastern state constitutions, but told them that the United States Constitu- tion itself was written by Englishmen, and most of it borrowed from our own Magni Charts, no other country having anything of a formulated character that could be used. This was more than they could stand. It was too true for a certain class in the crowd, and these hailing from Great Britain. Stones began to fly, two going through a large sheet of plate glass belonging to one of the paper offices and under whose windows I had done my talk. I now thought it time to quit, and creeping out of the crowd I was tendered a good hand shaking by a couple of Manchester men, and complimented on something I didn't know I possessed—pluck. Well, I had rare fun out of the matter and com- pletely snuffed out the German. San Francisco is the most cosmopolitan city on earth and this being so, it follows that the hatred of the whole of the European nations is well repre- sented here. The pity is that the hatred is of such a densely ignorant character that there is no doing anything with it. Tha hatred of the American born is just a consequence of the garbled history read by the children in the school books, and everlasting references made from the pulpit, the platform and the press to the war of the Revolution, and to that of 1812 If time and space permitted I could say a great deal on this subject, but it muat go. The Boer War up to the surrender of Cronje, and now to the relief of Ladysmith, had simply made a Eng- lishman's life here intolerable At every turn and wherever be might go he was sure of insult, so it can be imagined what 1 met with when I went among the crowd that Sunday morning. The papers took all the tit-bits out of Morley and Stead's speeches and writings, and these were heavy argu- ments for us to combat when used by the Irishmen and others around. One of the party I work with is a Frenchman and we nearly got to blows sevsral times when he hurled his English quotations at my head. Of course I know a bit of French history, and he got a bit ancient and modern We are now waiting for the next move and hoping other nations will keep their hands off. Of course we quite understand what Dr Leyds and his money are doing or trying to do. Every thing possible is being done just now to embroil England with other nations. The Democratic party here hoped to make capital out of the Macrum incident, but the man was so evidently a pro-Boer that he stamped himself from the outset a liar. An organization existing in this city having for</p>
        <p>A Genuine Cure.</p>
        <p>its purpose the aid of the Boers and the promotion of their cause, has just made a statement, It con- ducts an exLeisive theatre of agitation, and works hard to create sympathy for the Boers. It has a large active membership, at the bead of which is Pro. David Starr Jordan, the head of the Stamford University. and a prominent man in everything thbt is wrong-headed. In fact all the names are IriFh, but the poet of the moverneat„ Joaquin Miller, I cannot locate. lam inclined to think he is Irish, because he is always seeking trouble. This organization has collected £4OO. The statement goes 071 to say :—" Of thus sum 1,000 dols. has been sent to Dr. W. J. Leyds, in Brus,.els, Belgium, the European representative of the Boer Government. The balance of the funds are now being devoted to raising and equipping an ambalance hospital corps which, it is thought, will comprise 100 men, and will leave an Francisco for South Africa in about six weeks. These men will go unarmed and under the Red Cross. Mem- bers of the committee say significantly that they cannot control the actions of men whom they send after they reach Boerdom. There may be those among them who will leave the corps aud take up arms, for, though assistance in caring for the sick is greatly needed, yet the prime necessity is fight- ing men. 11, however, the committee proceeded to organise bands of men and send them with the de- clared intention of their" becoming soldiers in the war, it would be guilty of filibustering, and its acts would be stopped as being in violation of the neu- trality laws." One of Oona Paul's family is a great draw at the meetings. Miss Sannie Kruger is a good singer, and when she cries out in her pathetic broken English, " shall the English be allowed to exterminate my pecple ?" why she just brings the house down, but not the shekels. A meeting just held in the largest hall in the city crowded out into an overflow meeting computed at 10,000. The collection, £9O, didn't cut much ice. While writing the above my mind has been under considerable tension, and just as I concluded, relief has come. In December last my wife had a serious hemorrhage in the throat. The stopping of this was a question of time and patience. This was for- tunately accomplished ; but not before she was re- duced almost to death's door. The doctors concluded there was a cause for this, and an examination compelled them to tell me that sue must either allow an operation to be performed, or meet death at an early date. She went to the hospital, and the doctor has just called to inform me that the opera- tion has been performed successfully, and that she is going on well.</p>
        <p>1318 iluerrero-street, S.F.</p>
        <p>March 4, 1900.</p>
        <p>H. WHITHADI</p>
