      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>I'INER OPrORTUNITT</head>
        <p>I'INER OPrORTUNITT</p>
        <p>than that which offers now fDr showing that our cor- porate life is a fact and not a name; a force to bs reckoned with, a factor that cannot be neglected in our national life. Purity in morals, ethical morality in all our public concerns, right-thinking, right dealin and right living, ought to be made much more possible and actual by the closer relationship which we have assumed towards each other. bold, then, that put of our work is to keep a keen watch over our nation's life,to promote such legislation as will advance the cause of righteous- ness, and make it easy to live a good life, and hateful and difficult to lead a bad one. In the work of legislation we have come to see that much has yet-to be done in the interests of child-life. judge we are all &amp;lighted at the firm tone displayed, and the splendid enthusiasm manifested, by the House of Commons in carry- ing through the second-reading of Mr. Souttar's Bill for preventing the sale of intoxicating liquors to children under sixteen years of age. Tnis is not only a step in the right direction, but one ' which ought to have been taken lung ago. It is recom- mended both by the ,majority and minority reports of her Majesty's Licensing Commission, and we must work until this ,particular rocommeudation has bscome the law of the land. In some directions the"bansful" icliu- ence upon child-life, ,by the language heard in public houses, by what is seen of drunkenness, and by the inducements offered to children themselves, and 15 the still further temptations in carrying bottle or jug home to tare their contents, are pooli-posthed. I have seen in Warringtonquite young children drink some of the beer they have bought for their parents' consumption at home. I have, both in London and other places, seen boys and girls take sips of the gin and rum which they were sent to get for their parents- In some cases I have assumed the right to tell the children the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DOUBLE WIION 41</head>
        <p>DOUBLE WIION 41</p>
        <p>they were committing in taking what was not their own and in ,creating within themselves a taste for alcoholic drink. We have had to preserve childlife from a too eerly employment in mines and factories by legislation, thanks in the first instance to Mrs Browning's "Bitter Cry of the Children." We have had to protect the inlet.. tul life of the children against the employer on the one hand, and the parents on the other,by discouraging the half-time system, and raising the standard of age at which a child may leave school. We must new go still further and protect the health,the body, and the morals of the children by legally preventing their going to a public-house under 16 years of age. The Licensing Commission recommends that both parents and publicans shall be fined, those for sending and those for servine the ceildien. Again, no class of persons needs a Sunday's rest more than those belonging to the trade. Both in their interests, in the interests also of the general public, and having regard to the character of the day, the Licensing Commission recommends the reduc;ion of the hours during which the public-house shall he epeneto two ass maximum; but public opinion having regard to the value and results of Sunday dos- ing in Scotland, Ireland and Wales,will insist upon these trading institutious being closed altogether on the Lord'a day. to begin with, I havo always objected to one class of tradesmen being favoured by law above the rest, and that not in the interests of the public, but in their own interests' venture to think that a large num- ber of publicans look with longing eye upon the time when they, too, can spend a Sunday unmolested, and a well-known speaker has recent ly said that he does not think half-a-dozen putlicansiin Glasgow can be found to favour the re-opening of public-houses in that city onthe Lord's day. "ROB THE POOR PUBLICAN OF HIS SUNDAY" is a far better cry teen "Rob the poor man of his beer." , Apart, however, frem trading concerns, we have I another season for preserving ietact our Lord's day. Much less driaking wocid take place in connection with a certain class of funerals, and ;the rowdyism of drunken excursionists would at any rate be largely diminished. It seems to me that the so-called interference with the liberty of the private subject will ensure the retention of the quiet dignity which inseparably belongs to the Engish Sunday. Thenl in the name of our common Lord, let us interfere and embody such interference in a statutory form. It is a matter of great moment to me that the Minority Commission has not seen its way clear to recommend as of immediate practical ability " Local Veto." I am not one of those who do not willingly accept the lowest measure of temperance reform, failing any- thing better and higher, but, sir, lam a prohibitionist ; and though the report holds out the hope of the ulti- mate adoption of tee veto, yet it seems to me that, as a mere matter of extending local self-government, the people should have the legal right to say whether or not they shall have the drink traffic carried on in their locality. It is not as if this right were some concession no one had ever enj iyed before. Many wealthy land- owners object to public-houses in their estates. What ens man enjoys because ho's his father's son should certainly be enjoyed by those te whom the drink traffics is the cause of so much inisery and ruin. This is a graver question than even we can estimate. We may state it in a new form and under new terms, thus: Which is to triumph, the Christian forces of this country, organised in the interests of sobriety and morality, or the forces that emanate from the brewery and the public house 7 Don't however, mis- understand inn. lam tar from believing that the veto I will bring in the mitlenninm, but it will lessen the</p>
        <p>BABY</p>
        <p>LIKES</p>
