      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>DEATH OF MR-. C. H. LEWIN,</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR-. C. H. LEWIN,</p>
        <p>OF WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Profound regret was caused in Widnes on Tuesday by the sad news of the death of Mr. Charles Henry Lewin, deputy magistrates' clerk, which took place at his resi- dence, Birchaeld House, Farnworth, Widnes, at 3 a.m. The dec ‘ased gendeman, who was 53 years of age, has been suffer;nz for sum, time from liver complaint and impaired digestion, and has been attended by Dr. Dow, of Si. Ho'ens. Despite this ailment he has attended the Police Court, and was present at th., Petty Sessions no Thursday last. He appeared to be all light on Monday night, but in the early hours of Tuesday miming he became seriously ill, and expired as stated above, The deceaGed was extremely well known, and was held in the high.Ft respect. He always displayed a kindly and genial disposition. For 25 years he had held the position of deputy to Mr. Spenceley, the magistrates' clerk. As a test of his ability it was a remarkable thing the few app:al4 that had been lodged against his advice to the magistrates whilst he has been sitting at the court. The greatestsympathy is expressed for Mrs. Lewin in her severe brreavement. Mr. Lewin is well-known in War- rington, whore he has relatives, and his death will be much regretted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Reference at the Court,</head>
        <p>Reference at the Court,</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court on Tuesday morning th 3 magistrate (Alderman W. W. Gossagu) desired the Press to express his sorrow at Mr. Lewin's death. They re- gretted very much the loss of an extremely valuable servant, who had always done his nest to advise the magistrates on the bench. He had, always given the greatest satisfaction in the work he had accomplished. Tney expressed their deep regret and sorrow to Mrs. Lewin in her beretvement.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Keigbley endorsed Alderman Gossage'a remarks. Personally he felt the loss greatly. Mr. Lewin had been very willing to adviie him when he re- quired advice. Ha desired to express his sincere regret to Mrs. Lewin.</p>
