      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>IP=mm!A;;M*Olt_g</head>
        <p>IP=mm!A;;M*Olt_g</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By "Oval i st."]</p>
        <p>The" giddy Goolians " as another writer called them, had their feelings rudely shockfd and their giddiness toned down at Wilderspot-A oil Saturday, when they ran up a,sinst Warrington in the second round of the Nortnern Union cup-ties. Warrington do nt Lftea get so extravagant in the scoring line as they did on Satur- day, and it is as well for tne peace of mind of their opponents that they don't. I fancy it will take Goole some time to get over the unpleaiant experience of hav- ing 44 points complied against them. Yes 44 points? That was actually the score, and no wonder that a pan trained to more modeA fi,gures hesitates at writing them. There is no doubt that Warrington fully earned , the right to eater the third round, arid pi rhaps their I performance may intimidate Rochdale 'Hornets a bit.</p>
        <p>The funniest part of the whole affair was that the Gooliaus, being then at their gidaiest, came down from the north expecting to win. They had it in their minds that it would te a good tlailg financially it they could pit themselves against Bud Kingston Rovers for the third round. And no doubt it would have b. cn a good thing, only both teams received the order of the kieck- out on Lancashire sJil. Well, the men from the banks of the Rumb^r were a fine, healthy, plucky lot, and no doubt their dream of success was strengthened when they saw the modest proportions of the Warrington lads. They were only auxioas on one point and that was the identity of Fish—whose fame had reached them—and I hope and trust they will know him when next they see him. Seeing that he scored no less than 29 points him- self, it is not improbable that they will. Probably one or two backs had strict orders to keep an eye on the slippery youth. This was not difficult, but keeping a hand on him was another matter.</p>
        <p>A moderate crowd of about 3,000 turned out to ivitness the cont st, and for a quarter of an hour they probably thought the visitors were going to make a decent show. It took about this long for Warrington to get into their stride, but when they did there was no doubt whatever as to the ultimate winners. Eden missed a certain try, but atoned for it soon after by scoring near the corner. Fish landing a grand goal. Then the litter raced clean away and grounded behind the posts in his usual style, another goal following from the same foot. Two goals. two tries, was all Warrington got in the first half, but on turning round they began to rub in the agony merci- lessly. The backs and forwards raced in one after the other after splendid combination, Fish converting five successive tries. As he scored four himself in the second half, one being a particularly brilliant effort from half- way, he did his share. When the overwhelmed scorers had got their breath, and things were totalled up. War- rington's record for the afternoon was 7 goals, 10 tries. Goole's was nil—short and sweet.</p>
        <p>If I ant not mistaken Fish cagily holds the record for individual scoring in Northern Union matches, and his performance of kicking 7 goals, and scoring 5 tries, will take a lot of beating anywhere. Se w sin brilliant form,andalong with Isherwood formed a wing which was irresistible every time it got going. Toe more one feeds the oth, r, and the mors scoring there will be, and I am glad to see what an excellent centre liherwood is mak- ing. Nobody can charge him with spoiling openings, or I selfishness, now. A. year ago be was often to blame in this respect, and the wig man suffe.red in consequence.</p>
        <p>Though Warrington were without Burgess and Jones, they were immeasurably superior to their opponents, none of whom app:Artin to know much about the game as it should be played. The home forwards again die- tinguisbed themselves, and their speed in the open was marvellous at times. Oh ! that tbey had been in such form throughout the season. Hallam was again excel- lent at back, but Hockenhall seemed uncertain as a centre. Moores was tie better man of the two on the day's play. Bata took things emily, but was clearly head and shoulders above the rest in resourca and skill. Prespecte are bright at present.</p>
        <p>Contrary to expectations Warrington will not have to make the long journey to Hull to-day, as Rochdale hornets saved them the trouble by trouncing the Rovers on Saturday. I had rather fancied Kingston Rovers against the Hornets, but the latter played a surprising game and won by a good margin. Consequently War- ringt-m will have to go Rochdale to-day, and endeavour to get the best of the Hornets by hook or crook.</p>
        <p>Most people will agree that Warrington ought to win again, though raci'. tam won the home encounter in the League. Warrington are, howev.r, a better all-round team than Rochdale Horne:s, and have come back victorious from the Valley f the Rac twice in the last three seasons. They ought to have got two points there on September 23rd. but chanees were &amp;Bowel to go a-begging, and Billy Riga settled the matter by dropping a goal just on time. In the return encounter on February 24 they were easily the best ream, ai,d on by ten points to nil. It was in this Kroici that 3urgesi hurt his knee.</p>
        <p>Satisfaction will be felt that the latter will ancomrany Bate again to day, his fortnight's rest havtog dons him good. Jones also gets apl ACP again, perforrnincr on the wirg next to Hoekenisall. I hope b )th will be in god form. Jones has a lost reputation to retrieve, for ho simply threw openings away at 111(121a1e earlier in the season. There will be no other chinge from last week, and the team should be streng enough ro earn the y-rivi- lege of meeting either S tiford or FlLlicl3rsft -II in the fourth round.</p>
        <p>YLIF</p>
        <p>. Patentee vsr;,rv.t.s Free. _ought= Street ,rpool. elephoue 70E2.</p>
