        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held in the Board Room at the Workhouse on Wedoesday morning. Mr A J Jolley presided and there were also pr;sent:—Dr Patter- son (vice-chairman), Mrs Clay and Mrs Edelstcn, Rev W fi Drummond, Rev J H Riddette, hiesArs A Jolley, GI Pilling, H Hibbert, J U Ashcroft, William Hickman, S Davis', Henry Thornton, T B Malkin. 4 Brooks, T Halton, William Houebton, R Foishaw, J Clarke, with J C Sutton (clerk), C H Ward (workhouse master), and ; A Wright (superintendent Padgate Schools.)</p>
        <p>THE CHILDREN AT THE EXHIBITION. Mr. WEICIRT reported that on March 15, the children attended the exhibition in Farr Hall and were invited to see the performance of the American Midgets and the cinematograph. Tea was generou4:y provided by the committee; Mr. Birch nave the chtldren aa orange and flower each and Mr. Lawless liberally supplied the children with sweets. HOUSE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED ALTERATIONS AND EXTENSION: This c3mmittee at a meeting on March 16, recom. mended that the plans submitted by Messrs. Owen for alterations and extension at the Workhouse and includ- ing entrances, stores, laundry, and additional bath and bedroom (as shown on a plan marked scheme III) be approved and steps taken to carry out the same. The tenders referred to the committee were examined and the following accepted ; T G Lee for drapery; cord end twill; 11 Jackson for brushes and Richardson and Son for groceries. It was recommended that the tender of Messrs. S and F Acton for alterations at the sum of 262 be accepted. The CHAIRMAN rave notice that he would bring the subject of the alterations and extensim of tfie Work- house before the meeting of the Board a fortnight hence. In answer to a question by the Rev. W. H. Drummond the CLERK stated that the architect would attend at the neat meeting and explain the plans to these who were unacquainted with them. The minutes of the cornmilee weie then passed. MASTER'S REPOBT.</p>
        <p>The MASTER reported in his journal that he bad re - waived with thanks parcel of papers Ito., from Rev. J Davies, St. Ann's Church; grapes for sick from MrS. Fox and Mrs. Rymer Young and oranges from Mrs. II Roberts, members of Ladies' Conarnitre. tars. Langley had left the employment of the (U din and Mrs. !Mr- chall had commence d her duties. VACCINATION OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The CLERK repilrced that the Loc-a-1471;e"riament Board had written approvicg of the appointment of Dr. Valen- tine S 9 vaccinating illiCr. Lu concoction with the appointment ot Dr. Dowlin RR public vaccinator for the flaydock district, the Loci, it lament Board asked for a certificate of his expo...once in vaccination. The Clerk said he had received a certificatr from Dr. Domling and wau iNaiting for 006 for his deputy before submit- ting them to tha Look! Government Bnird. THE PROPOSED NEW BUILDINGS AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The CLERK s:.aid he had also•rece:ved a letter from the Local Government Board anknowledging the receipt of the Boaro's letter of the 16th inst., with reference to the proposal i.f the Guard;ans to purchase land and con- struct a relieving office and a relieving officer's residence at Earlestown. Under thecircurnstances mentioned and in deference to the wish of the Guardians, the Board would he prepared to consider a proposal to purchase a site and erect premises SR propored. The reply was considered satisfactory. I This was all the ho,iness.</p>
