      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MARCH 30TH, 1900.</head>
        <p>MARCH 30TH, 1900.</p>
        <p>THE selection of three The Boer Delegates. Boer delegates to come to Europe and then to visit the United States has far more importance in the eyes of the Boers than it has anywhere else. Their mission is to court intervention, and that has been already declined all round. .They will visit Berlin, Brussels, and the United States, making such representations as they think important. Some confidence is felt at Pretoria in the success of the mission or it would not have been sent. Upon what grounds this confidence is based we are quite unable to make out. The Boers have nothing to offer in return for anything they may claim. They are not obtaining military advantages which would place them npon equal terms, and they have no financial resources apart from their interest in the gold mines of the Rand and the metal they are extract- ing from such mines as are being worked and appropriated to their own use, robbing the British, French, German. and American shareholders. It is impossible to conceive a more desperate case. A war loan cannot be raised with the securities to be offered. Territorial or other advantages cannot be promised for an alliance, and as this country has declined to admit of intervention any form of it must assume that of a hostile character. But the Boers are subtle, and they may be seeking support for some 'desperate move, such as the destruction of the mines or the burning of their principal towns. The situation the delegates have to face is sufficiently desperate. At the time the Boers began the war they fervently believed that long before the critical moment arrived there would be an alliame against England in Europe, and armed intervention would become a reality. At Bloemfontein Mr. STEYN was always talking about this alliance, and a phrase he used has been remembered. "We hold England in the hollow of our hand," he said. Dr. LEYDS must have sueceeded in . creating this impression, and no wonder that he is now so unpopular that the Transvaalers accuse him of betraying them. He was their agent, and he had a mission to accom- plish. President KRUGER and Mr. Renz,however, are shrewd, capable men, and the wonder is how they can have been deceived. The information transmitted must have been constantly tested,ancl the Executive of Pretoria can only blame them- selves if they have placed constructions upon Dr. LEYDS'S reports which they were unable to bear. It is clear, however, that Mr. STEYN was de- ceivod,and that he deceived others by his confident assurances. The delegates now on their way to Europe will soon find, we expect, that there is not the slightest chance of any intervention by a single European Power, and there is little pros- pect of any batter result in the United States, even though Mr. MONTAGU WHITE is conducting a campaign of reviling against this country. The Boer cause is not one that admits of intervention, for several reasons. They are not independent states, in the European sense; they invaded British possessions; and Great Britain has dis- tinctly stated that it will not recognise their inde- dence or permit of the intervention of another Power. Only combined intervention would be effective, and there is no prospect of that, and events would not justify it. No, :the Boers had better seek peace at once, and then they might get better terms.</p>
        <p>THE happy relations between Englandda and England and Canada are Cana. largely due to the Liberalism of Sit WILFRID LAURIER and his party. The long reign of the Conservative and the Protectionist party had not been marked by anything better than a good deal of after-dinner loyalty, which yielded no practical fruit. With the Liberal re- action under Sir WILFRID LAURIER everything has been changed. We have subjected our commercial relations with Germany and ißelgium to revision in order to he free to make terms with Canada, and the Canadians have met us in generous spirit. At first they reduced the tariff on British goods entering their port by S por cent., and then by 25 per cent. A further reduction of 331 per cent. has now been arranged, and it should materially im- prove our trade, as imports from this country will now be only paying 11 per cent., whilst the like imports from other foreign countries will be pay- ing 35 per cent. The amount of trade between this country and Canada has risen under the new tariff about 2 per cent. to the general trade of the Dominion, and though this may not seem a large amount. because Canada has been so pros- perous and demanded so much from abroad, it bulks larger if we remember that the total trade between Canada and this country had risen from over 22 millions sterling in 1896 to over 33 millions in 1898. The organs of Protection in Canada do t regard the change as effective, and they con- tend that it is more convenient for Canadians te trade with America than with Great Britain,which offers no preferential treatment in return. It is obvious that we cannot offer to Canada anything better than the Free-trade wnich is our general policy towards all other countries; but, on the other hand, the good relations between us and the Dominion are a spur to our merchants and con-</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>sumer&amp; to purchase an iticerea.sing quantity of Canadian products. Events in South Africa will increase this disposition, and Englishmen will naturally desire to give the preference to Canadian products wherever they co], and to take care that the goodwill shall not all be on one side.. The Volunteers, who have been doing such ad- mirable service for us in South Africa, have en- larged our views as to the meaning of Empire, and in no sense in the direction which some conscien- tious politicians would resent. Mr. CAMBER' LAIN has wisely offered forty-two commissions in the Imperial Army to Canadians, and probably the preference will be given to men who have: been serving in South Africa. The repatriation cf the old Canadian Regiment will be another way in which we can mark our appreciation of the generous kinship of the Canadians in a time of some stress and difficulty. The Canadians are now so enthusiastically loyal, they have displayed such a broad-minded conception of colonial duty and aspiration, that we shalt always regard them as brothers in arms as: well as members of the great English family. We wish to include the French-Canadians in this: estimate, and to make no distinction between one part of the Dominion and another. The more• rapid communication between this country and Canada will always unite us more closely than with our Australian kinsmen, and though we must not excite any jealousy on the part of the latter we cannot forget that with Canada we have peculiar ties, and shall always hope to have more direct political and C".9MME rcial intercourse. The Australians have been grandly loyal, and they never had but one ideal—that of unicn with Great Britain. The Canadians, however, have had to resist temptation, and it has cost them some effort to remain loyal and British.</p>
        <p>The military spirit which has been so pronounced in Warrington during the present war is reflected to some extent in the large number of recruits who have recently enrolled themselves in the local Volunteer corps. No fewer than 500 have offered themselves and of these about 250 have been ac- cepted. An effort is being made to bring up the strength of the battalion to 1,000 s,!;-ong. Hitherto the battalion has been runer s:rnall in numbers but unequalled in erality any volunteer bat- talion in the country. in incrsing the strength the officers are desirous of keeping up the high re- putation which the battalion has gained and have been somewhat particular in their choice of re- cruits. Respectable men in steady employment, with good characters only, are taken and then only when they have satisfactorily passed the medical examination.</p>
        <p>We do not wish to be regarded as recruiting officers for the Volunteer corps, but we should like to see a still further enrolment of eligible men, and; thus bring up the battalion to the desired num- ber. There are still a number of vacancies and any young man who is desirous of doing his duty to his country might do worse than become a trained volunteer. To become a trained volunteer a recruit has to attend 60 drills (30 yearly) and perform his musketry courses during the first two years of his service; after which 12 drills and musketry only per annum are necessary. It will be seen that the demands made upon a voluneer are not inordinately great. There are numbers of young men in Warrington an district who seem at a loss to know how to spend their spare time with advantage. By becoming asso- ciated with the Volunteer Corps they would not only have the satisfaction of knowing that they were serving their country usefnlly but they would be benefitted by the discipline and improved in health and strength by the drill and exercise obtained.</p>
        <p>Scientific gentlemen are waxing mildly enthusi- astic in regard to the experiments that are to be carried out by astronomical observers during the coming eclipse of the sun, but the rest of the unfortunate dwellers in this kingdom probably begin to look upon the affair—if, indeed, they have heard of it at all—as a very sorry joke. For them the sun has been eclipsed most of the win- ter. This month it was hoped that an agreeable change would be experienced; but after a few hours of balmy, windless weather cold shadowy skies set in again, and they have had snow in London. Yet according to the calendar we have entered upon spring—that delightful season which Browning apostrophised from the sunny bays of Italy—and we are all labouring under a deep sense of grievance because the weather does not work harmoniously with the calendar. Browning prob- ably went by the calendar when he wrote: "Oh, to be in England, now that spring is there !" and had his wish been gratified he would have most likely have returned to the sunny land by the next packet, leaving a trail of Browingesque invective behind him. The weather does not trouble about invective, and perhaps it is just as well that it takes its own course, impervious alike to argu• ment, entreaty, disappointment or the prophecies of its own prophets. The farmer praying for rain, the holiday maker hoping for a cloudless sky, the mariner wishing for a strong wind, would soon quarrel if the prayers of the one neutralised the petitions of the other two; whereas now the peace is kept, since they are all sure that nothing they can do will alter the weather's course.</p>
        <p>General Joubert is dead, and while the Boers mourn the loss of their most distinguished general and truest patriot, we, his foes, keenly regret the passing of so brave and honorable a man. At first thoughts it seems as if it would hardly be un- natural were the people of this country to rejoice at such a blow to the enemy as Joubert's death must be, but, Englishmen can admire honesty and. courage wherever these qualities are displayed,. and it is to the credit of the nation that so much sorrow is expressed at the death of the. famous Transvaal Chieftain. He was a noble old man, courteous and considerate in warfare, and there is no fulsome praise in the Pretoria tele- gram which alludes to him as " a true patriot, gallantcgeneral, and an upright gentleman." it is a singular fact that on the day he died, Sir George White speaking at Capetown referred to his .re- cent antagonist in the warmest terms of admiration. There is only one circumstance connected with General Joubert's death that we feet any pleasure in recalling, and that is that he paqsed from earth while his country still retained the independence which he, all his life, had fought and laboured to maintain. He was spared the pain of seeing the land he loved subservient to the conquerer,. and we are thankful that such was the case.</p>
        <p>"What shall we do after the war?" is a question asked daily by thousands of people who might, perhaps, be more profitably employed in seeking an answer to the question, "What sh all be done after the famine ?" Unfortunately, the famine code upon which the British Administration in India prided itself has totally failed to meet exist- ing emergencies. At the end of the last great famine we were told that such an experience could never occur again, so perfect were the safeguards provided by the authorities. It brooks no resist- ance ; not, however, because resistance is impos• sible, but because it is not organised in time, and sufficient money is not forthcoming to pay for it. To employ starving agriculturists upon relief works is no doubt a satisfactory stop-gap, but it should not become a permanent panacea. The trouble is that no attempt appears to be made to place the ryots in a substantial position after the need for relief has passed. The native farmer Is thrown back into the arms of the native monere lender, who never gives him the opportunity 0.1 making provision for hard times. Tlaere, thou, 1! one way in which the Indian Government coal" help the native of India to face a bad dry seasal independently. A check should be put upon the, operations of the extortionate. Agricultural banks, as suggested by Mr. Nicholson iafter the 1897 disaster, should he started ; not onelbseks</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
