        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>• but ]X The c, reliq be wi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>Mi slaty, tion conic quit( cloub Th</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>, but many,in the heart of tho agricultural districts. ale cut and dried scheme to conquer famine by lehEl works ha, failed to justify itself. It would "e Ise to uiva the alternative plan a fair trial.</p>
        <p>It is a matter for regret that there was not a -I..._rger attendance at the annual meeting of the Warrington and district Free Church Council. The ' !übjects introduced were of first-rate importance to Free Churchmen and they were treated in a 331,. antler which could not fail to impress and en- the few who thought it worth their while to attend. The subject of sacerdolatism which as been so much to the fore in the religious world gas very properly under consideration. The Rev. IV. L. Christien, of Runcorn, effectively demolished some of the specious claims '39 t forth en behalf of apostolic succes- .Bl",. The Free Church Council steadfastly :scuews politics and consequently no refer- "nce was made to the war. There was, however, a Passing reference by the Chairman to the dis- turbances which have prevented ardent peace vocates from publicly discussing the advise- of stopping the war, and a strong claim was ka • defor unfettered and unhindered free speech. cile Rev. J. F. Matthews travelled along quite a I.erent line and lucidly detailed the progress -71211 prospects of the Temperance cause. Mr. .f).atthews is a prohibitionist but this does not - 11u1 him to the necessity of accepting half a loaf Preference to none. In this spirit he welcomed the recent legislation, which, if faithfully carried out, seems destined to protect child life from some i rthe insidious temptations to which t has long • ee.n subjected owing to parental degradation and itldifference to the danger of a bad example.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNcoR:4 RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNcoR:4 RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
