        <p>THE HIGHWAY ESTIMATES. The Surveyor submitted the following estitnate of the eXpentliture Li,hways for the year 1900-1901 : Alvan,y, £lO7 ' • As•,o7 t 1, £9l ; Aston Grange, £l6 • Bar- tingtoo, £37 ; C!ifton, £ll6 ; Dutton, £l4O ; Frodsioam, £305,; Fcc Ihm Lvdhip, £285; Eialron, £B7; He!shy, £162; £227 ' • Kingswoui, £9O ; Manley, £108; Neer'on-hy-Freeleham, £6B; Norley, £195 ; Norton, £95 ; Stockh-im, £32: Sut-en, £172; Weston, £289. The total wag £2,622 of which £2,171 was for ordinary re- Pairs, and £451 for imprevements. The total of the "timete for 1899-1900 was £2,160. Linaker 1 it uisllal to refer these estimates to !'ne Highway Clmmittee before they are adopted P If it is so I beg to move that. .LThe hairnrin replied that was the usual course, and baeY returned to the Council afterwards. Mt. S. Woodward seconded. Linakez also suggested that in addition to the tstirnates they should be supplied with a detailed list of Iti)w the estimates were arrived at by tha surveyors, and 1.2° with a statement of the actual amount spent upon lue highways, eo that the Council could see how the mstimatos actually compared with the amount really ex- PeVed. alc.? Clerk intimated that such statements would be rovided, and the estimates were unanimously referred 5 the Highways Committee.; WEI&amp;T MUST WE EXPECT. rtiMr..l Spruce gave notice of motion for the amend- of standing order 19 in such a way as he would aLe at the next meeting. The following is the standing order referred to :—"l9. toTmotion having for its object the increase of the salary an official, or the expenditure of money other than :ll:relief or in the payment of accounts properly incurred be moved at any meeting unless notice of the ti"v4.ntion to move the same shall have been given at the tix„.t Preceding meeting, and shall have been entered on 81.°minutes of the meeting. The subject matter thereof 2all be specified by notice posted by the Clerk to each glernber b al receipt thereof by any member, 