        <p>ROAD METAL WASTED. Was decided tio issue the U. 5111,1 advcrtivaients for -"Cidam for tin rep Air of the highway.</p>
        <p>TRE COUNCIL AND THE TRANSPORTER BRIDGE. ti,llr Linaker eaquired what was toe present attitude of ,sue District Councii respecting the Trans') irtsr Briige thr+ Mersey •e_The Clerk replied that the Ceur.cil had practically pre- rrved their future action. Linaker aided that he simplo wanted to call —4;ention to the fact that the preamble of the Bill had t'kaased the House of Commons. Ele drew atr.ention to "" disadvantages e.griculturati,ts would be at, ;f they all° ~ wed the pr.4sent high rates for agricultural produce Zo stand. Ile instanced a proposed cba•-gie of 64 a ton for ZaY and te-re yr, whi.4ila meant in a load r f two tiru, 121, about four or fiv, suiinic, for tie emns'Y c irt to re- Such prices rendered toe bridge absolutely value- a—s, to a2riallturalists. In lac r they were prohibitive. As tes'ert towards remedy Mr Li htltrir suggested that the ttter should 12e. taken in hani or the committee th'eeadY ap-ointed to con the q i-stion, and that aa-?, telrirt tothe C uucl ..t the next meeting, especially pas" aPpeared the Council had reserved its rights to op the Bill in the House of Lords. rere,,r; SY-Iv?s as one ot the agticulturalists most in LitiaVt as 'no resided near the bridge, supported Mr. Litl4-1,."Eq.• There was one good thing, however, that Mr. tniirr had forgotten to mention; and that was that Pro could be conveyed over for 2i a can. He ap- e() ved of the course suggested by Mr. Linaker that the roMittee should glean all the information possible and int°:tt to the Council. He further suggested, that an eo,:1"w between the promoters of the bridge and the ‘uthittee be arranged if possible. It would be an ad- Sub vantage. ' s()l4,lAntiv Mr. Linaker'a suggestions, together /*ith th " and rise of Stokes, were embodied in a resolution, agreed to. adltr• Stokes was aifo, on the motion of Mr. Linaker, ued to the already existing committee. Mr. FINANCES OF TNE COUNCIL. ",.IrDas Wright proposed the adoption of the fiti- nsAtement. The payments 