      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY APPOINTMENT Mr. H. A. Cook, formerly of NV arrington,Runcorn Wait sn, and Broad eireen stations, has been appointed stetienmaster to the Lendon and North- Western Railway Company at Belton. PRESENTATION AT WIDNES. During the course of the second portion of the concert at the Beihs Assembly Rooms on Saturday evening, Mr. Jackeon, the hon. secretary of the Unitise Alkali popularconcerte,was made the recipi- ent of a beautiful pipe and case, in recognition of the valuable services he had rendered during the past sessioce—The Chairman( Mr. e-Cerey),in making the presentation, remarked that Air. Jackson had been the best secretary they had ever had, and he trusted that he would continue in the office for many years.--Mr. Jackson suitably responded. FREE LIERA.RY—LECTURE AT WIDNES In connection with the Widnes Library Commit- tee a lecture on "Oliver Weneell Helmes," the Americen author, was given in the lecture theatre at the Technicae Schools on Friday evening, by Dr. Donnolley. Major Lewis presided, and there was a fairly good attendance. The lecturer dealt with the subject in an instructive manner, quoting from the author's works, principally "The Breakfast- table."—At the conclusion, on the motion of Mr. Showan, seconded by Mr.C. Lewis, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Dr. Donnolley for his ad- mirable lecture. A Fox SEEN ON RUNCORN HILL. On Sunday afternoon, as a number of youths were rambling across Runcorn Hill, in the vi•inity of the waterworks, they disturbed a fine specimen of an English fox. Reynard must have been lying very low with visions of a feed of poultry from the numerous pens upon the hillside,for the youths got quite close upon him before he was inclined to desert his cover, and, when he did, he snapped savagely at the nearest youth before he took to his heels. etrange to say that youth was as much frightened as the fox and took off as fast as his legs could carry him in the opposite direction. THE ELECTION ARRANGEMENTS. Mr F A. Lake has issued his notices for an election in Central Ward, of the Runcorn Urban district, from which we see there will be two pol- ling boothe, but only one polling station situated at the National Schools The presiding officers will be Mr E Marshall and Mr James Wilding, with Messrs A Austin, and J Naull as poll clerks.—Mr J II Sal- keld has appointed Mr T H Annett, as his agent, and Mr T Wood has appointed Mr W Millington (secretary pro tern to the Runcorn Ratepayers' Asso- ciation), as his representative.—The counting will be taken in hand immediately, the poll closes at 8 o'clock, and the return made by Mr Lake (the re- turning officer) about 9 to half-past. SERVICE OP SONG AT TH-E WELSH CHAPEL. A service of song entitled" The Musical Miller," was given on Wednesday evening ,at the Welsh Chapel. Rutland-street, Runcorn, by the members of the Milton-street choir, Widnes, under the conduc- torship of Mr John Davies (Llew.) There was a fair attendance, and the chair was occupied by Mr D H Jones (High-street.) The accompanist was Miss Annie Davies„ whilst the Rev Sidney Morris, of St. John's Ac ted as reader, a duty he per- formed with distinctness and feeling. The whole entertainment proved a regular musical treat, and reflected great credit upon Mr Davies and his choir. Votes of thanks to the Chairman, tbe performers, and the reader were proposed and heartily accorded upon the motion of Mr Hughes (pastor of the Welsh Choir), seconded by Mr Robert Hughes, Greenwae -road. Before dispersing the National Anthem was rendered by the choir,and the audience standing, joined heartily in the chorus. A most enjoyable concert was brought to a close by the Rev. S. Morris pronouncing the benediction A FEMALE THIEF IMPRISONED. Before Messrs F J 'Norman and Samuel Taylor, at the Runcorn Police Court, on Wednesday morning, Sarah Helsby, married woman, pleaded guilty to stealing a sheet valued at 2s the property of Hannah Sidley ; and a shirt valued at 2s the property of John Shingler, on the 27th inst —Hannah Sidley, widow, 23, Brook-street, identified the sheet pro- duced as her property which had been stolen from the clothes line in the backyard—Frank Watson, assistant to Mr Dodd pawnbroker swore the prisoner had pledged the sheet produced with him for 101 She stated at the time it was her own,—Constable Carrington gave evidence of the recovery of the stolen property, and of prisoner's apprehension.— Maria Shingler, wife of John Shingler, 27, Brook- street depesed to missing her husband's shirt from the back-yard where it was hanging after being washed.—Eliza Shone, wife of Thomas Shone, Wel- iington-street swore to buying the shirt from Iprisoner for 31. Prisoner represented that her husband had left her and that she wanted to buy food.—P.C. Bowyer spoke as to the recovery of the property.—Prisoner who had been before the Bench on two previous occasions was committed to gaol for 14 days with hard labour. ALLEGED WOUNDING- WITH A KNIFE AT RUNCORN. At an occasional court, at Runcorn, presided over by Messrs F. J. Norman,and F. Wiswalaon Tuesday John O'Niel, recently discharged from the ship " Silverland" was charged with unlawfully wound- ing one Joseph Mickielsol, cook on hoard tbe same vessel,at Runcorn, oa the 26th instant.—At the out- set the charge was reduced to one of assault.—From the evidence, it appeared prisoner went on board the "Silverland" on Monday morning,and quarrelled with the captain, wanting to know what he bad been saying about him and giving him a bad character. Prisoner was drunk, and behaved in a most extraordinary manner. Subsequently, he pulled out a knife, and struck at the cook inflicting a gash between the thumb and finger, and also in- flicting a small wound on his neck. Prisoner then left the boat, and information reaching the police he was locked up the same morning by Constable Wilson, who found him at the Sailors' Home.—The Bench considered the case a very 53ri0u5 one, and fined prisoner 21s and 70 cost or 21 days —Prisoner decided to go to prison.—Charles Whittle an,l Joseph Bassnett, both well-known to the police, for being drunk in Lock-street, on the 26th inn., were also committed to prison for 14 days. FUNERAL OF THE LATE Mn. WILLIAM STA.NWAY. The funeral of the late Mr William Stanway, coal merchant and grocer, of 29, Lowland's-road, Run- corn, whose death we regretfully recorded last week, at an advanced age, took place at the Greenway- road Cemetery, on Sunday last. The officiating minister was the Rev J Osborne. Messrs J Slade, J Corf, and T Lunt preceded the hearse. Messrs John • Moulton, Benjamin Crosby, T Carr, junr., and Temperley acted as bearers. The chief mourners who followed the hearse were :—Messrs J Stanway, S Stanway, A Stanway (sons), J W Stanway (nephew), S Rowlands, T Blundell, G Spender (sone-in-law), W Stanway, W Blundell, James Blun- dell, 8 Blundell, John Blundell, John Stanway. Harry Stanway. Willie Stanway, W Stan- way, Charles Stanway, Cyril Spender, and Frank Spender (grand children). There were not many floral tributes, as in accordance with the express wish of the deceased, the funeral was of a semi-private and quiet character. Deceased was a regular attendant at Bethesda Chapel, and identified himself usefully with the congregational cause in Runcorn. His latter days, however, were spent in suffering that not even the tender care of his friends and relatives could alleviate, and though his demise must have been a happy release, it was a regretful parting for those left behind. THE CHILDREN'S EVENING AT MERSEY MISSION The Mersey Mission presented quite an enlivened scene, on Tneeelay night, when the usual concert was provided by the children, who had been taught some exceedingly pretty songselances,and tableaux by the Misses Bowyer, Coward and Garvie. The chair was occupied by Mr Alfred Lightburn. The tableaux by the children included representations of "Santa Claus," " Dirty Boy," " Seasons."" Won't you come to my tea party," and " The United Kingdom," all of which were received with the highest appreciation. Vocal items and dances were contributed by Misses L Abrams, A Coward, L Shaw E Oyer, A M Bhaw, L Lawton, G Pearson, Garvie, E Sleeman, A Beckett. L Hampson, L Gibson, S Woodward, N Douglas,Messrs J Rutter,F Outhwaite W Shaw, L Tonks. The following were the children who took part : —Girls—J Oath waite, C Coward, L Coward, N Coward, D Shaw, E Hulse, L Shaysjoallie Shaw, L Tonkt, G Critchley, M Basnett, F Bra&amp; shaw, J Pasties, E Lunt, B Cawley; S Schofield, H Harrison, M Hignett, L Baines, N Callaghan M Callaehan, J Gibbs, 14 Holmes, A Jones, P Howell, A Whitfield. Boys—G Howard, W J Shaw, H Tanks, W Tonks,A Gibsonel Postlee,J Harrison,W Griffiths, G Harrison,T Howard, J Callaghan. Votes of thanks were accorded, the promoters Misses Bowyer and Coward; the accOmpanist, Mies Garvie; and the Cheirman, on the proposition of Mr W Shaw (miesieuer), seconded by Mr Shaw (senior), in a hearty style. The proceedings terminated with the singing of "Rule Britannia" and "God save the Queen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>PRESENTA.TION TO A YEOMANRY VOLUNTEER AT</p>
        <p>WESTON</p>
        <p>An interesting prese-nTatlOn- was made to Mr. H. S. V. Bankes, of Weston House, last Saturclay,upon the occasion of his leaving for the front, as a mem- ber of the 21st Cheshire Yeomanry. Mr. Thomas Ford, of Manor warm, in handing over the gifts, which consisted of an army service revolver and purse of gold, said : On behalf of the villagers of Weston, it gives me grea', pleasure to hand you this token of our esteem, and I have no doubt that you will do your duty in whatever sphere you will be placed. I wish you God-speed and a safe return. Mr. H. S. V. Bankes,who was clad in khaki uniform, gracefully responded, and said he was quite over- come with toe honour that they had conferred upon him. He should always try to do his duty as one of the soldiers of the Queen. (Applause.) A DISTRESSING CASE AT RHNCORN. At the Runcorn Police Court on Friday, before Mr. John Ockleston, Ellen- Hayes was charged with stealing a coat and a pair of trousers, the property of Robert Hampson.—Superintendent Pearson stated that the prosecutor came to the police station the previous day and gave the prisoner into custody on a charge of stealing a coat and a pair of trousers from the fiat Julia, trading on the Ship Canal. The , coat and trousers were found pledged at the shop of Mr. Lightfoot, pawnbroker, High-street. It appeared that the prisoner and prosecutor had been living together for a period of four years, and prosecutor was now desirous of withdrawing the ch arge if the woman would not melest him or follow him about.—The prisoner promised to return to her: friends in Liverpool,and the magistrates discharged her. OPEN MEETING AT MILTIN P.3.A. WIDNES. A fair attendance assembled on Sunday at the open services of tr,is brotherelood. The President (Rev J Pedlar), presided, and submitted a few de- tails of the business accomplished at the annual general meeting of the P.S.A. Miss liulme sang a solo, the men joining in toe chorus. A recitation was given by Master Lee with good eff-ct. Messrs Roberts, Thomas, Baggeridge, and Wilkinson ren- dered the quartet "Ora pro noble "very effectively. The various items were well received. Mr J Spiby delivered an address b :sed on the text" And the Lord opened the eyes of the young man." He said that though this was a brief prayer it was effectually answered, and proved that though they might be surrounded by mortal foes, if they had only faith, they could discern a far more powerful spiritual force which was more osWwhelming than any power that could be arrive I agAinst them.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY 9 MARCH 30, 1900. RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT. RUNCORN BRIDGE BILL ---- Medical Oflioer's Annual Report. BEFORE THE Dr. Adams, Medical Ogle ir ot ileaith for toe Runcorn PARLIAMENTARY COMMITTEE. Rural Discree, Council, has jest issue' his anauai report ---- upon the health aed mortanty of the distriet, of ernich we append toe following summary :—Before cornineuc- The Conveyance of Brine. lug witn tie holy of his report De A lens refers to his preliminary !WS rt. en ea ia east year's rep qt, wherein UPPER MERSEY NAVIGATION. he not ,e tee dimelutien. ye .r ey year. of waffle] cacir-s ---- ef infectious diseases, from 308, in £893, to 153. in 1897, New Clause Inserted. arid to 95 in 1893. He stated then that the Council's efforts bad been fruitful, but that this fact sheuld stimulate them to greater endeavours. In 1899, the notified cases of infectious diseases had risen to 146 a total largely contributed to bie• the extensive outbreak of scarlet fever in, and around Stockton Heath. An isola- tion hospital would have materially altered these figures if it had been available in the early phases of the out- break. 52 cases were notified in atocktoa Heath alone; SICKNESS AND MORTALITY. The Runcorn Rural Discrict censiscs of the whole of the Ruaeorn liable, with the exeeption of the Runcorn Urban District, ani comprises 40 eowaships. The eiis- trict, area comprises 49,309 acres. At too 1891 census, the population was 24,665. Dr. Adams estimated the population of the district, at the middle of 1399, to be about 27,212. Practically, he estime ed the population of the district by sabstraction of deaths', and addition of births:—ln ten years,there was a large margin for errors but we have allowea for a marked incidence ot increase in Stockton Heath,Frodsham,anci other popular and popu- lous, areas. He should be giad if toe Couocil would direct that an annual return of newly-built anl inhabi- ted houses should he furnished before December 15-.11 in each year, for purposes of esimation of population by their inspectors and serveyors, to him, efficielly. During the year ending December 31st, 1899, 595 births and 367 deaths were registered as having occurred within toe district :—comnered with, in the preceding year, 613 births and 358 deaths, a decrease of 18 oirtns and an increase of 9 deaths : very satisfactory fieurrs when compared with the very marked birth-increase in the two or three preceding years, which has been satis- factorily maintained. From the 367 deaths however, must be deducted 14 deaths occurring among pers ins dying in Dutton Workhouse, and not belonging to the Rural District Council, but to the Urban District of Run- corn. He had no knowledge of persons having ;died in other districts who belonged to Reacorn district • deducting therefore those 14 deaths from the 367, will yield a ccrrected total of 353 deaths, comparei arith a corrected total of 347 deaths in 1898, and 330 ia 1897. Of this corrected total of 353. deaths, 24 foresee the subjects for Coroners' inquests, and 10 deaths were " uncertified," no inquests being held upon them. The following analysis shows the. nature of the cases, and the reasons for the Coroners' inquests. Natural causes 4eburns 1, run over 3, suici !e by drown- ing 3, suicide by hanger, 1, found drowned 4, :accidently killed, 4, lockjaw, after injury 1, accidently drowned 3, total 24. Of the 595 births, 3!3 were males, and 262 were ' females, a largely increased proportion of wisles to females. Of the 367 total deaths, including those in Dutton Workhouse, but also belongine to the Runcorn , Urban District, 196 were males, and 171 were femaref : ' excluding the 14 deaths not belooging to the Runcorn Rural District, we have a corrected total of 189 males, and 164 ferrates. The oir h rate for the entire district during 1899 was at the rate of 21.5 per 1000 per annum of estimated peputetion ' • being lower than that of last year, when it was only 22.7 per 1000 per annum ; the birth rate of 1897 he described as low, in comparison with the aver• aere of the ten years bet Neon 1886 and 1896. He hoped it was not a general tendency for increasing civilizneen, higher culture, improved social condition of the people, to result in fewer being born to enjo, the privleges their "forebears" had won by toil and skill for their descendants. The death rate, estimated on the corrected total of 353 deaths, was 13.1 per 1,000 per annum of estimetel population was a distinct increase on the death rate of 1898, which was 12 a rser 1,000, whereas in 1895, it was so low as 12 4 Der 1,000 Still they may be proul of a death rate of 13.1 per 1,000 per annum, for boasted health resorts could not equal it. Thirty-four deaths alto- gether were caused by zymotic diseases, including dierrheee, which caused 18 (more than half), of these diseases :—while scarlet fever, chiefly through the Mu. fortunate outbreak at Stockton Heath, caused 6 deaths. There Was no reason to think that the Council's efforts in the suppression of infectious diseases was not neerine distinct evidence of marked utility, merely because of the outbreak in Stockton Heath—for their zymotic mortality was still greatly below the figures which ob- tained some years ago. Tae steam-disintector at Moore claimed a large shlare of credit for their improvement. I have already indicated that the condition for this year has been a favourable oas, in comparison with many which have occurred, and when I remind you of the census epidemic of scarlet fever prevalent for most of the year in the adjoining Township of Warrington, it would be seen that constant sources of infection must have been probable from that centre, the hospital accommodation there being insufficieat to secure the , isolation of numerous patients, while Stockton Heath ' was a residental locality for large numbers of people, whose daily occupation was in Warrington. We had 85 cases notified during tbe year, and were fortunate enough to escape with only six deaths, four being in Stockton Heath, and one each in Acton Grange and Walton Inferior ; beyond these deaths from scarlet fever but two deaths resulted from diphtheria,out of 37 noised icases, one from croup, three from typhoid fever, out of 14 cases notified, the remaining deaths being 4 from measles, 18 from diarrloce a (16 being young children), and 3 from rheumatic feve.. These facts justify a further appreciation of the efforts which your Council has carried.out for several years in reference t) suppres- sion of these diseases. Toe various deaths from zymotin diseases were : Scarlet fever 6, diphtheria 2, croup 1, typhoid fever 3, measles,4,diarrhcee, itc 18,rheumatic fever 3,from the above wao excluded inflaera a, which ha I caused 11 deaths—a lower number than in any year since 1893, while in 1891 there were 19 deeths. The 37 deaths from zymotie diseases (szcluding influenza) gave a death-rate of 1.35 per 1,000 oer annum of estimated population, compared with 1898, 137 per , 1,00. It would be observed that the death-rate from , zymotics was lower than that of last year, and only one year since 1887 had a lower zymotic death-rate, viz., 1597. Other diseases, or groups of diseases,worthy of notice, are the following :—Phthisis, caused 17 deaths, two being in children under 5 years of age, and 15 in persons of 5 years of age and upwards. The dimun..tion of deaths from this disease as maintained. Bronchitis, pneumonia, and - Plevitio caused 51 deaths, of which 21 occurred in ',Mons under 5 years of age, and 30 in porSns of 5 years of age and upwards. The death rate from these causes maintains the favourable 1 diminution observed in last year's report. Heart diseases, kl, caused 31 deaths, noun of which occurred iu pevons under 5 years of age, all being in persons of 5 years of age and upward. A slight diminution of deaths from the) diSeeses was to he noted. Injuries, accidents, and violence eatika 10 c.leal')l: all in parsec's of five years of age WO iinWasfde, A low average being maintained of d=a,ths from these causes. All other diseases . Caused 201 deaths.-68 of which occurred in persons under 5 years of age, and 133 in persons of 5 years of age, and upwards. The number of deaths from general diseases had only been exceeded in 1893, with 225 deaths, and in 1891, with 203 deaths. Dismissing statistics, Dr. / dams only wished to pay a tribute to the sound commonsense of the people goner - ally, by observing that his fears as to the results of the vaccinatiou concessions have not been realised, and that it appeared that the numbers vaccinated appear to increase, in ratio te population, luring the year the new Act had been in operation. Be appended a surnm ars-, with observations,on the 146 cases of lalectiou3 diseases reported to him during the year 1899. THE SANITARY CONDITION Or THE DISTRICT. As regards general hygienic conditions, Dr. Adams' psoreed nrogremme in reference to exs.mination of gqttati-gdpp-Ifej wig Intorrup:o by the necessity for action under the" Rivers Pollution Prevention Act," arising out of the able report by the gedfe al Offieer of Health to the Cheshire County Council : He had examined all the streams mentioned by him in that re oft, and from time to time repotted to the Council's cemmittee on results; but finally, he suggeshed teat ell-lents from all chase streams should be submittrel fo. the County Analyst for examination and report—thse was done, and only 5 or 4 were reported as bad or indif- ferent efflaente, " unfit to flow into any stream " ; this was so far satisfactory ;—.apd °Yr proceeding to deal with the had elilaents, it was fotind that all were being dealt with, either by the owners or by the local Parish Councils; he trusted when they emerged under the Council's charge, that they would ile Perfect effi cents. No great extensien of water supply, either from War- rington or from Liverpool VyrnwV water schemes, had been carried out during the year. Two distinctly needed requirements in the district were (a) the extort- sloe of and the increased efficiency of arrange- ments for night-soil ; if the existing arrangements were good, they were not carried out satisfac- torily ;—and, if they were bad, they should be improved :—and (b) an irriptovement in water sup- plies to various areas. Water main extension, and sewerage extension had been carried out in various townships. He hoped to report next year that all Frodshato Lordship had been supplied with Froisham water:—and that the Vyrriwy water had been laid on to other townships in need of improved water supply. In the statement of the Inspector's it would be seen that 58 houses had been supplied with water from water mains ;. that 59 houses were disinfected after infectious diseases e and 37 lots of bed- ding vsere removed, for steam disinfection at Moore; four lots of bedding were destroyed, after fever; and 23 samples of water were submitted for analysis, irrespsctive of the effilents under the Rivers' Pollution Prevention Acts. The proposed sewerage scnerne for Helsby has not yet received official approval. The Doctor's report was supplemented with several interesting statistical tables. Barx4 kictßsTos Packlngs: jointings. and Lubricants are always the BEST. Northern Depot e F, R. PUTZ, elan- heater.</p>
        <p>A :Select Cominittee of the House of Commons, under the presidency of 6ir T Gibson Carmichael, on Monday considered the Widnes and Runcorn Bridge Bill. It is a sctieme promoted by Sir John T Brunner, Mr. Joneph Beecham, Mr. II Wade Deaoon, Mr. .holbrook Glaskall, Mr. Duncan Nfliectinie, Mr. H Seton-Barr, Mr. H uttori Timinis, and Mr. J W Wareing for the con- struction of a transporter bridge across the Mer.iey and the Manchester Ship Canal between Widnes and Runcorn. Petitions against the bill were lolged by the Manchester Ship Canal Company, the Cheshire aCounty Council. the Weaver Navigation Trustees, the Salt Chamber of Commerce, the Salt Union, and the Upper Mersey Navigation Commissioners. It was stated that negotiations were proceeding which weie likely to move the objective of the Ship Canal Company. The opposition of the various bodies interested in the Cheshire salt trade was directed to secure that nothing in the bill should authorise the use of the bridge for carrying pipes for the conveyance of brine from the salt districts to Widnes. Mr. Worsley Taylor, QC., in opening the promoters. case, explained that both Widnes and Runcorn had grow.' considerably in recent years. They were separ- ated by the Mersey. and Lhe distance between them as the crow flies was 4-30 yards, bat if a person wanted to drive from one town to the othe: he had to go round by Warring,tou, a distance of seventeen miles. The London and North-Western Railway Company was entitled to charge a snort-distance toll of nine miles on Runcorn Viaduct, and the carriage of goods by the railway was therefore nit only inconvenient, but expensive. Pas- sengers wishing to travel by rail between the two placss had to go to Ditton Junction,and had frequently to wait , a conaiderable time for a counecting train, while the third-class tare for the distance was sixpence. There ' was a footbridge on the viaduct, which was used every year by 750.000 people, but it was difficult of access,and was not regarded as a convenient</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.] To-morrow (Saturday) the fate of the Runcorn Ratepayers' Ass Deletion will bs decided by an appeal to the p3lls in the Central Ward. Mr. Thos, Wood enters the arena as the " cause's champion," and measures strength with a formidable adversary in Ar. Joseph Hall Salkelj. Will he survive the ordeal? There are many who deride he Association, and dubb it "so-called." The vexed question of "whom do they represent ?" will, however, be settled to-morrow ia an unmistakable. mlimer. And, be the result what iG may, there will. I hear, be a revelation.</p>
        <p>The Transporter Bridge—vide Runcorn and Wid- nes Bridge Bill—is crossing stormier waters !Ilan those which flow between the borders of Cheshire and Lancashire at Runcorn Gap, in its journey through the HOMO of Commons ; and, considering the prophetic words of Nlr E Unison " opposi- tion seemed to be gathering !" there will he still more storms to face when the House of Lords is reached. Already clauses for the protection of the Mersey Commissioners; the Crown under Crown Lands Acts; the Duchy of Lancaster; the Widnes Corporation; the London and North-Western Rail- way Company; and special provisions for the use of Electric Light have been insisted upon and in- serted in the Bill. An whilst I write the Runcorn Urban District Council may succeed, possibly, in getting their batch of clauses for the protection of their streets through.</p>
        <p>In addition, to all these little alterations, which almost give the Bill a new appearance, there is the struggle of the Weaver Trustees and the Ches- hire County Council over the brine que,stion to be considered ;as well as the latest developments of the agriculturalists at Dutton Workhouse. After stage of indifference as to the fate or the Bill, the District Council—or at least that portion so often &amp;mcribed as" us farmers"—have awakened to the fact that they will, when sending hay or straw have as Kipling puts it to : " Pay! Pay !! Pay !!!' This is, naturally, distasteful to prodt-loving farmers; and, having reserved their rights to oppose in the House of Lords, unless the bridge promoters meet their views—there is going to he some more contention before the Bill becomes law.</p>
        <p>Some time ago, we heard a deal about a proposed Constitutional Club for Runcorn, and later, invita- tions to shareholders went a-begging all over the town. One would like to know what stage the pro- posed project has got to now? Could anyone answer the question ;or would they he oblged to say that the project has gone the way of the majority of projects.</p>
        <p>In striking contrast to this may be instanced the splendid energy of the Liberal Association of Run- corn, which has resulted in the beautified and ex• tended premises now available on the old site of the Runcorn Liberal Club, in Regent-street. The new club room is now—though not quite out of the hands of the decorators—open for the use of the members; and a right royal club room it proves. By the way, this is but another lasting monument to the generosity of Sir J T Brunner, B art, M.P.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer' of Health of the Runcorn Rural District Council, Dr. Joseph Adams, M. 8.. 0.M., etc., has just issued his annual report. A perusal of it has disclosed to me that the district generally has a satisfactory standing with regard to health, mortality, and ss.nitation. ; and, f.:nnugh there are many little defects wllethe Dactor still points to as requiring rem*, on the whole, the district compitrei Mc:lrk) than favourably with other Asa reort from a 4,3dical Officer to his District Council, however, t cannot say I accord with the tone of some of the remarks and recom- mendations submitted.</p>
        <p>The discussion at the Boari of Guardians regard- ing the collection of coutributions to out-relief is a short one, but reveals to Us either that the col- lector is remiss in not writing off bad debts; or that the Bos,rl of Guardians pursue a policy of try- ing to get" blood out of stones." Upon the Chair- man's own confession, we learn of a man, who has been to prison three or four times in liquidation of a debt. He was unable, or, if able,unwilling to pay. It is pleasing, therefore, to see that the action of the Auditor has put an end to this policy for at least a time. Now, it remains for the Out-relief Committee to see that the "inquisition "is not re- instated.</p>
        <p>I glean that at. the noxl, Urbin Cyuacil tneatiii,g there is going to be a7reckouing-up. Some of the Councillors are guilty of having given vent to several indiscrete utterances, and now then hangs over their heads the sword of Damocles. The ex- pected disclosures will, it is said, prove to be the "stone that rebsunied" with a vongeance. But, thereby hangs a tale!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>FOOTEAtt.</head>
        <p>FOOTEAtt.</p>
        <p>Luckily I rerved my judgment of last Satur- day's match, for quite easily might I have commit- ted mys3lf. Ylaryport proved a tougher nut to crack than was generally expected, a surprise at which everyone was delizhted ; but the Runcorn section of the crowd were more delighted at the re- sult than their visitors. And considering the home team won by 12 points to nil,they might well be.</p>
        <p>To-morrow, the journey t 3 Bradford will be an accomplished fact. The hopes of Runcorir lie centred in the fifteen who will don the green. At times One feels that Runcorn stand a brilliant chance, at others the heart—of a football enthuiiast of course—stands still appalled with the visions of defeat. But whether defeat or victory is the lot of the Linnets at Bradford, there is one thing we can and do pride ourselves upon, and that is the "red- tails" will play to a man, and die game to the end. The issues of th 3 match are great, Bradford being top of the Yorkshire section and Runcorn top of the Lancashire Section of the &amp;.U., so that the game besides deciding who shall have another bid for the cup settles the question of who are the .13,9 t team in the N. 11., and consequently in the world.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S ocot ha l a world-wide reputation as a del- cious, strengtkening beverage, ad a valuable nutritive focd The "Lane says it roinTs.nts " the standard of highest purity." Always insist on having CA.DRURY'S-30111 only in Packets and Tins—asther Cocoas are often substituted for the sake of•extra proat.—cd: (2</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MEANS OF COMMUNICATION.</head>
        <p>MEANS OF COMMUNICATION.</p>
        <p>Originally there was a terry, which the railway COM- panv had underteken to maintain, but it had gradually fallen into disuse, and in one week recently it was used by only fourtvu people. The communities concerned had been very anxieus to get, improved means of corn- municatien' and in 1892 they approached the County Ceuncils of Lancashire and Cheshi e, and proposed to them the erection of a bride° at the cost of £140,000. That proposal was not entertained, and now certain gentlemen had banded themselves together, and had come to Parliament, and proposed to construct a bridee. rbe capital of the company was £75.000, with borrow- ing powers for a further sum of £25 000, and it was ewe. I mated that the cost of the scheme would be £BO,OOO. Every one of the promoters had a substantial interest in the locality, and their names were a guarantee that there should be no difficulty in finding the capital. Behind them were a large number of others equally influential and ovally interested in the dis- trict, and therefore there could be no doubt that this wee a bona-fide bill, brought in iupply a public want. In addition, the measure had the support of the local authorities of Widnes and the di t:rict around. The proposed structure wee what wae called a transporter bridge, a form of bridge better known abroad than in this country. One had been constru^.ted across the Bilbao River, where the shipping traffic was much greater than on the Upper Mersey, and although it was placed in an exceedingly exposed situ- ation, it had worked without the slightest hitch for eight years. There was another at R seen, and during the present session a bridge of this kind had been Aucho• riseel at Newport by anoteer committee, so that already the practicability of the idea bad been recoguised by Parliament. The pr sposed bridge would be erected a few yards above the Runcorn Railway Bridge. A cen- tral span, 1,000 feet long, would be supported on two upright piers erected on either gide of the estuary. The height of this girder would be 82 feet above the ordinary wile tide, and as the height of the Runcorn Bridge, which was the</p>
        <p>KEY TO THE rOSITION,</p>
        <p>and determined toe size of the vessels that passed up and down the Mersey and the Snip Canal at this point, was only 79 feet 6 inches, there could be no objecteee to the scheme in this particular. The car would be 55 feet long and 24 feet wide. It would be suspended from the bridge at the height of 12 feet 6 inches above high water, and would be worked with duplicate electric motors. The time occupied in the whole journey across would be 2i minutes. Toe Mersey at the Runcorn Gap was nevig- aole only tor small vessels for about two or three hours before and after high water,and the Mersey Conservancy, who had censidered the Bill, were perfectly satisfied that the scheme woul.d not interfere with the navigation of the river. The promoters had deno their best to ovoid interference with the important traffic on the Ship Canal. The time occupied in crossing the genet would be only haif a minute. The only matter for discussion was in regard to the petitions from toe different b3dies representing the salt district. Salt was very largely used in the Widnes Chemical Works. In many cases the salt had to be resolved into its original form of brine and many manufacturers wou'.l like, instead of buying the salt, to bring the brine in pipes to Widnes. [ In the salt district the pumping of the brine caused very serious subsidences, and specal ' legislation had been pegged prey:ding for compensation. The local authorities were, therefore, exceeiingly jealous of any proposal to take away brine without giving Cheshire its duo share of the profit. They, therefore, said that although the bill! contained no proposal for pla.oing a pipe on the bridge for the conveyance of brine, it did not follow that the promoters would not put such a pipe on it, or allow someone else to do it for a consideration,and they sought a clause binding the promoters never to construct a pipe for the conveyance of brine across the bridee. He maintained that the committee should no; be asked to insert in the bill a provision which might be put forward as in the natur3 of a statutory bargain at a future time, when anyone asked for Parliamentary powers to lay a 'pipe for the conveyance of brine across the bridge. The promoters wanted to be in a position to hold the balance between the two</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>CONFLICTING INTERESTS.</head>
        <p>CONFLICTING INTERESTS.</p>
        <p>The promoters did not wish to raise any prescription in favour of Widnes on the one hand, er in favour of Clribire OA the other, but to leave the matter abso- lutely open. They did not themselves mean by the present Bill either direely or indirectly to ly3 ripe on the bridge, and they were contfl aver it itiai thfnigh desirab7e by Widnes to such a proposal, to have the whole threshed out In Parliament. They, ' therefore, propoTe to in_ert the following clause in the Bill ;—" The company shall not, without further Parlia- Meiiihrk P3V,ers, coney, Or authorise or permit to be I conveyed, any brine in any pipe, trough, or like work, I in, upon, over, or under the bridge by this Act authorised." In answer to the Chairman, Mr. &amp;limey, on bellalf of the Salt Union and the Salt Chamber of Commerce, and Mr. M for the Weaver Navigation Trustees and the Cheshire County Council, stated that they were not prepared to accept the clause. Mr. Worsley Taylor, proceeding, pointed out, in re- gard to the petition as to upper Mersey navigation, that clauses had been idled at the instance of the Mersey Conservancy which afforded every conceivable protection to navigation, and met all the allegations of the peti- tioners,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>SePibittrif</head>
        <p>SePibittrif</p>
        <p>Mr. B. Wade DRAZOII. examined by Me, Lloyd. stated that the cemmittee promoting the bill consistel of manta,. facturer3 and others interested in the welf ire of Widnes. Runcorn, and the district generally. Some moles of quick transport was most desirable. This was the natural point for connecting Lancashire with Cheshire, The charge for the carriage of goods by rail between Widnes and Runcorn wis so heavy that it Was oftmi as cheap to cart them by Warringtog. For passenger traffic the railway was naiiher convenient nor econeinicai, and tfo ferry vvos practically useless. In his view, the bill u4Aild' provide proper and efficient communication. Them+ \Would be no difficulty in raising the capital. By Mr. Minn : Ele was net aware that Parliament had consigten'tly refused to allow railways to be made through the sc/t district without a provision prohibiting the carriav of brine out of the district. The bridge was not in the salt dis.,:gict. No doubt brine-pumping did a c,rlat deal of damage in tbe district which at present got the advantage which resulted from having the industry, though he did not know the.people who suffered from the sabsidences get the benefit. The notices given for the bill covered power to lay pipes: The promoters de- sired to bi able to do it if thef thOught fit, but they had no intention of conveying brine across the bridge. Was there in any draft of the bill a clause to enable pipes to be laid across the bridge ? In the bill as at first drafted there wis such a clause.</p>
        <p>What wis the ohje—ci--", if it wa Dot to carry-brlWe. We took the power to do so if neces9ar.</p>
        <p>Is it nnt rather foreign to the idea of. a transporter bridge to take pipes across it P—lt seems-to me a most natural thing to carry pipes acress such a bridge. The clause has now been dropped. May I take it there is no intention on the part of the promoters to use this bridge for the conveyance of brine P—Okrtainly. Theu why do you object to the prohibition clauso 7 It would be unpatriotic to the Lancashire side: I object to having a general clause which will practically for ever prohibit an industry being carried on in Lancashire if desired.</p>
        <p>Witness, eontinning, said that at the present mothent he did not know that it would be an advantage to Wid- nes to have the brine, but he could imagine the circum- stances und3r which it would b 3 a great advantage. By Mr. Square.—rie believed :he bridge would bring in a large revenw,and would be a paying concern:'</p>
        <p>He had no desire to injure any industry. Mr. T Sutton Timmis, who has been in business in Widues for thirty-five years, agreed that the present means of communication between the two counties was most unsatisfactory, and that the proposed bridge would be a satisfactory method of improving it. Widnes with its 32,000 inhabitants, would certainly afford a considerable opening for the agricultural and dairy pro- duce of Cneshire, which was at present kept out by the high railway charges. By Mr. Pope : If the brine was brought across the bridge to Widnes there would, no doubt, be less salt maee in Cheshire. The Mayor of Widnes stated that the Town Council had passed a resolution in favour of the bridge, and had authorised him to give evidence in support of it. Mr. John J. Webster, past president of the Liverpool Engineering Society, and the designer of the bridge, explained the scheme, and stated that it would not inter- fere in any way with the navigation of the upper Mer- sey. By Mr. Balfour Browne: The weight of such a pipe as would b- necessary to carry brine across the bridge wou:d be 400 tons, and the bridge as designed would not be strong enough to carry it. He could not say exactly what weight iu addition to the car the bridge would carry. The Chairman (to counsel) : Is it your desire that the bridge should us strengthened ? Mr. Belfour Browne : Oh, no! weakened rather. (Laughter). Mr. Harris, C.E. (partner of Sir F Bramwell) spoke in favour of the scheme from an engineering point of view. This concluded the promot-rs' case. THE OPPOSITION. Mr. Balfour Browne. Q.C., addressed the committee on behalf of the Salt t nion, arguine that in view of the special legislation with regard to compensation for sub- sidences it was not desirable that brine should be re- moved from the salt district. He objected to the pro- posed clause, and asked that the status quo should be preserved. Mr. Pope, Q.C., for the Cheshire County Council, pointed Out that teat body had a direct interest in any propos il which would diminish the revenue of the Weaver Trustees. In its inception this scheme was not as innocent as it now appeared. The alkali manufac- turers of Widnes certainly did contemplate taking I power to carry a pips across the bridge, and the with- drawal ef that proposal, taken in conjunction with the clause now offered, raised a reasenable suspicion in the minds of those interested in Cheshire that the scheme was the first step towards an endeavour to obtain the sanction of Parliament for ithat which Parlianaeat had hitherto prohibited. Mr. Pember, repreeenting the Weaver Trultees, said that body was not prepared to accept anything short of an absslute prohibition of the conveyance of brine in pipes across the proposed bridge. It was stated that an agreement between the promo- ters and the Ship Canal Company had bean arrived at. I The committee passed the preamble of the bill, sub- j•ct to the insertion of the clause relating to the convey- ance of brine proposed by the promoters. TUESDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. Adjustment of Clauses. Dangers to Navigation. The Select Committee of the House of Commons (Sir T. Gibson Carmichael chairman), who on the previous day passed the preemtole of the Widnes and Runcorn Bridge Bill, giving powers for the construction of a bridge on the transporter system between Widnes and Runcorn, met on Tuesday to consi ler the =tested eta uses.</p>
        <p>Mr. Squarey, on bslealf ef the Upper Mersey Conger- vaecy Commissioners, suggested a clause requiring the promoters to make bylaws ensuring reasonably safe navigatioa on the Mersey in view of the working of the bridge. Mr. Fitzgerald, Q C ,wha appeared for the pr ornoters, pointed out that the Board of Trade would determine the question of by-laws. Sr George Nares, Acting-Conservator of the Mersey, was asked by the chairman of the committee if there was any danger to navigation on avouut of the bridge and its transporter. To this Sir George replied that the Mersey Conserv- ancy had considered the bridge only in connection with what effect it would have en the estuary. That was more immediately under his charge; hut as to the dan- ger to passing ships and barges, that was not in his department. In is opinion, however, some regulations were necessary, but the Board of Trade were interested in such regulations. The Chairman remarked that the transporter wou'd pass under the bridge at a distance of only 124 feet from the level of high water, and could Sir George Nares make any suggestion for regulating.the working of it. Sir George Nares' Suggestion. Signalling the Transporter's Movements. Sir George NArS would advise that it should be con- sidered much in the same way as a swing bridge, which had to give way to navigation, and give signals when it was about to move. Of course,ithese things gradually settled themselves, but on a strong tidal river signals shou'd be given, as in the case of the Tower Bridge in London Upon the suggestion of Mr. Fitzgerald,the committee agreed tnat the clause should read :—" The company may, and if required by the Board of Trade shall, make bylaws, Sze. Sir Cameron Gull (member) asked if there should not be some special provision in regard to fog. Mr. Filzgerald : The B oard of Trade generally deals with those matters. Mr. Squarey pointed out that one clause made the interests of nevigation subsidiary t the interests of the bridge. He asked for a reverstl. The Chairmen said the committee thought that clause of the promoters was a large order, as the navigation was there before the bridge. Mr. Fitz;erald agreed that the clause should be struck out.</p>
        <p>Mr. Squany observe' that the bridge was a new thing to this country, and nobody could say before having practical experience what the effect of its work- ing wou!d be upon navigation, and to what dangers and risks navigation in that part of the Mersey would be ex- posed. Thorefore, having regard to tbe enormoua un- certainty of the working of the bridge, he asked for a clause to make the company pay compensation for any damage done to ships with which the transporter or any part of the machinery might come in contact. Mr. Fitzgerald characterised the request as unreason- able, RH it might possibly hold the promoters liable for the clumsy negligence of a drunken skipper. The Chairman : We agree with Mr. Fitzgerald. Other claus?s of miner character having been dealt with, the hill was reported to the house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
