      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY'S FORTHCOMING CONCERT. Gounoci's Redempti9l."</head>
        <p>THE MUSICAL SOCIETY'S FORTH- COMING CONCERT. Gounoci's Redempti9l."</p>
        <p>The attention of all lovers of high-class music is called to the importance of Gounod's grand oratorio " The Redemption" and to the singularly appro- priate time, at the close of the Lenten interval, which has been chosen by the Warrington Musical Society for its first performance in tee town. In "The Redemption " we have undoubtedly the consummation of lifetime devoted to the creation of some of the finest tone pictures of our day, there having been nothing written which can be consi- dered as entitled to rank with it since Mendelssohn's " Elijah "was given to the world. Certain it is that the completion of the work MO felt by Gounod himself to mark the accomplishment of a great ideal, and equally true it is thnt he regarded the score as embodying the best work of which he was capable. The OrAtorlo,—or sacred trilogy, as it is more elactly ternied,—is planned on a most imposing scale std deals with the three great fads on which depends the existence of the Christian Churcb,--the passion and Death of the Saviour—His glorious life on earn from His RelUrtection to His Ascension —and the spread of Christianity in the world through the mission of the Apostlegf thd thi6d patts being preceded by a prologue on the thinticin, the Fall of our first parents, and the promise of a , Redeemer. The music is essentially modern in conception and treatment, and in the course of its performance will be found many of those passages whose rare melodic beauty allied with masses of tone colour- ing—often almost sensuous in richness and depth— we at once recognise as strikingly characteristic of the composer. To the usual material of chorus and orchestra necessary for the performance of a modern com- position of this class repleteness has been given in "The Redemption "by the introduction of a com- plement of six trumpets, a ham and a "celestial choir," end, as next Wedneslay'A performance will be on a most complete sCAIe, all these "extras " viii be requisitioned. The comic for the " cele choir" will be sung by a number of choir-boys f -om the Liverpool Cathedral, by the kind permission of F. A. Burstall, Esq., F.H.C.O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FRODSHA-M.</head>
        <p>FRODSHA-M.</p>
        <p>LECTIIEtE ON THE TRANSV4AL.--ThO Rev. 171 B Blogg (vLear) prsiled over a well-attended lecture at the Town Hall. Fro isham, on rueFday evening last. Tne sulect of the leit,•ire wAs " The Transvaal;" and was capanly dealt with by Mr J D Bidden (Chester), who was ably atsisted by Mr Turton, who acted as are efficient manipu- lator of the lime-light lantern which depicted beautiful scenes of the principal places dealt with. The object of the ereertainment was to supplement the war fund, and proved highly successful. A vote of thanks was accor- ded the lecturer, on the motion of the Chairman, secon- ded by Mr T D 'Timmins. The singing of the National Anthem 'brought the proceedings to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the above Board wag held at the Workhouse, Duttou, on Tuesday. There were present :—Messrs J Moseley-Williams (chairman), Siniul Taylor, J Me tdowcroft, Toornas Re.y nAcis, AR Norm ,n, Robert Wright; the Rural District Councillors, *Rh Mr George F Ashton (clerk), and Mr W E Hough (assisnat clerk).</p>
        <p>£ll5 FOR LAUNDRY IMPROVEMENTS. The Local Government Board wrote, with r9ference to the i:roposal of the Guaruiaus of the Runuoru Caton to puicnass new laundry macninery for the Workhouse, troki Messrs Timmins and S•n4, at a cost of £ll5, vritk- out ad vertising tor sealed tenders,asking fur tuturination showing whore it was proposed to place the engine in- cluded in Messrs Timmins tender, and as to whO was to take charge of it. The Clerk stated Ire had received a report upon the matter from the Master, and had repliAl forwarding the necessary informati, n.</p>
        <p>SCNOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTICE...--TSE CLERK'S SALARY. The Local Governrent Board intimated their sanction to the paymeat ot £3i to the Clerk, for ni3 services dur- ing the year as cle,rk Co the School Attendance Committee of the Runcorn Union.</p>
        <p>THE NEWLY-APPOINTED NURSE. Referring to the appointment of Mary Hampshire, as nurse at the Workhouse of the Runcorn Union, the Local Government Board saw no objection t 3 it. The salary was fitx.d at £25 per annum. with an annual increase of £l, until a maximum of £3O was reached, with £2 in lieu of uniform, and rations and other allowances assigned to her by the Guardians. BOARDS OF GUARDIANS AND THE LOCAL GDYERNMEWT BOARD. THE TREATMENT OF SOLDIERS AND SAILORS. Mr. H. E. Lawrence, secretary of the Association of Poor Law Unions in England and Wales, forwarded a copy of a res3lution for the provision of soldiers and sailors passed at the last meeting of the executive council of that body: " That the necessary steps should be taken by the legislature to adequately provide from National sources for (a) the wives and children of soldiers and sailors now being partly maintained by charity (b) all soldiers and s.ilors disabled whilst on active service (f) the widows, orphans,and otherstotally dependent up,n soldiers and sidors dying whilst on active service." The resolution was accompanied by a request, that, if the Board approved of such resc,lution, they wculd petition tuo H 'uses of Pialiameut (At the ' subject, and solicit the supp3rt of tl3, loaal aembers of Parliament.</p>
        <p>The Association also forwarded a copy of the follow- ing resolution, asking for the views of the Board regard- ing the same :—" That the relations between the Local Government Board and Bytris of Guardians need serious c msideration, with a view to securiug to Guardians greater freedom of action in the discharge of their public duties, and this Association requests the executive council to give the matter its consideration." ' The Clerk observed, with regard to the first portion of the Association's letter, the Runeorn Board of Guar- dians had already peAtioned the House, so that there was only the question of the second resolution to be con- sidered. The Chairman for his part thought it was a most desir- able resolution to support. Mr. Talbot questioned whether the Board had ever found the Local , Government Board hard upon them, that they wanted to come into contact with tutln in any way?</p>
        <p>rth. Chairman : I think we have sometimes. Mr. Talbot expressed the opinion that there was no reason to be dissatisfied with the treatment received lately._ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman added be was referring to the dietary, and several matters with regard to th, ngagetnent of servants at tha house. They had to receive the sanction of the Local Government Board to almost every little thing, and he was of elonion that Boards of Guareiani should have more liberty upon those things. He instanced a case which had recently come under hie notice whece the experience of a Cha•lton Board of Guardians was that by putting bread upon the tables, and allowing the inmates to help them,elveo, instead of doling out to them fixed allowances, the Guardianssaved about 20'wt. of bread per week. They applied for per- mission front the Local Government Board to f:ive deem bread in the way be had just menttened, hut the Local Goverttrat nt Board declined to permil tnev to nolke the alteratien, so they took the matter into their own hands.</p>
        <p>Tho master in answer to the Chahinaa admitted that the dietery of the house once tixe i•b; the L'cal Govern meat Board :could not be alt r,hn; stated that of Dutton was a vrry liberal one, and had only been re• cently revised. Mr. Linaker took tha view that if the resolution would do anything to faeili:ate he administration of the Poor Law they should support it, Per. S Woodward proposed, and Mr. Talbot seconded, that the communication lie ipon the table. This was untnimously agreed to. THE ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE .</p>
        <p>Mr. John Mclntyre submizteci toe rep )n of the Assess- ment Committee, whic.in hal met prior to the Board. Arnengst other matters the C mmittee had considered the remuneration of the clerk of the Aa.essment Com- mittee for the pas!', year. They found that besides the usual work of the Committee there had ir,en a good deal of extra work in connection with the appeals of the Weaver navigation; Helsby Telegraphic Company; and the Staffordshire Railway Con.pany, and their Clerk bad been obliged on several occasions to go eat of town. The Cornmittre recommended that the Clerk be allowed £lOO for what he had done for the Committee during th,, past year. He mlved the adoption of that recomm,mdation. Mr. S. Woolward sccondednd the report was unanimously adopted. THE RELIEF SUPER7I9ION. WRITING OFF BAD DEBTS.</p>
        <p>The Chairm in referred to a recomm•mdat'oa fr -rn the auditor, that the Guardians should not bring forward time after time, and quarter after quarter, the large amount of irrecoverable sure, whicti they did in the collector's book, in the matter of contributors to out.. relief. As a consequence the committee had. that day written off a considerable sum. Mr. Norman pointed out that in the case of a an go. lug to prison the sum should base beer% wriqen off as from that date by the collector. The Chairman instanced the ca :ae of one man who had . b-en to prison three or four tirrAs. (Laughter.) ' Mc. Linaker : Tata he hvAs (Laughter.) 114 id tbiee Pr fc4l? tilllel, The recomrnendat4- ..•tl Ini ac'tion of the Linmittee, were then agreed - 1 e. tO unanimously. r ..4.IITRACTs FOR PROVISIONS, RTC. , The fol. clethi• ./owing tenders for 1119 sup ,ly of provisions, . .fig etc., for the hole were accened be the ":-..a.rd :—George Bowcock. PuPcher's meat ; Fred Weir, Imilk, butter, eggs, etc ,• J fl Weston. drugs ; Newton, Chambers, and Co.. dis;nfectan:a ; Percy ii Wright, drapery ; T Jackson Lee, clo:hiag ; Ed wiu Gregory, arogerias ; Joseph W Bazley, boots, etc ; Frodsham Gae iad Water Co., coke ; Thomas Barrow, coffins ; T Booth and Sons, ccnvovinces ; and W Grounds, cartage. The tender of M •ssrs Richard Evans and Company for coal, was referred back with a requ,•st for a lower quotation. —...---- OUR</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>FOOTBALL COMPETITION</head>
