      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>INFLUENZA. INCREASING.</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA. INCREASING.</p>
        <p>The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the Country. The visitation is of a very prostrating nature, and the disease is certainty on the lir.rease. The origin of the disease or rather the circumstances which bring it about is as much a mystery as ever. You cannot trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Nervous System to a greater extent than aloaeat any other disease, and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man who imagines be had only a mild form of Influenza, and Can go about the street just as usual. It can hardly be doubted that the prevalence of Chest and Lung Com- plaints must be ascribed to the baneful influence of In- fluenza. whose after effects so frequently take this form. It was a bad day for this Country when this malignant scourge &amp;A visited our shores. Year after year it has never since failed to revive at its due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Having observed its ravages and its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visits, ue cannot but regard its recurrence with much fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever will, regain their former health. As a Prey, ntative, we know of nothing equal to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken in time have often proved effectual in warding off attacks of this Malady, and always succeed in mitigating their severity when a patient is under their influence. AP° you suffer from Chills, alternating with Sudd.en Flushes, Severe Headache, accompanied by difficulty of breath- ing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest under and between the Shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of the Muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousness. If you suffer from any of these symptoms, know that they are the forerunners of Influenza, and it behoves you to esort at once to a course of Gwilvm Evaos' Quinine Bit- ers. By common consent Patients everywhere admit hat they feel more depressed and miserable after an at- ack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The best way to banish these miserable feelings is to take Gwilvm Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. We can straugly and confidently recommend this Preparation, as it has treated severe cases of Influenza, Heavy Cslds, Extreme Weakness and Lan- four, with greater success than any other known Remedy. We may also add that as a remedy for Indiges- tion, Sleeplessness, Low Spirits, NervoiLness, and Loss of Appetite. Gallym Evans' Quinine Bitters stands un- equalled. It is sold in bittles 2s 9d and 43 64., by all Chemists. and Patent Mediciee Vendors, or direct car- riage flee, I r the above prices, by the L'roprietors : Quinine Bit era Manufacturing 0 mpany, , Limited, Llanelly, South Wsl,s. Avoid Imitations. See the name " Gwilytn Ea' e" on Label Stamp, and Bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A WAastsciraN 'Bus DRIVER CHARGED VVITH THETT. At the Dareabury Petty dri€loll Tui-slay, before 117'. J. 0, PA': lna oth r rolgislr.Ls, Witlimn g: 90ad- eneugh (aa old Warrington 'bus driver) traa charged with stealing 561b3. of iron plat,s and bolts, of the 'value of is 6d, at Acton Granee, on the B'h Match, the pro- perty of the Sip Canal Cornpany.—D fendant pleaded not guilty.—Mr. Newman (Messrs. Grundy and Ker- shaw, Manchester) presecuted on behalf of the coin- panv, and stated that the defendant was employed on the 8'.1) March in carting titaber on behalf of a man who had purchased a considerable quantity from the Ship Canal Company at Acton Grange. Goodenough was shout to go away with a load of timtrr when Daniel Campbell, a foreman, came up on a locomotive, and, having his suspicions, examined the contents of the cart and felted the articles mentioned in the charge. When charged with the theft the de- fendant asked to be forgiven.—Defendant said he had no idea the plates and bolts were in tha cart. He had not seen it loaded.—Camptell and William 'ripping, a timekeeper arid clerk in the ser- vic. of the company, also gave evidence, the latter statiog that Goodenough admitted the offence and begged hard to be forgiven.—Defendant made a long and iecoberent statement, repeating tbeasserzion that hehad no tnewledge the plates and bolts were in the cart.— The Superintendent of the Prace stated that there were 12 previous cnnvictions against the defendant, who was now fined 10i and costs, or a month's imprisonment in default with hard labour.—Mr. Newman stated that the company had been robbed in a very extensive manner both at Acton Grange and at Runcorn, but as the magis- trates had inflicted severe fines a the last named place, the robberies had ben stepped. He asked the BenCh, therefore, to pr, raise to inflict severe penalties in all Cues of the •Icind brought before that Bench.—Tha Chairman: We cannot prejudge cases, but we have no hesitation in expressing a strong opinion that offenders who come before us will be severely dealt with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Wrieht, :Councillor Smethurst. Dr Garnett and Mr. Heoherd. WORKHOUSE INMATE IN THE DOCK—Julia Patten, last from the Workhouse, was charged with absconding from the Workhouse on the 20-h March. taking with her clothing, the property of the Guardians—Mr. B :shearer who prosecuted stated that the prisoner was on Wednesday, allowed to go to Padgate Industrial ziehools, for the purpose of taking two of her children out She never returned end information was given to the police with the result that she was apprehended on Thursday morning Prisoner stated that she took her child- ren to their father at ECCIPS and returned to Warrington on hursday with the object of taking the clothing to the Work- house and going back again to Eccles. She had the money with her to pay the fare —P C Dennett, who arrested the woman, was called and said that when apprehended prisoner stated that she was going to the Workhouse to take her clothing and was returning to Eccles that eight.—On prisoner promising to take the clothing to the Workhouse the barge was withdrawn, SATURDAY—Before Messrs. T. Bitchall (in the chair). H. T. Fairelough and :r W. Hayward. WIFE essauvr.—John McMahon. was charged on a war- rant with assaulting his wife Bridget MeMahon on the 17th March.—Mr. Browne prosecuted and stated that. On the date In question prisoner same borne under the influence of drink. took up a fur pound weight an I ran his wife out of the house. A man took the weight from him and he went to a public house. He returned at 10-30 and threatened to finish' complainant with a garden spade. lie knocked one of her t eth out with a poker. bhe had to run jut) the parlour. and barricade the door. Complainant asked for a separation order.—ttrisoner stated tnat he had a drop of drink on St. Patrick's day and it effected him."— Superintendent Monaghan said that when priboner was apprehended he said he ought to have knocked all his wife's teeth out and she would eat less. —A flue of Us an.t costs was imposed and a separation order was made, prisoner to conttibute 10s a week towards the maintenance of his wife and children.</p>
        <p>MomraY.—Befors Mr. F. Monks (in the chair). Alderman Blaekhurst Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs J. W. Hay-</p>
        <p>ward and S. Wright. THEFT BY A Boy. —Jame Devereaux, a little boy residing at 1. Porter-street, was charged with stealing from the dwelling-house of. Charles Dickenson. 4, Porter-street, between one o'clock end three o'clock on Tuesday afternoon, a boy's jacket value 103.—efe ple eled gui:tv —Superintendent Monaghan stated that the jacket was seen safe in a drawer In the kitchen at one o'oloce na the date namei, an i was miss- ing at three o'clock. Between 3 and four o'elock the prisoner sold the jacket to a woman named Johnson who ke t a second-band shop in Mersey-street tor Is, 6d.—Mrs. Johnson was called, and said she bought he jacket from the prisoner for is. 63. He came to the shop regularly.—The Chairman thought it was a shady trensaetion.—Prisoner was ordered to reef:felt Ox strokes with the birch and be kept in the cells tit 11X o'clock. DreoftbEeleit Coenutve.—Thomas Balmer, Daniel Siesmith, George Sixsmith, see William Cringall ; youths, were summoned for committing a preach of the peace by behaving In a disorderly manner in Factory-lane on the 18th inet.— P.C. Kane proveet the case, and stated that the lads wore kicking a little 'call aeout, shouting and making use of bad language. They had all been previously cautioned about annoying the neighbours. —The Superintendent said that the lads 13A.d been guilty of horseplay, more than anything else. One of them stated that they w-re simply playing at t)p and pass "and no football.—Fined 33 6d each. Scealz ix Mottere's-couer. Joseph Garrett and Maria Garrett. of Morrisla-coert, were charg,d with having been drunk and disorderly in Morris's-court at 12.15 on Sunday morning.—The man pleaded guilty, and the woman not guilty Taylor said that at 12.15 on Suneay rnornieg the was called to Morns's-court. where he saw the prisoners th a drunken condition and making* a' big row "especially the woman. The man had a hatchet and was breaking open che door of the house where he lodged. —P.C. Johnson orreboratee.—Fined 5s each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions. MONDAY.—Before Messrs. John Ockleston and Walter</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions. MONDAY.—Before Messrs. John Ockleston and Walter</p>
        <p>Timmins. MINOR OFFENDER-S.—Patrick Doyle. of Runcorn. admitted using obscene language o, a most revolting charact,r, within hearing of the public street.and was fiael ss.—Edwarci Web- ster, and George Dennis were each fined 5s for drunkenness. A CANDIDATE FoR THE INtBRIATES ',lolllE.—James Rob- inson, Newtown, Prodehan., to a charge of drunkennEss and disobedience to a summons issue against him in 1889, re- plied he was guilty of bi lug drunk, but was never Served with a summons.— The Magistrates' Clerk : That the sum- mons was served was amply proved to the magistrates when they granted the warrant upon which prisoner had been arrested.— The case was proved by Constable Charles Nisbolis.—Superintendent D. Pearson added that the pri- soner had been 21 times previously convicted, and was at any</p>
        <p>time a candidate for an" Inebriates home." He pointed that out as he considered he was bound to.—Fined Sc and 14s costs. NEGLECT or BDUCITION.—The Runcorn School Board prosecuted the following parents for neglecting their several children's education, and the usual fines of 6d and 4s 6d costs were inflicted ; —Thomas Ireue, boats; William Cavanagh. Gilbert,-street; John Giles, Wellington-street; David North, Greenwav-road ; James Bate, Bridgewater-st.; Josepk Spencer, Stanley.villas. Cases against Samuel Wainwright, boatman; Enoch Walker, and Buoch Lee, were adjourned. YOUTHS IN TROUBLE FoR POACHING —George Deakin. Richard Deakin. Frederick Deakin, and Samuel hobinson, of Stanebills-lane ; Edward Dunn, of Perry-street, Runcorn. and John Whitmore, Barl-street. Crewe, pleaded guilty to being on land in the occepation of Henry Griefs. and William Griffiths. Helton, in search of game on the 11th of March.— Constable John Upton said on Sus;day. Aarch 11th, he saw the youths working a couple of dogs flr3t on Griffiths land and then on Grice's. They ran a couple of rabbits. and killed the second. Subsequuntly. he headed them off. and upon searching them found a water-hen upon Frederick Deakin, and Samuel Johnson. There was another man with them but he ran of. The dogs were an Irish terrier belonging to Frederick Deakin, and a retriever belonging to Richard Deakin.—Defendants had nothing to say.—The Chairman observed they were all liable to a fine of five pounds. but as it was a first offence, they would be fined 10,3 and Be 6d costs each; Whitmore would be fined 101 and 93 6d costs. CoNvICTED HoUBEBRRAICER ToLD To QUlT.—Thomas Pimiott was charged in custody with being a conviited thief, and with frequenting Bridge-street and Church-street with intent to commit a felony.—Serzeant Gregory stated about 5 pm. on Saturday he was in Bridge-street, in company with Detective Bowyer. when he saw Pinolott enter the market, and f/0 thronAh it in a suspicious manner. They followed him, and subsequently Pimlott entered a passage at the side of Mr. Oraddick's pawnshop. where he stayed for a few minutes acting suspiciously. Later witness locked him up, and on searching him found he had not a penny.—By Superintendent Pearson: He asked Pimiott his business, and prisoner told him to find out, swearing in the same breath.--Constable Bowyer bore corroborative evidence.— Prisoner having been sworn stated he was a convicted thief. having been convicted of hause-breaking in Runcorn, in 1896. He came to Runcorn on Seturdey, and visited his friend Martin Gilligan. He argued that he had no intention of committing any felony in Runcorn, and that if he had had it was not, likely he would have entered the town In broad daylight. He was bound for Chester. and was going to look for work at the lead works there.—By Superintendent Pear- son: It was true he had sever worked at the lead works before, but he understood they were always in want of men. —The Bench: Will you clear out of town in less than an hour ?—Pris mer : Yes, gentlemen.—The Chairman: Then ou that nucletstanding you can go. It is twelve now, so you must be gone at one.</p>
      </div>
