      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Frodsham Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Frodsham Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs. Chsrles Reynolds (presiding),</p>
        <p>and J. Crossland Tay FOOTPATH OBSTRUCTORS.—Three Hetsby youths, Ralph Bandies, James Egerton, and William Fox, summoned for obstructing the footpath by remaining thereon for 20 minutes, on the 24th of February last, plealed not guilty.— P C. Needham, stated he noticed the youths startling out- side the Victoria Buildings, Heisby. for about 2) minutes on the day in question. People could not get into the slitsp.-e Bandies and Bgerton admitted standing there. Fox stated be was not on the footpath only for the purpese of looking into the window of the shop. tie went into the shop and got what he wanted.—The Chairman said it was rather hard on the lads, but the obstruction had to be stopped.---Fox replied ttrit the foOtpath Was not much used by the public.—Yined 4s ed costs. A INCAPiCyFITED tilitta.—GeortZe TWeedale, green• grocer, Heisby, was summoned for being drunk whilst in charge o a horse and cart at Helaby on the 19th instant.— Defendant pleaded not guilty. - Constable Needham said he was out duty in Robin Hood-lane, on the date in question, when he found the defendant lying on the side of the road. The horse and trap was some distance off. One shaft of the cart was brokeii• Neer the defendant there was a bottle half full of whisky. Defendant had been refused drink at two public-houses that Leonel:lg. Defendant at the time the constable fo ine him was quite unable to take care of his horse and cart, not from the effects of the fail, but from the effects of drink. Witness assisted by another young man took the defendant home.—Defendant commenced to explain that his stupidity was Cue to the accident to the pony and trap in which he got a severe blow. He admitted, however, that he had been refused drink at the two public-houses.— The Bench considteed that was sufficiently corroborative of the officer's evideeee. and fined defendant lOs and 9s 6d costs or 14 days. A BRICKLAYER FINED FOR BID LANGUAGE,—SamaeI Brown, jun., of Ship-street, Frodsham, a bricklayer, e was summoned for using profane language within hearing of the public, at Frodsham, on the 11th inet.—Defendant pleaded utility, and the case having been stated by P.C. Dioleen, a fine of 5s and 8s 6d costs was inflicted. A CONRCIENTIoUB OKTACToR REFEMED.—WiIIiam Davies, of rielsby made application for a certificate of exemption' from vaccination for his child.— Che Chairman : Is there anything the matter with the child at the present time.— Applicant: Nothing at all I don't want it vaccinated at all. A nephew of mine was vaccinated and soon afterwards lumps broke out on its body. The child has been bothered ever since by that ailment. The Chairman: You cannot say that that is a reason.—The Clerk observed that the ap- piicant would have to satisfy the court the objection was a conscientious one.—The Chairman remarked that they did not think Mr. Davies had proved a conscientious objection, and refused to grant the certificate. • INDECENT BEICANIOUR.—WiIIiam loud, labourer, Main- street, Frodsham. was summoned for indecently exposing himself with intent to insult a female at Frodsham, on the 3rd of March last.—Mr. Knowles. Widnes, defended.—De- fondant was fined 10s and 16s 6d costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>ROMANCE OF LONDON LIFE.</head>
        <p>A ROMANCE OF LONDON LIFE.</p>
        <p>Of all the romances in reel life th: strange adveritures of a young lady of Msida Vale, London (reeentiy pub- lished in the" Weekiy D:spatcti"), may be placed on re- cord among the most remarkable. Eighteen years of ege, mest prepossessing in appeara.ace, an wile the brightest prospects in life, Miss Charlotte Bellamy has nevertheles, found herself situated in a most serious pre- dicament. To the preisman who calltd at her home, 233, Elgin- avenue, Maida Vale, she stated, with charmiag frank- ness : "I am just eighteen, arid, beyond a few weeks that I went to business, have been at borne au imy life. For years I did n:t enjoy what might be cal ted eood health,and three years age I had to undergo an operatioa at the Midalesex Hospital. I did not get well for some time, and to occupy my mind I obtained a si,eration in a City show-room. But even this slight labour was too much, and I had to give it up. A;1 I felt able to de was to lie huddled up on a couch. I was quite anemic (bloodless), and my eses were bloods-tot. Food was horrible to think of, every meal lay iike a great weight on nay chest for hours. My rsst at night waa disturbrd by dreadful dreams. I could see dectore with herrible long knives hovering about nay bedside. I was more like a corpse than a uerson alive. Frequently I woke up covered with perspiration. At last I became so ill that I went to another hospital at Hempstead, then to a con- valescent home; but wire' I returned home I was very little better ; my eyes were • glassy ' my lips black, and then a greenish-yellow, and I only weigned just under 7 stone. "Then I was sent to Lowestoft. On the day I left I was in a dead faint for close upon an hour. At that time we had a trained nurse, and she said to my mother, I don't think your daughter will ever come back alive.' Mother said, 'Don't say that; it does seem hard to say good-bye for ever.' Father nes since told me that he did not expect to see me alive again I grew worse at Lowestoft, and asked to be brought back home again. i'he week that I returned father reed about a young woman who had been cured of antemia,and mother said, 'Why, she seems to have been ill exactly like Charlotte, and if Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale pa )ple have done her so much good, suppose we try a box ? ' The very next day I cammencrd to take Dr. Williams' pink pills, and in less than a week I found an improvement. First I began to eat better, and I did not feel s dietressed alter wards ; then I began to be able to walk :anoet arid could go up and downstairs. I have centinued to im- prove,so that I am now not only strong,but I an putting !on flesh. All this has happened in two months, and thankful I am that I took Dr. Williams' pink pills." Mr. and Mrs. Bellamy remarked at the close, " Every word is true. Dr. Williams' pink pills have saved her life, and snatched her from the grate." The double function of our blood ie to cleanse and feed the body. Absorbing noarishment ficen food through the walls of the sto nach, the blcod carries this nourishment to every part of the system. At the came time it carries off impurities. Lack of hloorl i 3 th diteet cause of physical and functi-mal weaknese, neodecees, Ipalpitation at the heart, and painful breathleeeness after exertion, backaches, cold feet, and the aeneral wretched- ' ness of " delicate" people. The special troubles of gi cls in their derelopment nearly always arise from aemeia or " too little blood " ; and Dr. Williams' piek pills, which directly increase the supply and the richness of the blood, cure all the consequences of that lack in a manner often spoken of as miraculous. They atse cure paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rhournats:n, sciatica, and nervous disorders, They are genuine (oily with the full nameO.D:. Williams', pink pills for pale people, arid are tepid by chemists, and by Dr: Williams' Medicine Corn- racy, dolborn Viaduct, London, at two ehiliines and I ; ninepence a box, or six boxes for tnirteen aid nine. Pills sold loose, or from glees jars, are not Dr. Williams' pink pills ; accept them only in the pink eloeed wrepper as above described.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>ALARMING EXPLOSION AT</head>
        <p>ALARMING EXPLOSION AT</p>
        <p>WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Last Friday evening an alarrning explosion of gas cceurred at the Bridge Hotel, Widnes. The house bas been undergoing renovation, and the ceiling of the bar having been completed, a gas fitter and plumber, named Sebastian Keoricla, residing at 78, Vietoris-road. came to connect the pendant. The gas pipe must have been broken, for when tbe connection had been ex:•cuted, it was found that there was no gas. Mr. Hemingway's workmen stripped the ceiling for a Ginn area, The plumber endeavoured to find the spot from whenee the gas was leaking, but failed. He then naed his blow pipe,when immediately the gas,which must ha' passed from beam to beam and become compressed in the top of the rcom exploded. The plaster on the ceiling was torn off, and Kenrick received severe burns on the face, neck, and hands. Be was conveyed hame, and sub- sequently taken to the Accident FlesOtal, where his injuries were attended to by Dr. Hutchinson. Ho is now fortunately, making a satisfactory progress. Beyoni tearing away the plaster on the ceiling, the explosion caused no further damage. -</p>
        <p>'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds, Asthma, and Bronchitis Of Chenaists only.—Advt.</p>
