      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Warrington Annihilate Goole.</head>
        <p>Warrington Annihilate Goole.</p>
        <p>Fish: 7 Goals, 5 Tries.</p>
        <p>Warrington had an easier cesk than they expected in the second round of the Cup Competitien, for the Goole representatives peoved to be third-raters and were easily vanquished. They came with some hopes of winniug, and certainly they looked a sturdy promising lot of lads when they stepped on the field. But their Retie .s of football were only crude, and Warrington had no diffi- culty in piling on points. The weateer was cool and dry, and the ground in excellent order tor last play. Ahoet 3,000 spectators assembled to witoess the match, and they hed an enjeyable time, while the scoring lasted Mr. T. 8. Eagland (Huddersfield) was the refe.ree, an the men in his charge were as follows:— Warrington : Hallam, full back, Fish, Isher wood, Heckenhall, and Moores, threequerters; Bate and Du:k- worth, half backs; Taylor, Boardman, Swift, Mere- weather, Eden, •Scholtze, Autrotus and Morrison forwards. Gone, F Campsall, full back; Earnehaw, %Void, Scram, and G Canspeell threequarters ; J Bradley, and Greensit, half acks ; Leggett, Jackson, Ayre, Alcoek, Morton, Pearson, Shaw, and II Bradley, forcoarch. Taylor started the game, kickiog off in the face of a fairly strong breeze. Goole relearned ti touch and after a few unimportant. ex.:images, Warrington got down to the Goole end. Filth and Moorea were conspicuous by good play down the wings, the latter nearly scoring. Duckworth mulled, and Jackson lrici Earnehew led a rush to the home end where Hallam fell a victim to numbers. So far play had been fairly even, but War- rington were plainly the smarter lot. Isherwood and Fish combined.beautifully, and the latter after a good run kicked across for Eden who knoc'-ed forward under the posts. Warrington had now in seed quite enough chances and there was more than a little satisfaction felt whoa Fish dodged dawn the left and passed judicioesly to E lea, who scored near the corner. Fish took the kick and landed a goal from a difficult position. Warrington passed splendidly and after one or two failures to pierce the defence, Fish received from Isherweode and raced behind the posts, afterwards aiding another goal. At the interval, Warringtoa led by ten points to nil. Playing with the wind in the secoud half the hemostats were irresistible, Fish again taking up the tale of woe by running in from Eden's pass. Again he goaled. Isherwood was the next scorer, Fish kicking a fourth goal and raising the total to 20 points. Al. pack in midfield was won by Warrington, end Morrisoa dashed in under the posts, Fish landing his fifth cause-. calve goal. He failed a minute later to improve on a try:Ly;Moores,but scoring himself behind the posts after quick passing, he again converted. Fish was here there and everywhere, and scored twice more, from passes by Taylor and Bate. Rockenhall also scored, Fish kicking a goal, and when the whisrle sounded, the score read : Warrington, 7 goals, 10 tries (44 points) ; Goole, nil. Fish was the hero of the day and scored 7 goals, 5 tries, or 29 points—a wonderful individual parforrnance. Runcorn too Good for Maryport.</p>
        <p>Maryport's visit to Runcorn, on Saturday, was not looked forward to with any great excitement, and but few spectators ventured out in the cold bleak weather prevailing to see the encounter. There was, however, an apt) eciatively larger crowd than the previous Satur- day, due no doubt to anticipation of a little better game. Well, in this respect the additional few were not dis- appointed, for the game was decidedly better. At first l!etaryport pressed ; their tackling being keen and heavy. But it was not long before Runcorn, through the instru- mentality of Farm,r, and Butterworth, gave Maryport a warmer handful than they cared about. Charlie Rutterworth being nicely fed by his brother should have secured a try. Yet he did not, and some say it was because his hand was hurt a few seconds before. The fact remains, however, that a try went b3gging,. Shortly after J Butterworth marked, and got hurt. From a difficult position Farmer hazarded a kick, and succeeded grandly. Marypeit's kicking and fielding now stood them in good stead. Their pack too heeled out 19 times,to Runcorn's ouce,and for a considerable time they made all the play. Still notwithstanding all this, Run- corn pressed. and from a penalty Boughton put ov3r a second goal. At half time Runcorn were 4 points, to nil, Upon recearnencing Runcorn ran off in fine style, and established themselves firmly in Marypert'a half. Timone', however, made several good efforts to rals3 the seige, but failed As the result of beautiful passing J Butterworth got over and Faulkner landed a third goal. Then follow ed a sue- cessien Df attempts on the part of Runcorn to enhance their score, and several chances were missed. Though Maryport were far oat-classed, but one other try was secured by C Butterworth. As this latter point was not improved, the final read : Runcorn, 3 goals, 2 tries (12 points) ; to Maryport nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>THIRD ROUND.</head>
        <p>THIRD ROUND.</p>
        <p>Officials for To-Day's Matches.</p>
        <p>The officials for Saturda7's cup ties were appointed on Monday evening in Manchester as follows : Leeds Parish Church v Batley. Referoe, F Kennedy (Broughton Rangers) ; touch judges, J Oakland (Out- wood Church) and J fl Pickles (Leeds). Salford v Huddersfield : Referee, W H Bairstow (Hunslet) ; touch judges, J H Houghton (St. Helens) and J Uuderwood (Wigan). Bramley v Widnes. Referee, J Bruckshaw (Stock- port); touch judges, P F Farrar (Halifax) and H Sewell (Leeds). Swinton ir Olciham. Referee, F Renton ( aunslet) ; touch judges, E Holmes (Rochd tie Hornets) and G Jones (Salford). Stockport v Radcliffe. Referee S Williams (Salford) ; touch judges, J M‘Gowan (Brouglaton Rangers) and J E Newton (Altrineharn). Bradford v Runcorn. Referee, W H M'Cutchloti (Old- ham) ; touch judges C A Brewer (Hull) and A Brou n (Brighouse Rangers). Rochdale Hornets v Warrington. Referee, J H Smith (Widle) ; touch judges,J Pries:ley (Broughton Rangers) and P ,Inghana (Oldham). Broughton Rangers v Wakefield Triaity. Referee, T H Marshall (Bradford) ; touch judges. R Abbey (Stockplrt) and R H NwtAll (Rochdale Hornets).</p>
        <p>Runcorn Inter Works Competition. IX A meeting of the committee„ appointed to woilic the competition, was held, on Wednesday evening, the princirril bu4ine3s being the draw for the first round which resulted as follows : April 2. Hazlehurst v Timmins. • „ 4.. White Lead v Shop Assistants. „ 7. Runziorn Docks v Castner Kellner. „ 9. Al3els v Highfield. „ 11. Wigg v Weston. „ 13. Wiggs Manure v Spinch Yard. Australin Alum a bye. It was arranged to hold a general meeting of all the works interested, on Monday evening to decide several matters of interest. The Assistant Secretary Mr. Alfred Abel, 22. Byron-street, was appointed t 3 take charge of the competition, and all communications and correspon- dence must ba addr(ssed to4him.</p>
        <p>Other Matches. Oldham " A " V. Fletcher Russell's.</p>
        <p>At Oldham on Saturday. The home team began to pres 3 but soon the visitors liveried up, and Knight, the full back, dropped a goal. Half-time : Fletcher Russell's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>GRAPPEN HALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPEN HALL.</p>
        <p>THEFYS BY A LODGER.—Eliztbath McMullan, a res- pectatity-dres3ed el tirly woman, pleady guilty at the Daresbury Petty Sesiireas on Tuesday to stealing' a black cloth cape, and a gold ring, of the value of £l. 1.25., the property of Margaret Grimes, married woman living at Grappenhall. SI:13 had been lodging with the prosecutor but decamped with the articles roentioued. Information 1913 given to the police, and it was found that they had been pawned, one at Staforth and the other at Warring- ton.—The prisoner,who had only been recently liberated from Walton Gaol, was s-nat t priseu for a month with hard labour,</p>
        <p>Meeting of the Parish Council. A meeting ot the Grappenhall Pa.rista Council was held in the schoolroom on Tuesday evening, Mr. J. Charlton Parr presiding. There were also present:—.Yleeisrs Lister- Kaye, Jenkins. Boardman, Smith, Bradtord, Warburton, Edison, Chamberlain. L )mas and VVo4dcock (clerk).</p>
        <p>THE MINUTES OF THE LAST MEETING.- ME. JENKINS OBJECTS. The Clerk road the mioutes ot the last meeting. Mr. Jenkins : Befwe the minutes are signed I should</p>
        <p>like to refer to the matter wnich cropped up duriag the reading of the minutes at out last meeting. Tne Ohairman said that the coufirmation of the minutes would not interfere with anything that Mr Jen- kios wished to speak about afterwards. The question merely was whether the minutes were a correct report of what took place at the last meeting. Mr. Jenkins : My argument is that they should not be signtd because they are not entered accordiag to the Act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Trio Chairman: Thetis a fundament4l objection.</head>
        <p>Trio Chairman: Thetis a fundament4l objection.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins: Referring to ttae encerizig of the minutea in section 3 you will find an entty to this effect: " The names of the members present at any meeting of the Parish Council as well as of those voting on each question on which a division is taken shall be recorded sa as to show whether each vote given was for or against the question." There are two resolutions simply givir.g the names of the nriver and seconder. Nothing is said as to how the other members voted. The Ciaairman : I think it cAll be taken for granted _ _</p>
        <p>that they were passed without dissension. Mr. Jenkins : It appears to me that ,he Act does not wish us to take anything for granted. In case there should be any cause to refer to the ininut.,s in tho future we shosld want to see how everyone vited oa the mat :er in question. The Chairman : I don't read it so. If there were any objections and a division was taken I think the Act means th at tho nanyli should be entered but if there is no division there can be no object in taking the names. Mr. Jenkins If there was an addition to the effect</p>
        <p>that it was carried unanimously we should then-. la The Chairman did not agree with that at all. If the motion was carried without dissention they could con- clude it was cleriod untnimliply. If it WAS carriel tu</p>
        <p>_ thought it would be superfluous to put the names down. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkini said that the minutes of the previous meting wPre not entered as miuut 'S but sintply aa re- solved so and so.</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman did not think it WAS co,lsistent to open that matter as the minutes were passed and signed.</p>
        <p>Mr. JeGkini L soetni to av that it is a necesaity</p>
        <p>that we striuld keep the tlOO3 to the form the Act requires bacausa the County C9tlCCil if they discharge their duty should visit us pp,.rildically and see net only our beoks but whatever documents we have in our possessioa. The Act sass" Every C iunty Council shall from tune to time inquire into tho manner in which the public books, writings, papers and documents under the control of the Parish Council or parish meeting,are with a view to tke proper preservat,on thereof and titial make such orders as they think necessary for such pre- servation and those or lets nall be complied with by the Parish Council or parish meeting," rho Chairman : It is hartry for 123 t) call the County Council to order I think. If the 7 are not attending to their duties it is hardly tor us to say. They are not interfering with us. Mr. Jenkins: I simply read the Act to show what they have the power to do and should they inspect the books and find them kept irregularly someone will receive the</p>
        <p>blame. The Chairman : I lave 51. Th,y will certainly deeerve i:, but I don't feel myself we are at all open to it. I think that the miuutas which have just be read out seem to be perfectly in order. Mr. Jenkins:But you may remember that I objected to the minutes at the 14st meeting. They were signed then and as I said those minutes did not Lven show toe mover or seconder. The Chairman : That is past we.d gOl,. They have b3eo confirmed and signed by the Chairman. Any objections you had to make you should make at the time. I move that the minutes be confirmed. Mr. Boardman seconded and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>GdAPPENHALL PARISH PROPERTY.</head>
        <p>GdAPPENHALL PARISH PROPERTY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that in accordance wile the minutes passed at the last meeting he visited Runcorn and inspectA the books of the Rural Council for the last three or lour years. lie found that everything was in perfect order. He askni Mr. Ashton ho n' it was that, they could not have an annual statement and he said that the Council was now publishing a book showing the annual exoenditure. Re pointed out the page which contained the itf m for Grappenhall of 193 for each haif year which was duly credited, Mr. Jenkins asked the Clerk to read the previous cor- respondence which had taken place with regard to the parish property. Mr. Wooocock read the fllewin letter which he wrAe to the Rnncern Council in July:—"At our Parish Council meeting, held June 26, 1899, I was direc- ted to write and ask you to make arraEgements for the transfer to the Parish Council of the consolidated stock that brings in 383 per year in favour of the township, and which is paid to the Rural District Council. Our Parish Council think that it ought to be paid to them, and that also is the opinion of Mr. H. J. Lina'ker, late clerk to the late Daresbury Highway Board, as you will find by reference to his letter book of 13th May, 1895." He received the following communication in reply :, " Grappenhall Parish property. Dear Sir, I laid your letter of the 17th inst before the District Council at their meeting yesterday when I was instructed to inform you that the Council will not stand in the way of the Parish Council of Grappenhall proving their right to the con- solidated Stock standing in the name of that parish in the books of the District Council. Upon the duties of the late Daresbury Highway Board being transferred to the District Council, the Council proved to the satisfaction of the Bank of England that they were entitled to have the stock transferred to their name as the iiIICC?SSMS of the Highway Board and it will now be necessary tor the Parish Council to satisfy them that it should be trans- ferred to that Council. I may say that the quarterly dividends are always credited to the parish of Grappen- hall. Yours truly. GEORGE F. ASHTON " gaMr. Jenkins : Is not the parish quite as capable of managing the money as the Runcorn District Council. The Chairman agreed that it _would be more desirable to have it in the hands of the Parish Council. The only question was whether it was worth while taking pro- ceedings for a sum of that amount. he did not know what grounds the Rural Council had for declining te hand it over to them. Mr. Jenkins: But they do not decline. The Clerk They say we shall first have to prove our ; right. Mr. Jenkins : That would los a simple matter should think. The Clerk: Mr. Ashton told me it would not be as simple a matter as we thought. ; The Chairman : It would involve considerable legal expewe. After considerable discussion, Mr. Jenkins moved that the considerationlof the mat- ter be adjourned to the next meeting. , Mr. Smith qeconded and the motion was agreed ti. THE SMELL FROM THE SHIP CANAL.—" POISONED ANNUALLY."</p>
        <p>The Chairman referred to the state of the Manchester Ship Canal. He said that they were poisoned annually by the fearful stench arising from the water. He thought they should protest and endeavour to have the nuisance attended to. The matter was being taken up in the county and they should strengthen the halids of those who were petitioning. Mr. .I,,nkins prop ,sed "That this council earnestly petition the County Council Iso take steps to abate the excessive obnoxious smelts arising from the Manchester Ship Canal, which are most disagreeable and injurious to health." Mr. Smith seconded the resolution which was carried I unanimously.</p>
        <p>CHAIRMAN THANKED. This being the final meeting of till year Mr. Jenkins moved that the best thanks of the Council- be given to Mr. Parr for the just and pleasant manner in which he had presided over the Council for the past twelve months. Mr. Boardman seconded and the motion was unanim- ously agreed to. Mr. Parr briefly returned thanks and the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>PADdATE.</head>
        <p>PADdATE.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL GATIIERING.—A social evening was held in the Wesleyan Sunday School On Monday evGning in con- nection with the Padgate branch of the Wesley Guild. The programme opened with the song "Welcome, pretty flower," by Miss Lily Mason, who also contributed "Butterflies," both of which were loudly applauded. The mandoline and pianoforte dufts, by the Misses Glare, were well received, all their items being encored. Mrs. James Bennett (soprano) highly delighted the audience with her two conga " La Serenata " (Italian) and " To-morrow will do." for the latter song realizing a well-earned encore. Mr. E Lleyoellyn Pearce gave "Good-night, beloved "and "The Soldwrs ot the Queen" in a!manner which pleased the audience.and in re,pons to an encore he was equally successful. Dr. James Bennett's patriotic songs," Kruger's Broom " (the com- position of the vocalist) and" John Bull," were greatly appreciated. The Misses Gandy and Dr. Beunett received their share of applause for their violins and pianoforte trio, Miss a Bennett (Lynclh,..rst) acted as accompanist, and Mr. James Gandy presided. During the evening refreshments, supplied through the kindness of the ladies, were provided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. LANCASHIRE LEAGUE. Earlestown v. Crewe Alexandra. Earlestown Still on the Downgrade.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. LANCASHIRE LEAGUE. Earlestown v. Crewe Alexandra. Earlestown Still on the Downgrade.</p>
        <p>Crewe Aleatendra visited the Meanes enclosure on Saturday and eu- ior the wintry ceaditions which pre- vailed. there is no doubt there wuietd have been a capital attendance. However ouly about 500 people wen herly enough to brave a eearceiing east wind and the coffers of th local club suffered aecordingly. As it happened tiose enthusiasts who stayed at home did not miss much, as taken all round, the f eethall shown by both teams was ef a very peor charecter, and as will be seen by the prolific scoring wnich treik piace,the defence in each case was very shaky. Both teams were fully re- presented, although Hampaoa was absent from the home side, his place being tided by E ernondmon, who played for Eerleiitown teree years age ated has not since figured on the Mesnes. A strong wind was blowing across the ground and tee visitors tnern ore derived no levee&amp; from the winning of the toss, except the slope. which they de- i! cided to take advantage of Ellis set tee bail in motion correct to time and the visitors forwards immediately got to werk, Lockett dribbling the ball finely down the wisg and winding up with a pretty centre which Chew saved at the expense of a corner. Ibis was spits:welly placed and Hales spoiled a fine opening by needing behind. Toe ensuing goal kick aid not relieve the pressure on the home goal, as the leather was retureed in a trice. Ihe visitors forwards fairly bewildered the noine defenders by their tricky play in ' the goal m Juth and a transfer from tee right wing, left Lockett with a perfect; opening watch the speedy winger utterly failed to utilise, as he landed the hall yards wide, perhaps n one being more surprised at his wretched eff .irt than the p;aVer /nuns Af. Tee same player soon afterwards agate obtained possession and crossed to Ashworth. who in turn transferred to Hales. The inside man let fly for all he was worth but luckily for the homesters, the ball graz.d the crossbar—a very near shave indeed. Pretty passieg between Eilis and White- house changed the scene of opereedons, and after trick- iog the half Lantt, the later leveled to Settle who easily eluded the backs and banged the leadier luta the net, the custodian falling in a futile attempt to save his charge. Tillie suecee3 -edf en cane after 20 tninute3 play, served te enliven the proceedings, and the ensuing ex- clianees were fest and exciting. The homesters were seen again triubling the Crewe defence but weak shoot- ing spoiled numerous chances. The visiters made Aran- ison3 efforts to get on level terms and the equaliser was not lung in conning. The whole of the forwards perecipated in a nice passing movement, and during an exciting melos in the go deneuch Brookfield banged the ball through a sea of legs into the corner of the net, Chew ha. wing no pessible chance of saving. Immediately oil the re-stert [Leen dribbled the ball down the wing arid heat Chew for tee fe cella time, both coats cemiug as the result of loose play on the part of the Earlestown backs. Midfield play followed until nearing the interval hen BroulifieA dribbled past Cottarn and scored the ryigitora' taird goat,this melee the state of the game when toe whistle neew. The resainpteon saw the hemester3 take up tile running, but Eemendsen overran the ball and the visitors were at the odaer end in a twinkling ; Hales ollernattly FICO. ing No. 4.. A few minutes after- wards Ellis scored for Earlestewn with a beautiful sliest which completely beat Coventry. Before tile finish Elesbaua scered a fiite,ene Ashworth a s ith goal for the visi:ora, wile eventually ran out easy winners by six goals to ten The game was pleesautly contested throughout, although just before toe interval Johnson , was kicked in the stoonech and had to retire. He came on again soon after the resumption, but he was of very little use. Notwithstanding the entreaties 4 his corn- rides—who coula sea he was not flit to play, to go off the field, the plucky little fellow refused to budge, and played the rest of the game out. The play never reached a very high order but this may be put down to the fact that the homestere were off colour and to the puzzling vaind which prevailed. The Alexandra were easily the better team aad especially so as regarded the attack. Their forwards were quick on the ball, passed accurately aed with judgment and never lost a chance of having a pop at goal when a favourable oeportuaity presented itself. This mere particularly applied to the first half, for after, both teams fell away somewhat. Lockett and Hales were the most effective wing couple on the field, the former dribbling and centring very accurately aed the latter shooting with rare judgment and precision. Ashworth kept his wings nicely in touch, and although Hesham and Brookfield occasionally put in seine god work it must be remem- bered that for three fourths of the game owing to the injury to Johnson, they were allowed more scope than weuld otherwise have been the case. The Crewe halves , were a very hardeworking trio and time after time they broke up the combination of the home forwards. the backs were very safe and both played a clean, sound game whilst the custodian could not be blamed for either of the two goals scared past him. The home for- wards did not seem to be able te get going, and taken as a whole their attacks were very feeble. Edmondson by no means created a coed impression and if he would impart a little more dash into his play he would come off a great deal better than he did on Saturday. The half-backs were only moderate,Pennington,and Johnson, up to the time of his retirement, being the pick. The backs played a miserable game and seemed utterly un- able to cope with the dashes of the Crewe forwards. Time after time Heyea missed his kick and let in the visitors left wing pair and but for the excellent exhibi- tion of goal-keepiug .given by Chew, the score would have been much heavier than it was. Altogether the show given by the Locals was disappointing in the extreme and if they are to occupy a decent position in ,he League taele,they will have to play different football ;ban they played against Crewe on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WHERE THE MONEY COMES</head>
        <p>WHERE THE MONEY COMES</p>
        <p>FROM.</p>
        <p>"There would seem," says the London "Daily Chronicle of September 22, 1899, "to be no diminu- tion in the popularity of proprietary or so-called patent medicinea."</p>
        <p>Of course not. Why shou Id there be? There is no diminution in the popularity of bread and cheese, and many of these "so-called patient medicines" are next door, in usefulness, to those well-known brands of victuals.</p>
        <p>The people find out what is good for their pains and ailments and stick to it, whether Liberals or Conserva- tives are running the Government.</p>
        <p>And, speaking of the Government, we come upon a point that will interest those who wonder where the money comes from to grease the machinery and amp it going.</p>
        <p>"In th last financial; year," :,the ,';"Chronicle" con- tinues, "according to the Inland Revenue accounts, a duty of lid was paid on 27,856.686 boxes or pacilages of medicine, a duty of .3d on 5,076,805, a duty of 61 on 856,057, a duty of is on 108,280, a duty of 21 on 11,309, and of 3s on 5,703. Thus the Government stamp on 33,914,840 parcels yielded to the Nt.tional Exchequer the handsome sum of £266,572 14i 11d. Clearly the State derives some benefit: from patent medicines, whatever may be the fate of the individual purchasers."</p>
        <p>To be sure it does, and M these expensive times we hope the State will show itself deeply thankful. The proportion of this" handsome sum "that is contributed by Mother Seigel's Syrup is a matter of record, and the figure 3 which express it have been growing and swelling for the past thirty years, and will keep on doing so for twice as many more. _ . _</p>
        <p>There will be no diminution ; no fear. If the Ex- chequer doesn'c go bankrupt until Mother Seigel fails to pay her share of the taxes, Jrohn Bull and his Island will remain snug and sound financially until the day after we have paid the National Debt.</p>
        <p>For a medicine—patent or no patect--that multi- plies the number of sound stomachs and healthy livers, and, as a consequence, the number of clear brains and cheery tempers, does a public service so great and vital that the British !'peeple are not likely to let it be- come a back number in a isurry.</p>
        <p>Health (by the help of Mother SAgel) to our friends of the " Chronicle " and all the other printed news- mongers. Let theim not worry about the " fate of the individual purchasers "of this solid old remedy. They are doing nicely, thank you, and will come up smiling whenever there is work to be done, songs to be sung, or good dinners to he eaten.</p>
      </div>
