      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>MRS. E. HAWORTH.</head>
        <p>MRS. E. HAWORTH.</p>
        <p>(From a photograph.)</p>
        <p>Imagine buying plain cod-liver oil to take as a medicine, then finding you cannot take it,and finally rubbing it into your boots as a preservative of leather! Such was the experience of a lady in Man- chester. and womav add that in doing this she went back about 200 years to one of the first purposes for which cod-liver oil was used. viz., to soften leather.</p>
        <p>But time changes many things, and it has aunty changed the method of obtaining the medicinal properties of cld-liver oil, without its bad taste or its tax upon digestion. The lady herself ad-ptcd this modern improvement upon plain oil, and tt lls of its satisfactory results in the following letter :</p>
        <p>"32, Plymouth Avenue, " Longsight. Manche;ter, "March 16th, 1899.</p>
        <p>" Gentlemen,—Some little time ago my health was very unsatisfactory. I took Lnics, but they did not seem to do me much good. I was told I must take cod-liver oil. I bought a bottle and tried to take it, but I realty could not manage it. It was simply horrid, so I finished up by rubbing it into my boots as a preservative, and the rvmainder is still On the shelf. I had often thought I w( uld like to try Scott's Ernulsion,which I had seen advertised so often. Ooe day I was looking through a</p>
        <p>local paper, when I saw that the proprietors Sc,At's Erau!sion would give a .:ample bottle to all who sent for one, so I thought, Well, I shall not lose anything by sending,' and I did so with thO most happy results. I cannot praise it too higkl! I Faye bad several bottles, and I f.el wonderful'," I etter in every way. I recommend it to all mi frends. One young lady has eeen taking it for mor,ths, and feels greatly benefited by its use. .11.! any future time when I feel at all run down shall begin anther course of Scott's Emulsion, N' C4llAs I have evvry confidence that after taking few bottes I shall fel quite healthy again. Wish' ing you every success,—Yours very truly, (Signed) " (Mrs.) E. HAWORTH." Besides being easy to take Scott's Emulsion 10 actually more beneficial than plain cod-liver oil. The ease with which it is digested results in the absorr tion of cc re oil in proportion to the amount taken. and the hypophoephites and glycerine in Scott'S Emulsion are a further advantage. You can obtain a sample of Scort's Emulsion by sceding threepence to cover postage to Scott and Bovine, Limited, Manufacturing Chemists, 95, Great Saffron Hill, Lowlen, E.C., and mentioning this paper. Alt chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
        <p>week. He, however, kept selling things and " ran bile; of in trouble morn an,-1 more" and was not able„A draw himself out of it. The pictares in question al' • h. not cost £5 but or,ly £2 103, That was the first tun ,rne had been belore th's Court and he asked the Bench</p>
        <p>mercy. Mr. Browne: Mr. Paikin paid him 223 61 per 01f13/4..4 and has lent him money from time to time to help EP' out of difficulties. oil Mr. Roberts : We have a complete answer to all t charges. We can show you that the prisoner has De7r,,, mployed Paikin to take these things round. ovi are ti.e public to distinguish between an honest sale tilos a dishonest one. They were paid for at good priors. la pictures only cost £2 10 01 and tot £5. I have al° lit of g•Jod prices. M-. Faircltyugh : Didn't he know he was in the Or ploy of Paikin. Mr. R itwrts : Yes sir. dfal Mr. Fairclough : It seems to me that Gibbon - quit,'as rPprehel-,sible as this man. Mr. Rober's : The man was employed to sell goodS.ol Mr. Fairclougii : Mr. Gibbon must have known ti 3 the man was not sent to sell to second hand shops. it Mr. Gibbon was called, and aske by the Chairrloo he bad advanced money before haad ,to the prisoner ' any case. Mr. Gibbon: Never a halfpenny. 110 The Chairman: Didn't it occur to you, knowing t. 4 he was in the employ of Mr. Paikin, that it was a vti curious proceeding that this man should 130,bringl" goods to the extent he did to you. Mr. Gibbon: Be told me that Mr. Paikin had boogli a big bankruptcy stock and could sell cheaper than I cow.</p>
        <p>buy elsewhPre. •Mr. Rob rts : I only- wish the prosecution 12015 chi led my client. He could have answered these shag'. completely. 1110 The prl4oner : Mrs. Gibbon advanced me 10 Saturday for which they have not had any goods st I have had money advanced about twenty timer. iry Mr. Fairclough : I think Mr. Gibbon is very OP henslb!e. 00°</p>
        <p>Mr. Roberts : I quite agree. He should have greater prenaurion. k itt Mr. Fairelough : In the interest of justice I thin— oneht to alter the manner of doing business. _</p>
        <p>-- - Mr. Roberts: I hope it will boa lesson to him. 400 Mr. Paircloueh said that in a case of that descrilwos Gibbon should have gone directly to Mr. Paikin nog 4-;ked him if he had allowed the prisoner to melt things. • tiO Mr. Glbbon said the goods were all exposed 10 rum. reso After consulting with his colleagues, the Chair-.-di RAW the Bench took a very serious view of the 0.,701 They ha 3 been told that that was the third or foll time that Mr. Paikin had been robbed in that waY „of , people in his employ, and it seemed as if it was bee" go log scsematic. They had come to the conclusion se, send the prisoner to gaol for three months in each ntielt nine months in all. They also wished to express t;po opinion with regard to Mr. Gibbon. They though4ol had net taken the care he should have done to 'ONO' he was not receiving stolen goods. He might easily Pt eir ascertained whether it was a bin-la-fide sale or not, bOlll did not seem to h4ve taken precautions. They W- di4allow his expenses. co, Of On hearing the sentence prisoner exclaimed " God °IS Mr. Browne applied for an order for the return ofB, , whole of the goods to Mr. Paikiu which was grsote yen On behalf of Mr. Pikiu, Mr. B„wee thlool Superintendent Monaehau. Mr. Paikin would ton hire che mot of the robbery if the Superintendent not to; him that he keew where the goods were. The Chairman : He's a sharp man on felony. ft. Mr. Browne : He eeems to be able to smell a tbetog The Chairman : I always said he could smell _.ne goods, and his genial face always disartns Pr' (L e.e,hter).</p>
