      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EXTENSIVE ROBBERIES.</head>
        <p>EXTENSIVE ROBBERIES.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Canvasser Smartly Punished.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Saturday, before Messrs. T. Birched (in the chair), R. 'l'. Feircleugh and J. W. Hayward, Barnard Cohen, a canvasser of 22, Barry-street, was charged on remand with stealine sometime between the 2nd Februsry and 3rd March 2 overmantels, 2 oil paintings ane 8 pictures of the value of £lO 12s Oct. the property of Charlts Paikin, furniture dealer, of Mersey-street. He pleaded guilty.—He was further charged with stealing between Nevembtr Ist and December 24th, 1899, 2 oil paintings and four pictures. valued at £4 6s OJ, and between the 10th and 17th March, 1900, 13 quilts, 12 sheets, 3 boys suits, 3 table cloths, and 2 shirts value £6, the property of Charles Paikin. He pleaded guilty, to these charges.—Mr. A. Browne prosecuted and Mr. F. R. Roberts appesred on behalf of Mr. Gibbon of Mersey-street, whose name is mentioned below. Mr. Browne, in opening the ease, said that Mr. Paikin was a furniture dealer carrying on business in Mersey-street and since July, 1899 the prisoner had been in his employ as a collector and traveller. In conse- quence of what Superintendent Monaghan heard last Saturday, Mr. Paikin was communicated with, and he visited three houses in Dalton Bank where prisoner alleged he had sold three over-mantels. He found that there was not a single name correct and no overmantels bad been sent to the houses. Superintendent Monaghan knew where the articles were and in consequence of the result of inquiries Mr. Paikin consulted him (Mr. Browne), and a warrant was issued. When prisoner was arrested on Wednesday morning it was believed that only three overmantels were missing,but it was subsequently discovered that the other goods mentioned had been dis- posed of at a "putty shop occupied by a Mr. Gibbon of Mersey-at. At this shop things could be sold and if they had not been disposed of could be get back again by paying an increased price. Gibbon's shop was 150 yards from the shop of the prosecutor and the whole of the articles with the exception of two oil paintings had been disposed of to Mr. Gibbon witnin three weeks. On Thursday, when the prisoner was broneht before the court,he (Mr. Browne) applied for a remand wirh a view to considering whether to preceed against Mr. Gibbon as a receiver. It was a well kno we fact that ii there were no receivers there would be no thieves. It had been decided not to proceed against him for certain reasons although, from what they heard from the prisoner, they had not a shadow of doubt that Mr. Gibbon knew the things had been stolen. Mr. Roberts : It is uot fair to say these things if they are not going to prosecute him. I protest. Mr. Gibbon is an honest tradesman and must be defended. Mr. Browne, continuing said that the prisoner had actually obtained money from Mr. Gibbon in advance, before taking goods. After ha had arrested the prisoner on Wednesday morning, Superintendeat Monaghan saw one of Mr. Gibbon's daughters going along Academy- street with some quilts. He followed her and found she deposited then, in an empty house on Town Hill under a hamper. Mr. Roberts: I think this is very unfair. The Chairman : It seems to me Mr. Browne is b lund to prosecute. Mr. Roberts : Hear, hear. Or say nothing about it sir. Mr. Fairclough : It Seems to me the police ought to nrosezete in a case of this description (to Mr. Browne). If you are not going to prosecute you ought not to say anything about it. The Chairman : It does seem a case for prosecution. Mr. Browne said the prisoner had given every infor- mation since he was locked up. The books were in a lamentable state. There was a tremendous list of sup- wised customers and the prisoner had entered amounts in as though they were paying so much per week. Mr. Paikin put down his loss at £lOO at least. Be had not got all the goods back which were said to Mr. Gibbon. A number of things had been sold and he did not know to whom they were sold. Mr. Feirclough : I presume Mr. Gibbon would take a receipt. Mr. Browne: In this shop they have no receipts at</p>
        <p>all. Mr. Fairclough : It seems to be a lam, n'able way of trading if they have no receipts on record in a trade ot any description. Mr. Browne left the case in the hands of the 13,,neh. He remarked that the prisoner had a wife and two children. The prisoner said that Mr. Paikin paid him £1 a week for five months and out of that he had to pa 2s 6d for hire of furniture. He" got a few shillings behind" and sold a few quilts and sheets. He had to nut them down to different persons and intended to make it up neat</p>
      </div>
