      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>f4l-10 \Jig AR. 1,</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS. General Clements at Faure-</p>
        <p>smith. Telegrams from Lord Roberts. The following has been posted at the War Office: —From Lord Roberts to Secretary of state for War :</p>
        <p>• _ Bloemfontein, Wednesday, 4.5 p.m.—Clements oecupild Fauresmith yesterday without opposition. One 9-pounder and one Martini-Maxim discovered in prospecting shaft of mine, and large quantity of ammunition buried. Arms surrendered gradually and inhabitants settling down. Presid nt Kruger to Command.</p>
        <p>According to a private telegram received at Brussels President Kruger in person will assume the chief command of the Transvaal forces, in suc- cession to the late General Joubert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Boer Fighting Strength.</head>
        <p>The Boer Fighting Strength.</p>
        <p>The latest news is that the Boers have about 40,000 men of all sorts still under arms. Of these 10.000 are in and around the Natal border. The remainder are divided between the Orange Free State and the Transvaal.</p>
        <p>Mafeking. All Well on the 20th.</p>
        <p>It is stated that a private telegram from Mafe- king received on Wednesday, reports the town all right on toe 20th inst. This is later than any news previrin,ly received. When Shall We Reach Pretoria? A Rough Estimate.</p>
        <p>The military critic of the " Westminster Gazette" says it is clear that once the advance of our main body is resumed quite a weak will elapse before our infantry are in sight of the Kroonstad position, as it is hardly possible that so large a force will be able to average more than fifteen miles a day. Even fifteen miles continuous marching day after day is hardly likely to be continued for more than four or five days running, and allowing for halts for rest, for repairs to the rail road, and to permit the slow-moving transport to close up, it is pretty safe to calculate that the big fight at Kroonstad will not begin till at least ten days after the first infantry division has struck its camp and marched off from Bloemfontein. Supposing that next Mon- day s3C3 the Etart of Lord Roberts's army, I would expect to hear that our infantry were before the enemy's lines about April 11, that the Vaalsch had been crossed about the 12th or 13th, and that our march had been resumed about the 15th. In this case the Vaalsch would be reached on or about the 20th or 215t,Johannesburg about the 25th,and on:the 30th the bills encircling Pretoria should come into sight.Of course this rough timetable is purely con- jectural, and is based on the supposition that Lord Roberts will move next Monday. I expect him to move within a few days, and I dismiss as absolutely unreliable the report that he intends visiting Cape- town and deferring the start of the army northward for another month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Local Losses.</head>
        <p>Local Losses.</p>
        <p>Priva,e J. Neilson (3651). of th? lit Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, is reported to have died from sun- stroke 'n Natal on March 23rd ; and Private Canning (5,767), of the same regiment died at sea on the S.S. Tantall in Castle, which was c )nveying the regiment from Darhan to East London in order to reinforce General Gd.tacre in the South of the Or inge Free State. The 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment (Militia) lost its first man on March 19th at Nauwpoort (where it is at present stationed), namely, Private G Travers, who died from dysentery. In another list of nail] aries published on Tuesday morning, the following deatos appear: Naauwpoert.- 3rd Battalion S auth Lancashire Rsgiment, Private W Wood,dysentry. 14ilarch 23. Ist Soutti Lancashire Regi- ment.—Private P Webiter died of :nteric fever at Base Hospital, Maritiburg. Oa Wednesday morning the deaths of Sergeant G Gray (3239), and Private J Q lig ley :(4148), of the lit South Lancashire Regiment at Maritzourg, from enteric fever on March 26th were reported.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Warrington Ambulance Man Dies</head>
        <p>A Warrington Ambulance Man Dies</p>
        <p>at Capetown. We I.,eply regret to hear of the death of Private (1.48) J W Malkin, ot the Warrington C3rp4 of the St. John Ambulance Brigade which took pace at Capetown on March 19. A. The information reached Warringten on Se,turdaY, when Captain Waring, the chief superintend- ent of the corps, received the following letter from the Brigade Chief Superintenielt —" Brigade 2f ead Quarters. St. John's-gate, Clerkenwell, Londen, E.O March 23rd. Dear Captain Waring,—l deeply regret to inform you that Private ,J W Malkin of your corps died on the 19th lust at Capetown of enteric fever. The Commissioner will send on the official letter, but as one who knowi and takes an interest in the Warrington Corps, I want to ask you to expreas to his relatives my sincere sympathy with them in this their deep hour of trouble. Poor boy. He died in carrying out his voluntary duties, and we, his comrades, will treasure his memory as having die 1 in action.—Yours very faithfully.W •J. Church Brasier." Malkin was the third member of the Warrington Corps to go out on ser- vice in Soith Africa. He left on December 19th with the Ist West Riding Yorkshire Regiment, but was stationed at a hospital in Capetown. It appears that he is the first member of the St. John Ambulance Brigade which has supplied over 700 men for service, who has died from enteric fever. He was 23 years of age. His mother r-sideiin Mer4ey-street and much sympathy is expressed with her in her sudden bereavement. At a dinner and social gathering held at the Midland Hotel, Wiuwick-street, under the auspices of the War- rington Trades and Labour Council on Monday evening, the Chairman, Mr. A. C. Binks said ho thought it was their duty to pass a vote of condolence with Mr. Thomas Malkin, on hearing of the death of his nephew,—one of the Warrington Ambulance men who went out to South Africa. He was sorry to say that a repot:, had come to hand that he had died of enteric fever. It was very sad that one of their Warrington lads should go out and before see;ng much service in that fore4,ne land should be struck down- He proposed that a vot: of condolence be forwarded to Mr. Malkin. The motion was passed in silence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Lancashire Reservists Leave</head>
        <p>Lancashire Reservists Leave</p>
        <p>Southampton. The UMIn-Castle Company's Royal mail steanacx, Briiion, which left Southampton on Saturday, as the ordinary South African mail steamer, conveys a large number of private passengers and 59 officers and 1,400 non-commissioned officers and men (including 269 Reservists of the Bth King's Liverpool Regiment, who left Warrington on Friday night last). The Briton has carried ,roops to South Africa on two occasions already, and has male the fastest passage between England and Snuth Africa of any transport.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Warrington Fusilier at Colenso. "Selling Horses for a Cigarette."</head>
        <p>A Warrington Fusilier at Colenso. "Selling Horses for a Cigarette."</p>
        <p>Writing from Colens° Camp under date February 22nd to his brother-in-law, Mr. W Cann, 44, Parr-strest, War- ington, Private W Burton, of the Royal Welsh Fusiliers says .--*, We have got Colenso at last and hope to be in Ladysmith this week. We have had some rather hard fighting since the 14th of this month and have had to take bill atter hill. IF is really surprising to me how we have managed to do It for each hill that we have taken has been a veritable stronghold, especially the one my regiment is on at present. It is like a range of loose rocks. There are great big stones that they could have hidden half-a-dczen in3n and horses behind most of them affording splendid cover from any sett of fire. We cannot make out why the Boers left them in such a hurry for they left a lot of things in the shape of tents, blankets, rugs, suits of clothing that had never been worn, boots, horses, cattle, saddlery of all sorts and other things too numerous to mention behind them. We have been emjoying our- selves latcly lean tell you. I have to seep leaving my writing to look whether those beggars have blown up our pontoon bridge for if they do it will be a case of swimming I expect: Most of the troops have crossed and are fighting like tropes at the other side. I am sorry I am not among them. My company collared 13 horses and some tuilloel.s the other day so we have rather more than we care for especially when they are selling at the very big price of one cigarette...</p>
        <p>' UNSHED COnPOUID ' (Trade Mark) gives ex- pectoration without strain. 9d., 130. Sold by Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Letters from Warrington Yeomen. Incidents of the Voyage. Mr. Gerrard. toe oropjetor of tee "L:Yerp-ol House," Green-,e-reet, War6reton. bee rereev d alener from Private James A. P:insen. Ye Li .1 ia •ving in S ,utia Africa the Imperial Yeomanry, who, previous to "going south " was in his employ. The letter is dated Capetown, South Africa, February 25th, 1900, and reads as follows :—•• Just a line to tell you of our arrival here safe and sound. We have had a most lovely voyage, nothing could be mere beautiful. The first two or three days after leaving we experienced a bad sea but after we left the Bay of Biscay, it was most deligtrifol. The sealwae just like a pond. We had bad food until we got to Las Palmas but we made complaints to the captain and there soon was a change. We have been living a gentleman's life since but luck favoureo, me, I was never more surprised than when I found young Hatton aboard. He is the on of Mr.Hatton.the chemist,formerly of Market Gate,andhe was so pleased to see someone from Warrington. I was glad to meet him I can assure you for I have been dining in the cabin ever since. I have had everything you could mention. He is the Captain's orderly. Now to the fun we have had en route. The first thing we do is to groom horses at 6.30 in the morning and then we have breaktast. After that we go through our ablutions. We get in a large bath on deck, and a man plays on us witha hose pipe (sea water you know), and with the sun being so hot (the heat is oppressive), it is grand. It makes a new man of you. After dinner we play deck cricket. It is champion fun. We played the Lancaslaires one day and beat them by 40, and then we played the War wicks and beat them easily, so that left us champions on board. One night we have a concert on deck, and another night we have a boxing competition. One of our chaps won the 9 stone and a Lancashire one the 12 stone. So you see we have been enjoying ourselves. Bum, by the way, when we reached Las Palmas some Blue-jacirets boarded us and gave es orders to proceed at once to Capecown saying we were wanted. We should have stayed there 12 hours to coal and our chaps thought that Buller had had another reverse. We were Inisraken, for when we arrived a ship spoke us going in the bay saying that Kimberley had been relieved and Cronje was surrounded. When our fellows heard it they went frantic with ex- citement. I thought they were going mad. That was a grand bit of news for us on our arrival,it fairly pulled our men together: They are anxious to get to the front hut I am afraid from the way our troops are going on that we shall never see the field. I fancy we are going to Wyeberg from here and stay there a whi'e, but I should like to see a bit of fighting. There wouldn't be any honour due to us if we did not lend a helping hand. But never mind I have enjoyed mysel' up till now. I never felt healthier and happier in my life. You should just see my skin it is ac brown as a berry and another thing they have advanced our pay and your humble may be pro- moted. There is a eorpiral sh,rt in our eection and I think I have got the first ch;nce. I don't think there is anything to ear, only please ratnemer me ta all cue- tsamere wh, Pnvirs. I remain yours clerk.. James A. Prinsep, 1770, 22 Company, 2nd Batt. Imperial Yeo- manry. February 28th. Since penning these Cronje having surrendered we have orders to proceed to Kimberley without delay and then on to Mafeking to Baden-Powell.</p>
      </div>
