      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Cronje and His Army at Capetown.</head>
        <p>Cronje and His Army at Capetown.</p>
        <p>"A Rum Lot." Letter from Trooper A. Ashton.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Ashton, of the" Bridge Inn," Warrington, has received a letter from his brother. Private A Ashton, who is serving in South Africa with Captain Leigh's Company, of Cheshire Yeomanry. It is dated Mait'and Camp, near Capetown, March 4th, and is as follows : " It is Sunday, and as this is the first chance we have had of having a few hours to ourselves. I take the opportunity of writing a few lines. I won't have any other chance after, as we are at it all day. You can't real ze in England what we are uedergoing. You fancy the same as I did, that being at Capetown we should have a fine true, but you are mistaken. The bnele sounds at 5 and we are on parade from 5.30 until half-past seven. When breakfast is served we get a pint of tsa or coffee and a pound of bread without butter and the sews for tea. At dinner we have half-a-pound of beef. Jam we get once a week. That is what is called field rations. We are ge,ting the same food as those at the front, where we should have been by now but since Croup surren- dered and Ladysmith and Kimberley were relieved they , can't get trains to take us as they are all taken up 1 with sending fresh supplies and the people are going up the country again. I have sent Cou the "Cape Times" and you will sse in that more than you would see in an English paper in a year. You will see; in it places where there has been and is at present severel fighting which you never heard of at home. It gives you a good account of bow the news of the relief of Ladysmith was received in Capetown so I need not de- scribe it. I never saw anything like it in my life. There were 500 of us on mounted patrol duty in the town helping the mounted police to keep order as they have sent a Corps up to the front. The people didn't care what they did. They were inad with joy ' and went rushing about regardless of anything. The night after I was in town and saw the Boer prisoners come down from Croeje's 'eager. They were amm lot-, and if it had not been for the escort the people would have pulled them to pieces. You can't imagine the feeling of the people here. They stop at nothing over this war,and they treat us chaps with the greatest kind- ness possible. For instance I went to town one night I and going off the platform I was looking at the train for Kimberley, when a young fellow, and a toff at that, came up to me and exolaine 1 it all to me about the De Beer's private car which was attached with directors going back into Kimberley. Then he would have me go for a drink to the Royal Hotel and treated me to drinks land cigars and even paid for me a cab to go beck to camp. All the people are the same but of course they %don't spend as much as that. You remember me writing in my last letter about the Bengal Lancers. Well, I have since heard 'that there are no black men to carry arms against white men so they are used as camp orderlies, mending saddlery and clothing. It is an amusing sight to see them at work. They take their shoes off and sit on their haunches in a circle and hold almost everything they stitch between their feet. I thought we were going to have a quiet day but while I am writing this I am warned to be ready with 20 more t o go ,to Capetown for remounts for our troop. From what e can hear we are in for a rough time as the ponies are perfect terrors to ride. They are wonderful things. They are only as big as a good sized dog (you can take the size from Wilkinson's pony) but can carry as much las our horses and are better suited to the climate. I , entered for the 100 yards Western provinces champion- ship of South Africa at Capetown but could not get off. I would have won it if I had bren there, as there are no swimmers out here worth speaking of. I met a chap in town one night belonging to French's flying column who came down as an escort with Crnnje I and he was telling me of the times they are having up there. He said before the relief of Kimberley they had been on the road with four days' rations and never stopped only after sle many hours to give the horses a wind and they used to sleep with reins slipped round their arms so as to be ready to start again at once. He said the B oers were the same. They used to shoot them in the day time and when they went for them they were gone. He said the capture at Cronjs's Laager included new saddles and harness from London. The only draw back here is the water, but grapes are only 31 per pound and hawkers bring them up to the camp. I think after the campaign I will be as hard as nails and able to stand anything, Perhaps you won't believe that we do a lot more work than the regulars, as we are drilling all day, and they only drill early in the morning and late in the afternoon when it is cool. I saw in the "Athletic News" that Warrington beat Brouelitoa, and that Runcorn are still at the top of the League. We can get the English papers here but when we get them they are three weeks old. When you take them regularly it is like having them at home. There are some joiners :working in the camp, fixing horse troughs. I asked one how was brick setting out here and he said it was a good trade and the wages £1 a day : and lodgings with board included at 251. per week. I think I will stay out here for a year or two when this job is over, which can't be long now. Since I commenced writing I have been to Capetown for the remounts and while we wore there another tram load of Boer prisoners cerne in. They did look beauties I can tell you. They were the dirtiest and filthiest looking wretches you could imagine.</p>
        <p>Trooper Broadbent on Camp Life.</p>
        <p>The following are trants frlm a letter from Mr. J. W. Broadbent, 22nd Company of Imperial Yeomanry, son of Mrs. Broadbent, The collies, Latchford.—Mait- land Camp. March 4th. I like camp life very much and never felt better. This morning we had stables from six to eight, first watering, then greeming, feeding, and haying ; half the men cleaned up the lines, and Half exercised. Certain men are told off for cooking, butcher- ing, 2ec., and we eat our rations with a pocket knife. For breakfast sometimes only coffee is civet] out, but we buy tinned meat, bread, butter,and milk in the canteen. It is very hard to get leave. I have only been into Cape. town once. It is rather a nice place. It was the night of the relief of Ladysmith.and I have'never heard such a row or seen such excitement. Trams were lit up, and every one wore union jacks and marched abut in crowds hooting when they passed a Boer house. Capetown is trulylßritisb. I must may the voyage is a bit of a night- mare still to me, but I knew what I was in for—crowds and smell of food awful. The Warwicks, who came with us, were a nice lot. Lord Dudley's brother-in-law and Ear' Spencer's eons are booth troopers. I saw a lhorse in he lines that had been wounded by a shell at Magersfontein ; it bad a piece blown out of its neck, but looked all right. I have seen several grey horses painted khaki colour. Arthur Edelsten's pillow, haversack and maps are of the greatest poFsible use to me. There were several officers' servanta and grooms at our mess, and very good fellows , they were. There is a nice young Davenport from .4 Latchford. We are 13 in a tent, and are only provided</p>
      </div>
