      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>MILITARY NOTES.</head>
        <p>MILITARY NOTES.</p>
        <p>Qaarter-master Sergeant Roberts, 2nd Battalion Liverpool Regiment, and who was for a consider- able period serving with the dep t companies at Warrington, has been selected for a commission as quarter-master with the honorary rank of Lieut. and becomes quarter-master of the new 3rd batta- lion Liverpool Regiment now being formed. Mr. Roberts is an experienced soldier who has well earned his advancement in the service. _ _</p>
        <p>Lecond Wimberley, 2nd Battalion Welsh Regiment, whose death has just bean reported from South Africa, commenced his career in the 6th bat- talion Liverpool Regiment at Warrington. He joine4 the local militia in 1897; was promoted Lieut. in August 1898, and in May of last year got his commission in the regular service when he was appointed to the Welsh Regiment. Major Phillimore, reserve of officers, has assumed duty as staff captain for Royal Artillery in this dis- trict and will discharge the duties of that post dur- ing the absence in South Atrica of Captain Stave- ley, R.A. Arrangements are now being made for the train- ing of the 9th Yeomanry brigade which consists of the Lancashire Husars and the Cheshire Yeomanry. This brigade which is under Colonel Lord Gerard will it is hoped this year be able to train in camp as a brigade in accoraance with the desire of the War Office that each Yeomanry brigade shall this year train or 28 days in camp at a centre where there are good tacilities for carrying out the musketry course. Surgeon Lieutenant Ranshaw,of the 3rd Volunteer Battalion Cheshire Regiment with headquarters at Knutsford will next aionth be promoted Surgeon Captain on completing 3 years' service as a medical officer of the corps. Lieutenant Richardson who has been about a year an officer of the old Ist Cheshire Militia has been given a commission as second lieutenant in the regular service and joins the 2nd Battalion ICheshire Regiment. ! Great demands are being made upon the Depot companies of the King's Liverpool Regiment at Warrington not only for men to form the 3rd and 4th battalions of the regiment now being raised but also to reinforce the let battalion in South Africa. A draft of about 250 non-commissioned officers and men—has bean prepared to be sent to Natal to join the battalion and even then the corps will be some- what weak owing to the losses it sustained by killed and wounded in action and also deaths from disease while forming part of the force under Sir G. S. White shut up in Ladysmith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS. Mr. G. F. Clarke's Candidature.</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS. Mr. G. F. Clarke's Candidature.</p>
        <p>A meeting in support of the candidature of Mr G F Clarke for the Town Hall Ward, was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Tuesday evening. Mr S Findlay presided, and amongst those present were:—Dr Latham, Messrs J Clarke, P Foden, J Dobson, 0 Pickett, M Dun- can, E FAzeerald, P Naylor, T Kenney, Astley, King, J E Clough, &amp;c. 1 Mr. G. F. Clarke said in the first place he came for- ward as an independent candidate, not bound by any clique or authority, nor controlled by any oreauisation, but simply as a free lance to represent the ratepayers, and to discharge those duties, should he be elected, to the best of his ability. (Hear, hear). He thought everyone would agree with him that these positions should be held by the men who were unfettered and not put in a position to voice the opinicei of any individual or organisation, and if he was elected by the majority of the voters of the Town Hall Ward,he should take his position on the Council free and uncontrolled and free to voice the opinion of the ratepayers without any restriction whatever. Another reason was, that without anyeattempt at egotism he thought he might be able to do scene little that might be of service to the ratepayers. So far as the Town Hall Ward was concerned there were ene or two matters of very great importance, but for the majority of the ratepayers in the ward, and he might say for the district generally, he did not consider there was any question of grea'er importance than the question of the water supply. They knew what the position had been for many years. They knew how the supply had been short in some districts more than others. It was a len own fact that many of the colliers in the edistrict had had to go to their work without obtaining water to wash their faces; and in other ways the supply of water had been very keenly felt. He did not thiuk a township like that should be under the finger and thumb of any authority for the supply of its water, more espe- cially an authority who would use the water for its own purposes,and then consider the needs of the township as a secondary matter. He should give the water scheme that had been instituted by the majority of the present Council his hearty support. lii this respect he wanted to call their attention to the attitude that was taken by certain members to oppose the new scheme. He did not intend to be personal that night,or to say anything about his opponent except to say that his attitude in opposing the water scheme when it was first introduced into the Council did not represent the feelings and opinions of his constituents,and it did not represent the feelings and opinions of the electors in the township generally. The remark was made by the retiring councillor that they had dug a big hole and spent £l5O on it, and had got cloth- in, arid that was all they were likely to get. When they had remarks and opposition of that character, the scheme could not be approached in the way it ought to be approaehed. It ought to be looked at in a frank and independent manner and all vested interests put aside. He thought they would agree with him that the action of the Council in endeavoering to get an independent supply was tor the interest of the district, and ought to receive the commendation and unanimous approval of the ratepayers. Referring to the question of gas supply he contrasted the slot meters with the ordinary meters. People had told him that the number of feet allowed through the slot meters was not anything like equal to what was charged per 1.000 feet by the ordinary meter. He thought no member of the Council would object to see that the amount supplied through the slot meter was equal to that supplied through an ordinary meter for the same amount. The quality of the gas was more a matter for the experts. The meunre deposits over the canal bank were a nuisance and he could not understand how the residents in the district were able to eat their food and live comfortably with such an intolerable smell blowing into the house back and front, and he thought the Coun- cil could deal with it through their own bye-laws. H" should endeavour to get to know the position himself, and promised, if elected, to leave no stone neiturned to get at the bottom of the matter to see that the nuisance was removed, or that the question was raised to see the Council's proper position in the matter. He briefly referred to the question of street making and to that of sanitation, He had a special interest in the financial business of the Council and would devote his energies to seeing that the affairs of the township were economically conducted, with efficiency. If elected, his interest in their affairs would not be a passive one. He should endeavour to de whatever he could, and be as active as he could in seeing that the ward that he should represent was well looked after, not only for the ward itself, but also for the district generally, and that being so he begged to ask all the electors to at leaet give him that confidence and support that would merit a trial at their hands. A ratepayer asked if Mr. Clarke was in favour of the question of the housing of the Working Classes. Mr. Clarke said he was not in favour of the Council entering into competition with local builders, as they had to pay rates to the Council and might be put in a serious condition. He was not in favour of the Council entering into socialistic schemes. (Applause.) Mr Duncan proposed that Mr G F Clarke is a fit and proper person to represent the electors of the Town Hall ward. _ _ .. . -</p>
        <p>Mr. P. Foden seconded, and the resolution was unanimously c&amp;rried. A vote of thanks to the Chairman terminatel the proceedings.</p>
        <p>• LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds allays irritation. Gives immediate relief.—Advt.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS km) SHOES UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt</p>
        <p>ASK or CUNNINGHAM HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS—Advt.</p>
        <p>KITCHEN RANGES, Tile Grates,and Chimney Pieces Large Stock —.J . S. CtAaLaND,lronmonger,Bridge-st. Ada</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE LAST MEETING OF THE YEAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. B. F. Borron presUed at the !est meeting of the Newton-in-Makerfield Urban District Council prior to the annual elections, on Monday evening. There were also present Me,isr4s W Caltingwood, J W Emmett, N Slddeley ,J Simpson, R Stone, Dr PaLterson, W Thornp- a,n, W Beatty, T Ormand, E T Eiraus, J H Fo;ster, with W W Shilley (clerk), A Bowes (surveyor), and D a Lloyd (gas engineer.) THE CHAIRMAN THANKS THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Cnairman, prior to the commencement of the business said that was the last meeting of the Council's year and it would devolve upon the members at the next meeting to elect a chairman. He wished to take that opportunity of tendering to them his thanks for the generous support they had extended to him during the past year. They had done him the honour of electing him to the position of chairman in five successive years and it was an honour he greatly appreciated, but they were aware that he intimated to them a year ago that be would terminate his position as Chairman at the close of the year. Looking back on the past five years he noticed the losses of members and officials. Some had died and others had retired from the worries and anxieties of public life to enjoy a well-earned rest. Personally he regretted that one work which he had b,pecl would have been completed before he retired from the chair was still unfulfilled. He referred to the water- works undertaking by which means it was hoped to give the township a gold and ample supply of water, hut owing to repeated delays on the part of the contrac- tor the work was still in hand. He was, however,given to understand that before six months had passed the township would have a supply of water which would te accept iaile to them. In conclusion he would like to say that he was confident that the members of that Council had" no axes to grind "but that on the contrary they were aniinet-d by a desire to beneE.t the ratepe rers and public generally. If they contiutvd in that path he was certain they would receive the approbation and confi- dence of those whom they represented. (Applau,-.) Mr. Gilmour remarked that as one who had given the Chairman as much trouble as anyone, he should hays been very pleased if the Chancellor of the Duchy had seen his way clear to place their chairman on the magis- terial bench permanently. He had discharged his magisterial duties during the past year by virtue of his poAdou as chairman of the Council, but he thought it would be an everlasting disgrace if the local bench were to lose the services of Mr Borron. The Chairman: I hope_l may have a very long holiday. Tim TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Technical Instruction Committ,e recommended that the sum of £B5 received from the Technical Instruc- tion Committee of the County Cnuncil be banded over to the Technical instruction Committee of thn Viaduct Institute.</p>
        <p>The recommendation was una.nirnnu,ly approved. GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meet in of this committee held on the 12th inst a letter dated the 28th February,was read from H.Rylance, junior clerk at the gasworks, applying for an increase of wages. It was resolved that his wages ho increaced from 93 to 12s per week.—The gas and water engineer read his monthly report, in which he stated that boring at the No 2 borehole at the waterwnrke had reached a depth of 256 feet from the surface, and recommended that boring should now be stopped, and read Utters from Mr. Gaskin, consulting engineer, in support of his suggestion. It was resolved that boring at No 2 bore- hole be stepped (subject to approval of the Council), and that the specification for the pumps be altered so as to specify them to be fixed at 200 feet from the surface, instead of 300 feet as originally specified —lt was orderei that theexisting gas mains in Princes-street and Oxford-street be examined, and, if found necessary, re- placed—The following claims for damages caused by footpath in Wargrave-road being left unpro- tected at night during the laying of gas mains, were laid before the Committee, viz.— Harry Davies, 9, Bradlegh-road, £1 103; John McCor- mick, 43, Mtil•lane, £1 10s, It was resolved that the Clerk refer the same to the Contractor. A claim of £2 10s from Mr. Isaac Whittle, of Earle-street, Eatles- town, for demages to his arm alleged to have been caused by falling over a gas main in Earle-street was referred to the Clerk for his investigation and report. It was ordered that the Clerk write to the Contractor for the cutting of the trenches for new gas mains in War- grave-road, and call his attention to the unsatisfactory state in which the footpaths hadbeen left, and request him to be good enough to place the same in good eon- dition immediately.—Mr. Lloyd having applied to be formally appointed gas manager in room of Mr. Swann, resigned, was informed that he was already the gas engineer, which included the management of the gas- work3.—The Gas Meter Inspector repotted that upon inspecting the autematie meter at the house of Patrick fierney, 36, Gas-street, Newton-le-Willows, he found that the lock and box for containing the coins had been removed, and that there should have been 2i 5d therein. It was resolved that a claim of 51 ba made upon Patrick Tierney (to include the damage to the meter),and failing payment legal proceedings to be taken against him. It was rssolved that the supply of gas, to consumers by automatic meters refusing to pay the difference between the value of the value of the gas registered and the amount of meney in the box, be discontinued, and that the price of gas to consumers by automatic meter be charged at the rate of 28 cubic feat per one penny, and not at the rate of 3s per 1,000 cubic feet as heretofore.— A letter dated the sth March, was read from the Evesen Coal and Coke Co., ' Limited, Birmingham referring to a contract with this Council for immediate delivery of 250 tons of coke in February, 1899; and asking for the balance of coke to be forwarded them, as they had only received a portion of the same.—lt was explained that they had sent no wagons since October, 1899, where- upon it was resolved that the Clerk inform Messrs Eveson that, as they ceased of their own accord to send wagons, the Committee consider the contract was annulled, and also to press them for immediate payment of their account.—The Committee resolved that where an existing supply of water is changed from "Assess- ment " to " by meter " by the orders of the Council the whole of the expense shall be borne by them. HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE. GENEROUS ACTION BY THE CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>This committee met on the 12th March. A letter dated the 9th March, was read from elessrs. Houseman and Co., solicitors to the Right Hon. Lord Newton, with reference to the period of completion of the proposed new r)ad from Crow-lane to Wargrave-road, stating, in answer to the Clerk's letter pointing out the reasons of the Council for desiring the wording of the agreement to read that the road should be completed and opened for teaffic within one year of the receipt of the sanction of the Local Government Board to the borrowing by the Couesil ef the money required,that they were instructed by Mr. Leslie (Lord Newton's estate agent) to adhere to the two years already named by nim as the period within which the propose .1 new read should be completed, as this (in their opinion) should give ample time to obtain the eecessary sanction.— A letter, dated the 10th March, was read from Mr. Leslie, agent to Lord Newton, stating, in answer to the Clerk's letter informing him that the Council had de- cided to eh sndon the powers cenferred upoa them by deed in 1893 with regard to the laying of a sewer from Mill-lane to the central outfall sewage works and re- questing Mr Leslie to be good enough to grant a way- leave in lieu thereof, that it must be distinctly seeder- stood no arra.ngernent.e could be entered into or the surface of the land disturbed until the existing deed bed been surrendered and cancelled. It was resolved that the Clerk to th.e Council obtain the opinion of Messrs peace and Ellis, snickers, as to the position of the Council in these matters. A letter, dated the 10th March, was read from Mr Leslie, stating, in answer to the Clerk's letter, that be agreed to the setting back of the fence on the south side of Wargrave-road as per plan &gt;submitted to him. It was resolved that the Sur- veyor proceed with the work—The Surveyor submitted his estimate of the damage done at the glass bottle- works of Messrs Barron and Co., owing to toe neelieent stopping up of a culvert at the gasworks which hae in consequence backed up on the premises ot Messrs Berron, amounting to the sum of £45. The committee thereupon resolved that the Council be recommended to pay the amount estimated by the Surveyor Mr. Barron, who had retired whilst the matter was being discussed, stated that whilst recognising he had a legal claim aains,t the Ceurcil, he felt it to be his duty as Chairman ef the Gas Committee to forego his claim altogether.— A letter, dated the 23rd February, was read from the Llnceshire County Ceuncil, offering the sum of £650 for erdieery repair and maintenance ot main roads in this eictrict for the year ending the 31st March, 1941, and £3B for special work in Church-street. Other improve- ments p;stpened on account of the proposed tramways. —lt wee resolved that the offer of the Lancashire County CunCil be accepted, subject to other special grants being deferred as suggested by them —A letter, , dated the Ist March, was r ad from Mr G H Bentley, eleetrici an, asking permission to lay electric feeder cables underground in Cross-lane. It was resolved that the committee cannot see their way to accede to the appli- cation.—lt was resolved that the occupiers of the huts on the market be served with 14 days' notice to carry out the previous resolutions of this Council with regard to the placing of them on wheels, and failing compliance therewith legal proceedings to be taken against them,and that a formal authority under the Seal of the Council be given to the Toll Collector, empowering him to seize on refussl to pay toll. It was further resolved that a sub- committee, consisting of Cnuneillors Borron, J.P , Col- linewnod, Wood, Beatty, Simpson, Evans, and Thomp- son, be appointed to censider and report upon all public frotpates within the district. Mr. Colinewood moved and Mr. Simpson seconded the adoption of the minutes which were agreed to. GENERAL PURPOSES _COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>The question rof the rating of Grouud Values was again adjourned sine die at a meeting held on the 12th net. A circular I, trer dated the 20th of February, was read from the President of the Royal Institute of Public</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the members of this club was held at the Viaduct Institute ou. Monday even- ing, when there was only a smell attendance. Mr. T. Orinend °resided, and amongst others present were Messrs. T F ;Roberta, J H Alderson, T Mitchell, J Saunders, J fludale3ton and J Owen (secretary.) Toe minutes of the lest annual meeting were taken as read, and the secretary submitted his annual report and bal- mier) sheet. Tne report was as follows i--‘• Perhaps a word or two of apology would not be out of place here for the delay of a month in holding our annual meeting.Severai reasons might lte aivanced for the apparent neglect, the chief cause however, lies with those members wno re- p,ateely leave their subscriptions unpaia until January or February, and as this practice is becoming more general year by year, it is as well that your attention should be directed to what is undoubtedly a growing evil. In many respects the year under review was sinalae to 1898. Of the 19 matches arranged 3 were cancelled owing to wet weather, 16 were played, 5 won, 9 lost, 1 drawn, and 1 (with Wigan), resulted in a tie. ;These figures oiffer very little from those contained in our report 12 months ago. If we except the district junior match, fortune on:7 smiled elan' us on one occa- ' ram on the Institute ground, the visitors being Mars- cheater Cefford ; away from home we obtained victories at tao expense of Wigan, St. Helens and Leigh. This performance will hardly bear luoking at when viewed in the light of a few years ago, when to lose a match, especially at home, was to sly the least, an unusua occurrence. Of course, we had not the same stamp of opponents in those days, but making every allowance it is more than questionable whether our present players ale equal to those we have to meet week after week. Our oatsmen scored 1,535 runs for 159 wickets giving an average of 9 104 runs per wicket, and as we lost 157 wickets for 10'47 in the previous year it will be noticed that the batting fell off 1 run per wieket. In bowling there is an improvement, as 160 wickets were obtained at an average cost of 12 156 against 149 wickets averaging 17.120 in 1898, a gain of 5 runs per wicket. The batting was undoubtedly weak and the fact that our opponents totalled in round figures 500 runs more than we did for practically the same number of wickets points to this pretty calclusivelye Once only were we called upon to pay talent money, furnishing a further instance of the batsmen's deteriera- tion. The 2nd eleven played 15 matches, winning 6, losing 5, and drawing 4—a record rather below that of I last year. To criticise would be unfair, seeing that tbey were so often called upon especially in the latter part of the season to furnish recruits for the let team. There is I no deubt that if the members looked upon as second I eleven players could play regularly together they would ' be exceedingly hard to beat. In many bestances the I demands made upon their services meant the loss of opportunity to better their aver ages in their own class, vet your committee cennot recall a single instance in which Mose services were not willingly given in order to benefit the club to which we are pleased to belong. Our rxperience last season 441 not warrant us in again engaging a ground professional; we have instead re-en- gaged our old match professional, Mr. J. Miller,who will In a lane ad degree, act as coach Co the juniors, and if as we anticipate, his connection with us is as profitable as heretofore, you will have no reason to regret the com- mittee's action in this matter.. No less than 21 players had opportunities of distinguishing themselves in the first team, and 23 in the second team. Some of these have doee all that was expected of them, J H Alderson, who along with the professional did the blunt of the bowling has exceeded all his past efforts and easily carries off the bat awarded for bowling. G Husband though not doing so well as in former years, takes the bat offered for batting. R A Robiuson and W Harrison have met with most success in the second team, and have therefore the honour of receiving a bat each. The latter has often by his bowling won distinction, but in the case of the former this is the first time he has appeared amongst the prize winners. We venture to ' predict it will not be the last. The Institute Commit- tee's grant of £2 has been equelly divided between W Jenkinson, F Butler, James Halton, and H Davies I Matches have been arranged with Cheetham, Wigan. I Leigh, Liverpool, Tyldesley, St. Helens, St.Helens Recs, I Little Leer, Manchester, Clifford, Winnington, and 18, District Juniors. Financially the year has been a grew-, success the amount dealt with being the largest sine the inception of the club. Including a balance of £3 its id, the (Intel receipts amounted to £69 15s 11d, an increase of £22 upon the previous year, and though there were a number of wet Saturdays, it is pleasing to note an increase of about £5 in gate receipts. A special donation of £lO from the Institute Committee gave U 3 a very welcome lift. If we except the £34 lOs paid for professional assistance, our expenses are very light. Owing to the matches being arranged nearer home than usual, we have been enabled to effect a saving in travel- ling expenses for players, umpires and scorers to the amount of £6,whilst talent money has been £4 less than the corresponding period. There is a balance of £1 6s 61 in the Treasurer's hands, but as we have, during the winter months overhauled the playing material, the amount spent in this direction is equal to cash in hand. We have once more to acknowledge our indebtedness to the Institute Committee of Management, who have in a very practical way showed their appreciatien of the club in as efforts to further the interest of athletic ex- ercise. Were it otherwise, the game of cricket would still be in its infancy so far as this town is concerned. They do all that can be reasonably expected from them. It remains with the members to see that those efforts are not made in vain." (Applause.) The Chairman said the Secretary's report so far as the operations of the club during the year under review, was certainly not of a gratifying nature, apart from the financial aspect.Ele did not know whether the committee had been fully alive to their responsibilities in giving second team members opportunities of playing ire first team matches, and weeding out those fossilised members who did net come up to tbeir expectations. There were a lot of promising members in the second team who had to play in the first team several times, and which natur- ally prevented them obtaining distinction in their own class, but they bad willingly given their services for the benefit of the club, and this showed a true sportsman. like spirit, which he hoped would be emulated by others The subscriptions which were not paid before January or February caused the secretary a large amount of treub'e. The bulk of this work fell upon the secretary's shoulders and it would be a great help to him, and a benefit to the club if the members would pay their sub- scriptions before the end of the season which would facilitate matters, and make the Secretary's path, which was not one of roses,much more comfortable than it was at the present time. He was glad to see that the financial state of the club was healthy, but it was to be reeretted that they had not spent more talent money. It would have been very grae ifying to him if the Secretary, had said instead of being £1 6i. 6d. in hand, they would be 22135. 61. in debt, as they had to pay as much talent necney as last year, as that would be an acknowledge- ment of prowess as well with the bat as with the ball, and it would show that those members of the Earlas- town club were making it a name to be feared in the batting and bowling world- (Hear, hear.) He felt cer- tain if more money was wanted for this purpose, that the Committee of the Institute would be only too ready to respond to the best of their ability, as they waro will- ing to help ha every possible way they could every game which led to the physical advancement on the grounds there. The report reflected very great credit upon the Secretary for its compilation, but beyond ihat he did not think he could not congratulate them. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS. RESIGNATION OF SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. Emmett, J.P , was re-elected presilent,a ad Messrs. Hat. wool and Dr. Harrison, vice-presidents, Mr. T. Ormand was re-appointed treasurer. On the question of secretary Mr. J. Omen said he did not think there were many in that room but were a mare that with that meeting his position as secretary of the Club would cease. At a committee meeting held on the 31st January, he announced his determination of resign- ing, and the resignation was then accepted. It was then decided that Mr. J. El. Alderson should succeed him and he now had great pleasure in proposing him as secret try As a playing member be thought it would be of very g,rpat interest to the Club to have him as secretary, and he (Mr. Owen) would give him the benefit of lais advice and experience. During the 18 years he had been secre- tary of the Club, he had done all he could for it, but his time was so much 02c0pied that he could not give it the atten!ion he would like. A little new blood sometimes did pod and the change might be beneficial to the Club. Mr. J. Huddleston seconded.</p>
        <p>The Chairman could not let that opportunity go by without saving how much he personally regretted Mr. Owen's severance from the secretary's duties connected with the Club. Mr. 0 men had just intimated to them that for 18 years he had performed thole duties, and he (the speaker) could say that during the whole of that time he had performed them remarkably well. In fact he had rendered yeoman service to cricket in Eirlestown and to that Club in particular. However much th9y might thank him, they could not repay him in any pos- sible way for the very able and energetic way in which he had discharged his duties. (flear, hear.) fie believed they would find a very able successor in Mr. Alderson, and with the promise that Mr. Owen had made te give him the bsnefit of his experience they would get along all right. •He thought it was a wise selection, and was glad the Conatnitee had made it, and he understood Mr. Alderson was willing to accept it, and throw into it all the life and vigour that he did into his bowling. He was sure they owed Mr Owen the greatest</p>
        <p>thavlts for his services in connection with thu club since 1882. (Appiaus). Mr. Roberts said he knew the sterling worth of Mr. On, and MO amount of work he bad done, and the cheerful way in which he performed his duties. He had worked loyally for the Club. and he felt sure that a mere vote of thanks to Mr. Owen would not let it end there, but that something more tangible would result. He proposed that it be recorded on the minutes how much they regretted his severance from the position he had occupied. (Applause). Mr. Alderson seconded this, and fully endorsed all that Mr. Roberts had said, and was sure all the playing mem- bars of the Club would do the same, as whatever Ur Owen had done had been for their benefit. He was much °bilged to them for appointing him secretary of the club, and thanked Mr. Owen for his offer to give him advice, which he would not be slow to take advantage of.</p>
        <p>Mr. C W Emmett was appointed cap'ain, of the first team and Mr G Husband. vice-captain. Mr. A Limond was appointed 2ad team captain, and Mr J Wainwright, vice-captain. Tbe following were appointed committee, Messrs J Owen. F Elora ley, J Saunders, James Seddon. r F Roberts, W Jeakinson, J Huddleston, G Husband and R A Robinson. Messrs Gooding and Tarback were appointed auditors. _ _</p>
        <p>The following were presentel with bats and bags for batting and bowling :—G lausband,bat for battinl ; J H Alderson, bat, for bowling; R A Robinson, bat, for senonn team batting; W Harrison, bat for second team bowling; W Jenkinson, boots ; F Butler, bat ; J Halton, bag; El Davies. bag.</p>
      </div>
