      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PRESENTATIONS TO R. BELL AND F CLARE.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS TO R. BELL AND F CLARE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman was sorry that Mr. Bell for whom that silver coffee pot and tea pot were intended was not there to receive from him the very handsome present. He had hoped to be the medium of conveying them to him on behalf of the subscribers. They knew the very great services he had rendered willingly and unselfishly from time to time. He was one of the best men they had, and they deplored very much indeed the injury which be met with and which hid rendered him for a time unfit for cricket. Since he had played for them he had entered the state of matrimony, and it was gratify- ing' that they wished to show Us jr appreciation by pre- senting him with those articles as a mark of their esteem for the great services he rendered unstintingly to the club.</p>
        <p>Mr. Clare owing to his removal to Nantwich had had to sever his connection with the club, and the preleata- tion to him took the flrm of a county bag as a token of esteem for the very valuable services he hd renderei from time to time to that club. The subscribms were delighted to have that opportunity of showing co Mr. Bell and Mr. Clare that although they had gone away their services would live in the memory of pricket&lt;u.s in Earlestown, and they offered thosi presents as a slight token of esteem and appreciation. (Applause.) A vote of thanks to the Chairman, on the motion of Mr. Roberts, seconded by Mr. fludlleston was hear thy accorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ketuts to tin ebitov.</head>
        <p>ketuts to tin ebitov.</p>
        <p>Noncs TO CORRESPONDENT:q.</p>
        <p>luthors of setters intended for publlc3ti4n nlngt in at</p>
        <p>cases send their names and addresses to the Editor. Matters intended for puhlica-ion must; be written on me side only of the paper. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter hasmuch greater chance of loser:ion than a long one.</p>
        <p>Seven Points of a Housing Charter. Sir,—May I strongly urge your readers to write to their members of Parliament asking them to move or support the following amendments to the Government's most meagre and inadequate Housing Bill. 1. Power of local authorities to hold land as wall as to acquire it. 2. Power to acquire by means of the Allotments Clauses of the 1894 Act, rather thaa by the Lands Clauses Acts. 3. Power to take the assessable value as the basis of the purchase price. 4. Power to purchase enough for future as well as present needs. 5. Power to lease the land to private persons, co- operative societies,etc., for housing purposes. 6. Power to extend the period of repayment. 7. Power of representation as to Parr 111. to be the same as that for Parts I. and 11. of the 1890 Act. The importance of polite but earnest pressure from the constituencies can hardly be over estimated.—Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>JOSEPH HYDER.</p>
        <p>Land Nationalisation Swiety. 432, West Strand, London. P.S.—I shall be plea.aed to send a gratis copy of " Better Homes for the Workers" to mnv of your readers.</p>
        <p>Newton Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>THITRSDLY —Before Messrs. I'. J. Gillespio_(in tie chair), 3</p>
        <p>Stone. C. B. F. Borron and John jackson. A DANGERMIS PRACrICE.—Peter Kelly was au mmoned for di.char4ing stones from a catapult at Newto a n the 12th March, —P C. Schofield stated that at noon 02 he date ill question (Sundae) he saw the defendant shorting stones from the catapult produced at broken windows in Vitriol.' square. Ho also saw him shooting at random in the road. way.—Defendent denied shooting at Vacs windows and laid he shot once at some sugar bars which were handing osier the railings He called a witness who stated that they were shooting at a place caLed " Cats Stils."—ln answer to the Superintendent of police he said that the place was not me;l' frequented. In fact no respectable persons would go there.-- The Superintendent: You don't mean to say that you are not a respect%ble man? (Laughter.)—A fine of Is and costs wag imposed.</p>
        <p>. -- His 46TEI APPEARANCE.—Patrick isonoghue, who wag charged with being drunk, made his 46th appearance. As he had not been befo-e the court for three Tiara he was Is off with a tine of 5s and costs.</p>
        <p>TRESPAsSING IN' SEARCH OF GAME.—Thomas Downs vial charged with trespassing on land in the occupation of Messrs Richard Evans and Co., Limited, at flaydock, in search oe game on the 11th Mareh.-111r II J Longton, of Warrinzton. prosecuted. He sail that on the date in question the under gamekeeper for Lord Newton was following his duties near the Wood-pit, Haydock. The Wood-pit was a colliery ena close to there was a disused tip• on whioh was a certain amount of cover in the shape of grass, ftc. It was a favourite habit for hares to lie on this spot. The gamekeeper saw the defendant there wi h a dog. He went to him and had.s conversation with him. Ile charged him with trespassing 10 search of game and said he woula have to report him. De- fendant said he was only doing his duty but he (defendant) had only come there to see the place where the Wood-Pit Colliery explosion took place 22 years ago —Defendant wee denied the offence and said his dog was blind ana could not catch a hare, was fined 30s and costs.</p>
        <p>A VIOLENT PRISONEE.—WiIIiam Precott, Silcox-streetp Golborne, was charged with being drunk and diserderly 50 Golborne on March 24th.—He pleaded &gt;uilty. —He Was further charged with assaulting P.O. Summer, whilst acting in execution of his duty at the same ti cud with damair ing his uniform.—Defendant said he aid not remember having done so. He was so drunx.—P.O. Summers states that at 12.15 on the date named he saw the prisoner drunig in Leigh-street.Golborne. He took him to the Police Statictt and he b:scame very violent. He i.utted witness la the face with his head and alcl/4ed him on the left knee damaging his trousers —Defendant said he did not remember anything about 11.—Re was fine,i 2s 6d and costs for drunkenness, 55 and costs for the assault, and ordered to oav the amount of damage done to the constable's uniform (13s 6d).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. CHNSTER —Teams representing the Liberal Clubs of the above places met at the Liberal Ctub-room, Runcorn, on Wednesday night to play off the return match. At Chester, the home team won bv 62, ant the match at Runcorn resulted in a win for Chester by 1. The finish was exciting, the list game being between J. Joyce and W. Aldis who scored 150 and 142 respectively. Scores RUNCORN. CHESTFR. I Blllott W Lawton l2O G H Crawford B Johnson It Mason Crag T A Smith</p>
        <p>W Brimelow D Mclnnis T Carruthers J Joyce l5O W Alddie - 1299</p>
        <p>1293</p>
        <p>CONCERT AT BATHS ASSEMBLY Rooms, WIDNES On Saturday evening the final concert in connec- tion with the United Alkali popular events was held in the above rooms, when Mr. E. Carey presided over a large attendance. The programme was an excellent one, some first-class talent having been secured. The Runcorn Quartette party admirably acquitted themselves in the items" Good Night," "Allan Water" (Bell), and "Calm be thy Slumbers" (Bishop). Miss Ada Banks was heartily applauded for her rendition of the song "Dream of Paradise," whilst the applause which succeeded the interpre- tation of "Killarney" and "Her Majesty," by Mrs. A Carey was well deserved. The last-named effort was vociferously encored. Miss Pattie Banks also gave a song in pleasing style. Mr J R Lea, a well- known baritone, delighted the audience with several songs. Mr Willrad Sykes, of Liverpool, made himself very popular by his humorous songs.- The topical item "National Letters" was exception- ally well sung, and the artiste was obliged to con- cede an encore. Mr. W H Parry, of Liverpool, die tinguished himself in his musical selections. He also created great amusement. and amazement by his startling feats of prestidigitation. At the con- clusion of the proceedings a vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman. Mr J Gill was the accompanist. ANNUAL PARISH MEETING AT WESTON.</p>
        <p>The annual parish meeting of Weston was held at the Church Schools, on Monday evening. The Rev Canon Wood (chairman of the Parish Council) occu- pied the chair, and there were also present :—Rev W H Stables, Messrs A G Guest, J Basnett, P Ford, and James Collier (clerk to the Parish Council).— The Chairman commented on the small attend- ance, and jocularly remarked that the parishioners only turned up at those meetings when there was likely to be a 'row. (Laughter.) It was likely that the attendance in the future would be less, inas- much as they were only to have an election once in three years, instead of annually as heretofore. The present Council would remain in office for another twelve months,and the next Council which was elected would hold office for three years.—Mr. Collier (clerk), submitted the half-yearly statement of accounts, and also the accounts of the Orred Charity. From the latter it appeared that there was a balance from the last account of 9s 2d; inter- est on consols amount Id to £27 154 41 ; and bank interest, 6s id; total receips, £2B 108 7d. The amount distributed on December 27th was £27 17s 6d, and the expenses were us lid; total dis- bursements, £2B 98 sd, leaving a balance of Is 2d.— A vote of thanks to the Chairman, proposed by the Rev W H Stables, and seconded by ?AT A G Guest was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' (Trade Mark) for Coughs and Colds, of proven efficacy. 131 d. Sold by Chemists only .—Advt.</p>
      </div>
