      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>ALLEGED EXTENSIVE ROBBERIES AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>THE ALLEGED EXTENSIVE ROBBERIES AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Prisoner Again Before the Magistrates. At Newton Prt:y :Sessions on l'hursday, before Mr. 2 J Gillespid and other magistrates, Agnes Hudson young woman e mployed at the " Oddfellows Arms, Earlestown, was charged on remand with stealing one cash boa, five sugar crushers, one pint t ottle of whiskel and one pint bottle of rum of the value of 7i 6cl and £42 63 lid in money, the property and mJnoys of James Lythgoe. She was also charged vital stealiug one gold breast pin value 21s, the property of Thomas Lythgoo. Mr. J W Knowles, a !el Ones, appeared to prosecute, and said that as the amount in the charge *xceeded it was only on a plea of guilty that the prismar could be dealt with at that court. If she phaded mt guilty she would have to go to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions for trial.</p>
        <p>The Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. J. D. Buckton), read out the formal caution to the prisoner, who pleaded noG guilty.</p>
        <p>_ She was again remanded until Tuesday nest wheal she will be brought up at the Warrington Oceasiona Court and cowl:rutted for trial.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY SdHOOL BOARD. The monthly meeting was held on Tuesday even' ing in the Boardroom, when there were present:—' Messrs Thomas Halton (in the chair), Jcseph Wright T Perks Joseph Kilshaw, W 1) Jeans, jun (clerk), Basil Shearer (school attendance officer), and Geo. Clerk (headmaster).—The Clerk reported a balance in hand of £l7B 33 3d.—Mr. Clarke (headmaster)l reported for the month as follows :—March 3, oø books 281, present 252, fees 13s; March 10, on books 282, present 252, fees 12s 8J; March 17, on books 281, present 260, fees 12s 4d; March 24, on bookS 281, present 270, fees 13s 4d ;or a total average for the month of 238.9, and the total re° ceipts £2 lie 4d. Mr. Clarke added that the average was one of the beet he had had this year.--- The Clerk read a letter from Miss Matthews, io which she stated that she had been an ex-pupil teacher for a year, and would be pleased to be granted an increase of salary. She had. Mr. Jeans explained, awn engaged since March last year. Het salary was £35 a year.—Mr. Clarke said he wag under the impression that it Was understood the question of the salary would be revised at the end of the year. Miss Matthewe had done good work in the school.—The Chairman was willing to leave the matter ill the bands of the Board,although if they had a good teacher it would be advisable to keep her, lie was willing to grant an increase of £5 a year. kie thought it would be better if a teacher wan had * good case came before the Board and stabed it ia future. (Hear, hear.)-Ir. Wright was in favour of an increase of £5, and ultimately that sum wag granted to Miss Matthews.—The Chairman: This may be establishing a precedent for the other teachers. Still Ido not like changes. Our teacherS are net badly paid.—The Chairman proposed the election of Mr J Fultcn Smith as a member of the Board in succession to the Rev 1i S Jackson. resigned. He had seen Mr. Smith, and he had con- sented to accept office if elected on behalf of the die trict. (hear, hear.)—Mr Wright seconded, and expressed his pleasure at learning that Mr. Smith would accept office. (Hear, hear.)—The motion wag carried.</p>
